    title: 'Drag-along, tag-along and the trapped majority bidder',
    setup: '<p>A UK private limited company has three shareholders: Alpha Holdings (60%), Bravo Ventures (30%) and a founder, Carol (10%). The articles include a drag-along clause triggered at 75% and a tag-along clause triggered at 50%. Alpha negotiates a sale of all of its shares to a strategic buyer at £10 per share.</p>',
    prompt: 'What rights does each minority shareholder have, and what does the buyer get?',
    modelAnswer: '<p>Alpha cannot trigger the drag-along on its own (it does not meet the 75% threshold). Both minorities can invoke their tag-along (Alpha alone at 60% exceeds the 50% trigger), forcing the buyer to also purchase Bravo\'s and Carol\'s shares on the same per-share terms.</p><p>If both tag, the buyer ends up with 100% &mdash; but is paying for it. If neither tags, the buyer ends up with only 60%, with two unhappy minority shareholders subject to whatever consent rights and fiduciary protections their original shareholders\' agreement gives them.</p><p>In practice, deals like this are conditioned on either (i) the drag-along being amended to lower the threshold, (ii) the minorities being convinced to sign separate share-purchase agreements, or (iii) the deal being restructured (e.g. a UK scheme of arrangement to sweep up all shares at once via court sanction).</p>',
