    title: 'Revlon and the higher overbid: when the board cannot say no',
    setup: '<p>A Delaware corporation\'s board approves a sale of the company to a strategic buyer for $50 per share in cash. A competing bidder emerges three weeks later offering $58 per share, also in cash, with no financing condition. The board refuses to engage with the competing bidder, citing the existing merger agreement and a fiduciary out clause that limits termination to a "Superior Proposal" defined by reference to long-term value.</p>',
    prompt: 'What duties does the board owe, and what is its likely path?',
    modelAnswer: '<p>Once the board has decided to sell the company, Revlon enhanced scrutiny applies &mdash; the board\'s duty becomes the maximisation of immediate stockholder value (<em>Revlon, Inc. v MacAndrews &amp; Forbes Holdings</em>, 506 A.2d 173 (Del. 1986)). A $58 cash offer is, on its face, a higher immediate value than a $50 cash offer.</p><p>The fiduciary out clause, properly drafted, must permit the board to consider unsolicited superior proposals and to terminate to accept one (subject to a break fee). Ignoring a clearly higher cash bid would expose directors to a Revlon claim from stockholders.</p><p>The likely path: the board engages with the competing bidder under the fiduciary out, runs a brief auction or matching-rights process, and either the original buyer raises its price or the board terminates and accepts the higher offer (paying the break fee). Refusing engagement entirely would breach Revlon duties.</p>',
