    setup: '<p>You are reviewing the first draft of a UK private-company SPA. The "Loss" definition reads: "Loss means any loss, damage, cost or expense incurred by the Buyer, including indirect or consequential losses". The buyer\'s tax adviser has flagged that, post-completion, HMRC is likely to issue an assessment of approximately £4 million for an unfunded historic transfer-pricing exposure that the seller has not disclosed.</p>',
    prompt: 'Is the inclusion of "indirect or consequential losses" in the "Loss" definition buyer-friendly or seller-friendly? What further drafting changes would you suggest?',
    modelAnswer: '<p>At first glance the inclusion of "indirect or consequential" looks buyer-friendly, but in English law it is at best neutral and at worst counter-productive. English law treats "indirect or consequential" as a term of art covering only second-limb <em>Hadley v. Baxendale</em> losses, so explicit inclusion of those does not expand recovery beyond what would otherwise have been recoverable under a generic "Loss" definition (first-limb losses are already recoverable as direct loss). More importantly, including the words might invite the seller, on the next draft, to negotiate a <em>carve-out</em> of "indirect or consequential" — which would now genuinely narrow the buyer\'s recovery.</p><p><strong>Suggested drafting:</strong> redraft as <em>"Loss means any loss, damage, cost or expense suffered by the Buyer, including loss of profits and goodwill that flow from the breach as a natural consequence and including all third-party claims, fines and penalties, but excluding [defined narrow categories]."</em> This preserves recovery of natural-consequence lost profits, captures specific concerns, and avoids the consequential-loss term-of-art trap.</p><p><strong>For the HMRC tax exposure</strong>, do not rely on the general indemnity / warranty regime; negotiate a specific <strong>tax indemnity</strong> (often called a "Tax Covenant" in UK SPAs or "Tax Deed") on a £-for-£ basis with no de minimis or basket, and a <strong>specific indemnity</strong> (with its own survival period and cap) for the identified transfer-pricing matter — this isolates a known issue from the general warranty / indemnity regime and removes the materiality and de minimis hurdles that would otherwise apply.</p>',
