    prompt: 'How do you manage the disclosure form question, and what are the practical consequences for the disclosure exercise?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Pick one form and stick with it.</strong> Mixing the two creates risk: a disclosure mentioned in one document but missing from the other can be argued not to have been "disclosed" effectively against a particular warranty.</p><p><strong>For a Delaware-governed SPA</strong>, the conventional choice is US-style disclosure schedules, numbered to track the warranty article (Schedule 3.7(a) qualifies §3.7(a)). Schedules are attached to the SPA and form part of it. Each schedule item should cross-reference the specific warranty it qualifies; a "general" disclosure section is uncommon in US drafting.</p><p><strong>If the parties insist on a UK-style Disclosure Letter</strong>, the SPA must include a clear interpretation clause specifying that (i) the Disclosure Letter is delivered as a separate document but is fully effective to qualify the warranties, (ii) general disclosures (e.g. "matters publicly available on Companies House" or "matters in the data room as at [Date]") are deemed disclosed against each warranty unless context requires otherwise, and (iii) specific disclosures are linked to numbered warranties.</p><p><strong>Practical consequences:</strong></p><ul><li>The disclosure exercise is the seller\'s primary tool for limiting warranty liability — every known issue must be disclosed clearly and specifically. Vague disclosures ("general legal risk") do not work in either Delaware or UK practice.</li><li>Update mechanism: if the deal has a gap between signing and closing, the SPA should specify whether the disclosure can be updated to closing (typical in UK; less common in US) and what effect a closing-date update has on warranty claims.</li><li>Bring-down conditions: if warranties are brought down at closing, the bring-down typically refers to the disclosure as updated at closing, narrowing the buyer\'s ability to claim for matters disclosed in the update.</li><li>Specific indemnities for known disclosed issues: the buyer should negotiate specific indemnities for material disclosed risks (e.g. the open litigation, the open tax dispute), separating these from the general warranty / disclosure regime.</li></ul><p><strong>For the Italian subsidiary,</strong> ensure that any disclosure schedule items relating to it satisfy local-law concepts (e.g. environmental matters, employee claims under Articolo 2112 of the Italian Civil Code on going-concern transfers) — Italian counsel should review the relevant schedule entries.</p>',
