    setup: 'A US private equity sponsor is acquiring an Italian family-owned manufacturing target with operations in Italy, Germany, and Poland. The sellers are Italian individuals. The target has key intellectual property rights registered in Germany. The buyer demands an arbitration clause; the Italian sellers are unfamiliar with arbitration and concerned about losing access to courts.',
    prompt: 'Recommend a dispute resolution structure: governing law, institution, seat, number of arbitrators, language, and any carve-outs. Justify each choice.',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Governing law:</strong> English law (neutral; well-developed commercial law; familiar to PE sponsor\'s counsel; party autonomy upheld under Rome I).</p><p><strong>Institution:</strong> ICC arbitration (most flexible internationally; familiar to Italian counsel; Paris is the institutional headquarters but seat is parties\' choice).</p><p><strong>Seat:</strong> Paris or London — both arbitration-friendly with sophisticated supervisory courts.</p><p><strong>Number of arbitrators:</strong> Three (each party appoints one; they appoint the chair) — appropriate for a substantial deal value.</p><p><strong>Language:</strong> English, with explicit permission to file Italian, German, and Polish original documents without translation (saves significant cost; reflects the reality of the underlying business).</p><p><strong>Carve-outs:</strong> (i) interim relief (freezing orders, preservation orders) available from any court of competent jurisdiction; (ii) IP enforcement matters carve out to the relevant patent court (Germany and Poland have their own patent courts; English IP claims to the IPEC).</p><p><strong>Multi-tier clause:</strong> 30-day senior-executive negotiation period before mediation, then 60-day CEDR mediation, then ICC arbitration — drafted with specific institutional rules to be enforceable under Cable & Wireless v IBM.</p><p><strong>Confidentiality:</strong> explicit confidentiality clause (ICC Rules do not impose default confidentiality, unlike LCIA).</p>'
