/* Scenario fragment — Module 00 (per-module override). Replaces all letter-fragment items with module === '00'. */
window.__SCEN_M00 = [
  {
    id: 'M00-01', module: '00',
    title: 'The partner sentence — translate or freeze',
    setup: '<p>You are sitting in your second meeting with a senior M&amp;A partner you are pitching to co-found a boutique AI law firm. After 10 minutes on the AI tooling roadmap, the partner pivots to a current deal she is running and says, almost in passing:</p><p style="margin-left:1em;"><em>"It\'s a UK PE exit, locked box dated end-March, ticker at 5%, ten-percent escrow for the W&amp;I retention. We\'re fighting over a knowledge scrape on the IP warranty and whether tax sweeps the basket. The buyer wants a true deductible at 1% but seller is pushing for a tipping basket. Hell-or-high-water on the antitrust because of the Phase II risk in Germany. Bring-down on the fundamentals only — they accepted MAC-qualified on the rest."</em></p>',
    prompt: 'Translate the sentence in your head and identify the four most-negotiated points. What is one sharp question you could ask the partner that signals you understand the architecture rather than just the words?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Translation.</strong></p><ul><li><em>UK PE exit, locked box dated end-March</em> — UK private-equity sale; price fixed at 31 March balance sheet; no post-closing true-up.</li><li><em>Ticker at 5%</em> — daily interest-equivalent of 5% per annum on the equity from the locked-box date to closing, paid by buyer to seller.</li><li><em>Ten-percent escrow for the W&amp;I retention</em> — 10% of price held in escrow to fund the W&amp;I insurance retention (the deductible the policy doesn\'t cover); released after the survival period subject to claims.</li><li><em>Knowledge scrape on the IP warranty</em> — a buyer-friendly drafting move stripping "to the seller\'s knowledge" qualifiers from the IP warranties for indemnification purposes (so the buyer can claim even where the seller didn\'t know the warranty was breached).</li><li><em>Tax sweeps the basket</em> — sub-de-minimis tax claims count cumulatively toward the basket threshold rather than being disregarded.</li><li><em>True deductible at 1% (buyer) vs tipping basket (seller)</em> — buyer wants the threshold gone forever (only excess recoverable); seller wants tipping (recovery from dollar one once threshold crossed).</li><li><em>Hell-or-high-water on antitrust because of Phase II risk in Germany</em> — buyer takes all regulatory risk including divestitures, because the German Federal Cartel Office (Bundeskartellamt) is likely to push the deal into a Phase II review.</li><li><em>Bring-down on the fundamentals only — MAC-qualified on the rest</em> — at closing, the seller re-confirms only the fundamental warranties (title, capacity, authority) as still true; the general warranties are bring-down at the lower MAC-qualified standard (must be true except for breaches that don\'t rise to a Material Adverse Change).</li></ul><p><strong>The four most-negotiated points.</strong></p><ol><li><em>Tipping basket vs true deductible</em> — direct economic value at stake; seller saves the entire threshold under tipping.</li><li><em>Knowledge scrape on the IP warranty</em> — IP is high-value, hard-to-diligence, and the qualifier shifts allocation of unknown risk.</li><li><em>Bring-down standard (MAC-qualified vs true and accurate)</em> — controls the buyer\'s right to walk between signing and closing if business deteriorates.</li><li><em>Hell-or-high-water vs reasonable best efforts</em> — controls who wears the risk of the German Phase II antitrust review (divestitures, behavioural remedies).</li></ol><p><strong>One sharp question.</strong> "Has the W&amp;I underwriter signed off on the knowledge scrape, or are they pricing the IP risk separately into a higher retention?" — this signals you understand that (i) knowledge scrapes are W&amp;I-relevant (the underwriter prices unknown risk into the policy), (ii) IP is a typical underwriter exclusion or higher-retention item, and (iii) the SPA negotiation and the W&amp;I market negotiation interact. A senior partner will register that the question is at the W&amp;I-underwriter / SPA-drafting interface, which is where partners actually spend their time.</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'M00-02', module: '00',
    title: 'Heads of Terms marked "subject to contract" and the asymmetric civil-law forum',
    setup: '<p>A UK seller and a German buyer sign heads of terms (a non-binding term sheet) for the sale of a German operating subsidiary. The document is headed "subject to contract" throughout. The HoT contemplates a UK-law SPA but a German pre-contractual negotiation phase under German law. Six weeks into negotiations the buyer pulls out, citing a deteriorating market. The seller has spent £400,000 in advisor fees and has been distracted from a parallel sale process. The seller threatens to sue.</p>',
    prompt: 'What are the seller\'s realistic recoveries under (i) English law and (ii) German law, and what does this tell you about why "governing law" of pre-contractual conduct is itself negotiated?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>(i) Under English law.</strong> Weak. "Subject to contract" is a recognised English-law formula meaning the parties do not yet intend to be legally bound. Heads of terms so labelled are not binding (<em>Walford v Miles</em> [1992] 2 AC 128 confirms there is no general duty to negotiate in good faith under English law and an "agreement to negotiate" is itself unenforceable for uncertainty). The seller\'s only English-law recoveries would be (a) payment of any specifically agreed and drafted exclusivity-period break fee, or (b) a breach-of-confidentiality claim if the buyer misused information disclosed under an NDA — neither of which is automatic.</p><p><strong>(ii) Under German law.</strong> Considerably stronger. The German doctrine of <em>culpa in contrahendo</em> (Latin: "fault in negotiating"), codified at BGB § 311(2) since the 2002 Schuldrechtsreform, creates pre-contractual liability for breaking off negotiations in bad faith — particularly where one party has caused the other to incur costs in reliance on the negotiation continuing. Damages typically cover the reliance interest (wasted costs, foregone alternatives), not the expectation interest (the deal that would have been). On these facts, the seller could plausibly recover advisor fees and possibly some element of foregone parallel-process value.</p><p><strong>Why "governing law" of pre-contractual conduct is negotiated.</strong> The buyer wants English-law governance of the pre-contractual phase to neutralise <em>culpa in contrahendo</em> (and its French equivalent <em>abus de droit</em>); the seller wants civil-law governance to retain it. Mature heads of terms address this expressly:</p><ul><li><em>Buyer-friendly:</em> "this document is governed by English law and is subject to contract; no party shall have any liability for the costs or expenses of any other party arising from the negotiation."</li><li><em>Seller-friendly:</em> a binding exclusivity clause, a binding break-fee, and a confidentiality clause carved out of the "subject to contract" header.</li></ul><p><strong>The takeaway.</strong> The "subject to contract" header is necessary but not sufficient — under civil-law governance it does not extinguish reliance liability, and a sophisticated seller in a cross-border deal will negotiate either the law applicable to the pre-contractual phase or specific exclusivity / break-fee protections inside the term sheet itself. Module 03 develops the pre-signing document architecture; Module 16 develops the governing-law / forum-selection negotiation.</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'M00-03', module: '00',
    title: 'The partner pivot — moving a known environmental risk from warranty to specific indemnity',
    setup: '<p>A UK PE-backed manufacturer is being sold to a US strategic buyer. Phase I environmental DD has flagged a likely contamination claim at one historical site (legacy 1980s solvent use; remediation cost estimated £2–8m on Phase I alone, undefined on the upside). The buyer\'s lawyer pushes for a specific indemnity outside the W&amp;I cap; the seller\'s lawyer says the existing environmental warranty is sufficient and the W&amp;I policy already covers it.</p>',
    prompt: 'What changes if the parties agree to move the risk from warranty to specific indemnity? Walk through the domino effect on at least four other clauses.',
    modelAnswer: '<p>Moving a known risk from warranty to specific indemnity is a textbook domino: a single allocation choice cascades into multiple clauses. Concretely:</p><ol><li><strong>W&amp;I policy scope.</strong> A known risk identified in DD is almost always a W&amp;I exclusion — the underwriter will not cover what the buyer already knows about. So the W&amp;I policy excludes this contamination claim regardless of whether it sits in a warranty or an indemnity. Implication: the seller\'s residual liability outside the W&amp;I structure must back the indemnity directly (escrow or parent guarantee or holdback), or the buyer accepts the risk into its own balance sheet.</li><li><strong>Cap.</strong> A specific indemnity for a known environmental claim typically sits <em>outside</em> the general warranty cap — a separate, higher cap (often the full estimated downside, e.g. £15m) or uncapped on a true-up basis.</li><li><strong>Basket / de minimis.</strong> Specific indemnities almost always sit outside the basket and the de minimis — they are dollar-one recoveries, with no per-claim floor.</li><li><strong>Survival.</strong> General warranty 12–24 months becomes inadequate for environmental long-tail liability; the specific indemnity typically runs 5–10 years or even open-ended (until claims are time-barred under environmental statutes).</li><li><strong>Conduct of claims.</strong> A separate tiered conduct-of-claims regime is needed because the indemnitor (seller) is paying but the contamination likely involves the buyer\'s ongoing operations at the site. Typical compromise: indemnitor takes control above a threshold; indemnitee retains conduct on regulator-facing claims that could affect the operating permit.</li><li><strong>Knowledge / materiality scrape.</strong> Becomes irrelevant for this risk — the indemnity is debt-style, no qualifier needed; the buyer\'s knowledge does not reduce recovery.</li><li><strong>Escrow / security.</strong> A 5–10% escrow may be inadequate for a £2–8m+ exposure. A separate, larger escrow or a parent guarantee may be required to fund the specific indemnity.</li><li><strong>Tax structuring.</strong> An indemnity payment may be taxed differently from a price adjustment — the parties may need to gross up the indemnity, or restructure as a price reduction (which is not what the buyer wants because it goes into the goodwill, not the P&amp;L).</li><li><strong>Disclosure.</strong> The fact that the risk is "known" must be disclosed in the disclosure letter (UK) or disclosure schedules (US) for the warranty package, even though it is then carved out into a specific indemnity. Failure to disclose properly can affect both the warranty and the indemnity.</li></ol><p><strong>The headline insight.</strong> A senior partner saying <em>"let\'s move that to a specific indemnity"</em> is making a single drafting choice that triggers six to ten downstream changes. That is what the report\'s "domino effect" frame is trying to install. Modules 12 (warranty), 13 (indemnity / conduct of claims), and 14 (W&amp;I) develop the full mechanics.</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  }
];

/* Module 01 scenarios refresh — stress-test v3 fix-pass.
   Replaces A-01-* scenarios. Refreshed scenarios surface stress-test concerns:
   founder voting class + s.979 dual test interaction; Revlon updated to QVC
   change-of-control framing; § 363 architecture and synthetic W&I implication. */
window.__SCEN_M01 = [
  {
    id: 'M01-01', module: '01',
    title: 'Founder voting class and the s.979 dual test',
    setup: '<p>A UK private limited company has a two-class share structure. Class A ordinary shares (10m issued, 1 vote each) are widely held by venture investors and employees. Class B founder shares (1m issued, 10 votes each) are held by the two founders. A US strategic acquirer launches a contractual offer at £8 per share for all shares (treating Class A and Class B identically by economic value), and after the offer closes has accepted at 95% of Class A shares but only 60% of Class B shares.</p>',
    prompt: 'Can the buyer trigger s.979 squeeze-out? If not, what should it do?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Squeeze-out under CA 2006 s.979 fails.</strong> The dual test requires 90% in <em>value</em> of the shares to which the offer relates AND 90% of the <em>voting rights</em> carried by those shares.</p><ul><li><strong>Value test</strong> (assuming uniform per-share economic value): 95% of Class A (9.5m of 10m) + 60% of Class B (0.6m of 1m) = 10.1m of 11m total = ~92% in value. <em>Pass</em>.</li><li><strong>Voting test</strong>: 9.5m × 1 + 0.6m × 10 = 9.5m + 6m = 15.5m votes accepted. Total voting rights = 10m × 1 + 1m × 10 = 20m. 15.5m / 20m = 77.5%. <em>Fail</em>.</li></ul><p>Because the voting test fails, the squeeze-out is unavailable until the buyer reaches 90% on both axes. With only 60% of the founder Class B accepted, the gap is structural, not cosmetic.</p><p><strong>What the buyer should do:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Switch to a scheme of arrangement</strong> (CA 2006 ss.895–901). Schemes need majority in number representing 75% in value of <em>each voting class present and voting</em>, plus court sanction. Class B founders would form a separate class for scheme purposes — but at 75% the threshold is much lower than 90%, and the buyer may already have enough commitment.</li><li><strong>Negotiate directly with the holdout founders</strong>: a top-up offer on the Class B (a separate higher consideration, justified to advisers and to the Takeover Panel under Rule 16 / equality of treatment), or a side-letter sweetener that survives Panel scrutiny.</li><li><strong>Plan for the dual structure as the entrenched outcome</strong>: live with a holding-company minority, recognising that the Class B economics still flow upstream proportionally and that a future restructuring (e.g. court-sanctioned scheme later) is achievable when conditions change.</li></ol><p><strong>The takeaway for the user.</strong> The "s.979 needs 90%" mental shortcut is wrong on dual-class targets. The dual test is the credibility-driver — a senior partner asks early in any UK takeover: "is there a founder voting class? what does the s.979 math look like?"</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'M01-02', module: '01',
    title: 'Drag-along, tag-along and the trapped majority bidder',
    setup: '<p>A UK private limited company has three shareholders: Alpha Holdings (60%), Bravo Ventures (30%) and a founder, Carol (10%). The articles include a drag-along clause triggered at 75% (with a separate class consent from preferred holders required) and a tag-along clause triggered at 50%. Alpha negotiates a sale of all of its shares to a strategic buyer at £10 per share. Bravo holds the only Series A preferred class and refuses to consent to the drag.</p>',
    prompt: 'What rights does each minority shareholder have, and what does the buyer get?',
    modelAnswer: '<p>Alpha alone (60%) cannot trigger the drag — the 75% threshold is unmet, <em>and</em> the class consent of preferred (which Bravo holds) is unobtainable. Alpha can sell its own 60% but cannot compel Bravo or Carol.</p><p>Both minorities have the right to <strong>tag</strong>: Alpha\'s 60% sale exceeds the 50% tag trigger, so Bravo and Carol can each elect to require the buyer to purchase their shares on the same per-share terms. If both tag, the buyer ends up at 100% — but is paying for it. If neither tags, the buyer is at 60% with two unhappy minority shareholders.</p><p><strong>How a senior partner walks this:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Buyer side</strong>: the deal economics typically require 100% (financing covenants, governance reset, integration). The buyer pushes Alpha to deliver tag-acceptances pre-signing, or restructures via a UK <em>scheme of arrangement</em> to sweep all shares at once with court sanction (75% by value of each class plus court).</li><li><strong>Alpha side</strong>: needs to either (i) negotiate a price uplift with Bravo and Carol to secure their tag election, or (ii) accept a partial sale and live with the residual minority (rare for a 60%-holder seller). If a scheme is contemplated, Alpha may need to amend the articles to lower the drag threshold (a special resolution at 75%, which Alpha plus a small portion of preferred can carry).</li><li><strong>Bravo side</strong> (Series A preferred): the class consent gives an effective veto. Bravo is paid (i) for its 30% economic interest plus (ii) any preferred preference / participation rights triggered by the sale. The negotiation is whether Bravo is induced to consent (e.g. a side-letter on its post-closing employment, a price uplift, or an indemnity carve-out for its passive-investor position).</li><li><strong>Carol side</strong> (founder, 10%): typically tags for liquidity, possibly with a partial rollover into the buyer\'s equity if the buyer wants to retain founder commitment.</li></ul><p><strong>The deal architecture compromise.</strong> Most deals like this end up as: (i) Alpha amends the articles to lower the drag threshold and remove the class-consent requirement (special resolution, 75% — feasible if the Series A class consent on amendments themselves can be negotiated); (ii) the buyer structures as a scheme of arrangement to bind the entire share register at 75% per class plus court; or (iii) the deal is restructured as a part-sale with a put-call mechanism delivering 100% over 18–24 months.</p><p><strong>The takeaway for the user.</strong> "Drag at 75% with class consent" is not one provision — it is two. The class consent is the choke point, and the entire deal can hinge on whether one preferred holder can be brought along.</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  }
];

/* Per-module scenarios fragment for Module 02 — The M&A Landscape & Process.
 * v3 stress-test refresh. Replaces A-02-* with M02-01..M02-02. */
window.__SCEN_M02 = [
  {
    id: 'M02-01', module: '02',
    title: 'Bilateral 8x EBITDA offer vs full auction: the German specialty chemicals seller',
    setup: '<p>A founder owns 100% of a profitable, family-built specialty chemicals business in Germany generating &euro;30m EBITDA. He has been approached unsolicited by a US strategic acquirer offering 8x EBITDA. His investment banker recommends running a competitive auction instead, projecting that 2&ndash;3 strategic and 4&ndash;5 PE bidders could be brought to the table. The banker proposes a 1.5% success fee on EV with a tail of 24 months and a "Datasite" VDR. The founder is impatient and worries about leak risk to customers and employees.</p>',
    prompt: 'Walk through the trade-offs the founder should evaluate, the engagement-letter terms his lawyer should push back on, and the structural compromise an experienced M&amp;A partner would propose.',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Trade-offs (the headline decision).</strong></p><ul><li><em>Bilateral acceptance:</em> faster (4&ndash;6 months vs 8&ndash;12 for an auction); confidential (avoids customer / employee disruption from leaks); less management distraction; lower process cost. But: leaves money on the table &mdash; even a single competing bid usually adds materially to price. At &euro;30m EBITDA the difference between 8x and 10x is &euro;60m; surfacing one credible competing bidder typically pays for the entire process and then some. An auction may also surface a strategic with synergies justifying a higher multiple than the unsolicited bidder offered.</li><li><em>Auction risks:</em> leak (mitigated by tight buyer list, staggered information release, no-touch protocols at the data room); failed auction (residual buyer leverage damaged if the process drags or signals are weak); cost (banker fee + legal + financial / commercial / tax / environmental DD often runs 2&ndash;3% of EV mid-market).</li></ul><p><strong>Engagement-letter pushback (six core levers).</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Success-fee base:</strong> 1.5% on EV is reasonable for &euro;30m EBITDA at 8&ndash;10x (i.e. &euro;240&ndash;300m EV). Push for equity value, not EV (after net debt deduction); push for a minimum dollar floor only, not a ratchet up.</li><li><strong>Tail length:</strong> 24 months is bank-friendly. Negotiate down to 12&ndash;18; tighten "introduced by the bank" so it excludes the unsolicited US strategic and any party the founder already knew or sourced through other channels.</li><li><strong>Retainer credit:</strong> ensure dollar-for-dollar credit against the success fee.</li><li><strong>Fairness-opinion separateness:</strong> not a public deal so probably not needed; but reserve flexibility to commission a separate opinion if the founder later wants one.</li><li><strong>Expense cap:</strong> &euro;250&ndash;500k for mid-market; uncapped is bank-friendly.</li><li><strong>Indemnity carve-outs:</strong> bank seeks broad indemnity; carve out gross negligence, wilful misconduct, fraud.</li></ul><p><strong>Structural compromise an experienced partner would propose.</strong></p><p>Run a "<strong>limited / targeted auction</strong>": invite 5&ndash;8 carefully selected bidders (the unsolicited US strategic plus 2&ndash;3 strategic competitors plus 3&ndash;4 mid-market PE funds with chemicals capability). Compress the timeline (single bid round instead of two if the deal is straightforward); use vendor DD (financial QoE + legal + tax) to accelerate. The unsolicited bidder is invited but no longer has bilateral leverage. If the founder genuinely values speed and confidentiality, propose a "<strong>stalking-horse-style</strong>" hybrid: agree exclusivity quickly with one credible bidder while signalling availability of two or three others in the background as competitive insurance against retrade. Keep the second-best bidder warm even after preferred-bidder selection &mdash; the partner&rsquo;s standing instruction.</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'M02-02', module: '02',
    title: 'US public-target board: fairness-opinion banker conflict and the cleansing playbook',
    setup: '<p>A US-listed industrial company\'s board has just received a non-binding takeover proposal from a European competitor at a 20% premium to the prevailing share price. Management is divided. The board\'s long-standing M&amp;A advisor (Bank A) has been simultaneously providing staple financing to the proposed buyer and has a partner who personally owns equity in the buyer. Bank A has not yet disclosed these facts to the board. An associate at the company\'s outside counsel quietly raises the issue at a pre-board call.</p>',
    prompt: 'What are the litigation and process risks if the board proceeds to recommendation without addressing the conflict, and what cleansing playbook would a senior M&amp;A partner deploy?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Litigation and process risks.</strong></p><p>The fact pattern echoes <em>In re Del Monte Foods Co. Shareholders Litigation</em>, 25 A.3d 813 (Del. Ch. 2011) (Vice Chancellor Laster) &mdash; staple-financing conflict not timely disclosed; ~$89m settlement &mdash; and <em>In re El Paso Corp. Shareholder Litigation</em>, 41 A.3d 432 (Del. Ch. 2012) (Chancellor Strine) &mdash; banker partner with personal Kinder Morgan stake; ~$110m settlement, deal allowed to close because enjoining would have deprived stockholders of premium. The Delaware Chancery message: the duty of loyalty governs <em>process</em> as well as <em>price</em>; banker-conflict claims can survive even where the deal price is fair. Failure to address the conflict risks (i) a stockholder injunction enjoining the vote pending corrective disclosures; (ii) an extended "go-shop" forced by the court; (iii) significant settlement payments funded by the company\'s D&amp;O insurance and ultimately by the company; (iv) reputational harm to directors.</p><p><strong>Cleansing playbook (the partner\'s drill).</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Disclose immediately, fully, in writing.</strong> Bank A discloses to the full board: the staple-financing engagement, the partner\'s personal stake, the magnitude of fees on each side, the genesis of the buyer pool. Disclosure goes to the proxy / Schedule 14A.</li><li><strong>Commission an independent fairness opinion from a second bank not on the deal.</strong> "We need a second opinion to defuse the appraisal action." The partner\'s standing playbook: identify a bulge-bracket or specialist boutique with no buyer relationships; engage on a separate engagement letter with no contingent fee; deliver opinion before signing.</li><li><strong>Reconstitute the deal team.</strong> The Bank A partner with the personal stake is removed from the deal; a separate Bank A partner with no buyer exposure runs the seller process going forward. Consider engaging a separate advisor for any fairness-related work.</li><li><strong>Form a Special Committee of independent directors</strong> (if not already in place) with separate independent counsel and its own financial advisor. The committee runs the negotiation and the recommendation; the conflicted advisor is supplemental rather than primary.</li><li><strong>Run a market check.</strong> Banker (preferably the unconflicted second bank) quietly contacts 2&ndash;3 likely alternative bidders to test interest. Documentation of the market check in board minutes is critical.</li><li><strong>Negotiate appropriate deal-protection.</strong> Break fee in the typical 2.5&ndash;4% range &mdash; not preclusive (<em>Omnicare</em>); fiduciary out preserved; matching rights of 3&ndash;5 business days; consider a go-shop window of 25&ndash;50 days to actively market the company post-signing as further <em>Revlon</em>-defence cleansing.</li><li><strong>Document everything.</strong> Board minutes record the conflict, the disclosure, the steps taken, the second opinion, the market check, the negotiation. Counsel prepares a complete "process record" for the inevitable stockholder litigation.</li></ol><p>Result: with timely disclosure, a second independent fairness opinion, a Special Committee, and a market check, the board has constructed a defensible process record. The bank&rsquo;s conflict no longer presumptively taints the deal &mdash; under <em>Corwin v KKR Financial Holdings</em>, 125 A.3d 304 (Del. 2015), a fully informed, uncoerced stockholder vote can lower the standard of review to business judgment.</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  }
];

// Module 03 (Pre-Signing Documents) per-module scenarios override fragment.
// Replaces all B-03-* scenarios via build-data.sh per-module override mechanism.
// 2 scenarios per stress-test-03 v3 fix-agent priorities.
window.__SCEN_M03 = [
  {
    id: 'M03-01',
    module: '03',
    title: 'NDA, exclusivity and the system view: a UK-listed software target',
    setup: 'You represent a UK-listed software target ("TargetCo") approached by two PE firms (US BidderA and German BidderB) about a take-private. The board worries about: (i) confidential pricing data leaking to BidderA\'s portfolio companies in adjacent markets; (ii) being "locked up" with a single bidder; (iii) employee poaching; (iv) being caught by Delaware <em>Revlon</em>-style claims if a topping bid surfaces post-signing; (v) US BidderA pushes for "10-week initial exclusivity, automatically renewing while DD continues" and "no go-shop, no fiduciary out, no break-fee cap." German BidderB asks for a six-page LOI labelled "non-binding" but specifying detailed price, structure and 12 weeks of "good-faith negotiation."',
    prompt: 'Identify (a) the NDA provisions most important to TargetCo and how to calibrate them; (b) the Code-imposed framework that affects the deal-protection package; (c) the specific drafting risks in BidderB\'s LOI; (d) the system-level effects of agreeing to BidderA\'s 10-week auto-renewing exclusivity.',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>(a) NDA &mdash; key levers for TargetCo (UK plc).</strong></p><ol><li><em>One-way NDA</em> (TargetCo discloses; bidders receive); resist mutuality.</li><li><em>Standstill</em> 12&ndash;24 months minimum, prohibiting any equity acquisition, tender offer, proxy solicitation or joint action; standard fall-aways (third-party definitive agreement; tender offer for &gt;50%; recommended third-party transaction); avoid "don\'t-ask-don\'t-waive" against credible alternative bidders (<em>In re Celera</em>, <em>In re Compellent</em>); carve-out for action with TargetCo board\'s prior written consent (preserves the structural fiduciary-out function).</li><li><em>Use clause</em> tightly scoped &mdash; "evaluating a possible negotiated transaction with TargetCo." Creates implicit standstill (<em>Martin Marietta v. Vulcan</em>). Explicitly capture the <em>RAA Management</em> non-reliance language for the seller side: "the buyer relies only on the express representations and not on any pre-contractual statements" &mdash; this is upstream of, and capable of overriding, the SPA\'s entire-agreement architecture.</li><li>No residuals clause; if pushed, "general industry knowledge only" with explicit exclusion for trade secrets, customer lists, pricing, source code, R&amp;D roadmap, personally identifiable information.</li><li><em>Non-solicit</em> 18&ndash;24 months covering all employees; non-hire (stronger) for senior management. Note FTC non-compete rule was permanently abandoned 5 September 2025 &mdash; sale-of-business and senior-executive equity-linked carve-outs explicitly preserved by the FTC.</li><li>Tiered disclosure &mdash; pricing, customer data and R&amp;D roadmap only via clean team after Phase II indicative offer (Module 04 mechanic).</li><li>Return-or-destroy at end of process; written officer\'s certificate.</li><li>English law; English courts; "irreparable harm" injunction language.</li><li>Bidders contractually responsible for breaches by Affiliates and Representatives (avoid the "reasonable efforts to ensure compliance" gap).</li></ol><p><strong>(b) UK Takeover Code framework.</strong> The Code displaces much of the contractual deal-protection apparatus US lawyers reach for:</p><ul><li><em>Rule 21.2</em> &mdash; offer-related arrangements benefiting the bidder are categorically prohibited. Where break fees are exceptionally permitted (formal sale process under Rule 2.6(d); white-knight rescue; serious financial distress; Rule 21.1 transactions under the 2024 RS 2024/1 reforms in force 3 February 2025), the cap is 1% of aggregate offer value on a fully diluted basis, payable only on a competing offer becoming unconditional.</li><li><em>Rule 25</em> &mdash; the target board must publish its substantive recommendation; the structural duty replaces a contractual fiduciary out.</li><li><em>Rule 2.7</em> &mdash; a firm intention announcement is permissible only with committed financing. Triggers the certain-funds requirement and the Rule 24.8 cash confirmation by the bidder\'s financial adviser.</li><li><em>Rule 2.6 ("put up or shut up")</em> &mdash; possible offeror must announce a Rule 2.7 firm intention, or withdraw and accept a 6-month lock-out, within 28 days of being identified.</li></ul><p>Bottom line: BidderA\'s "no go-shop, no fiduciary out, no break-fee cap" demand is largely incompatible with the Code; TargetCo can rely on the structural protection rather than negotiate them as contractual carve-outs.</p><p><strong>(c) BidderB\'s LOI &mdash; specific drafting risks.</strong></p><ol><li>"Non-binding" label is not conclusive in Germany. <em>Culpa in contrahendo</em> under <strong>BGB §311(2) read with §§280(1) and 241(2)</strong> can impose pre-contractual liability for bad-faith withdrawal despite the label. Damages: <em>negative interest</em> (out-of-pocket costs and lost opportunities), not <em>positive interest</em> (lost deal profit). Italy: <strong>CC Art. 1337</strong>. France: <strong>Code civil Arts. 1112 and 1112-1</strong> (post-Ordinance n°2016-131, in force 1 October 2016).</li><li>"Good-faith negotiation" obligation: under English law unenforceable for uncertainty (<em>Walford v. Miles</em> [1992] 2 AC 128 (Lord Ackner)); under Delaware law potentially enforceable as a Type II preliminary agreement (<em>Teachers Insurance v. Tribune</em>, 670 F. Supp. 491 (S.D.N.Y. 1987) (Leval, J.); <em>SIGA Technologies v. PharmAthene</em>, 132 A.3d 1108 (Del. 2015), $113m on remand). Risk: the parties may be litigating the SIGA framework offshore even though the deal is UK-flavoured. Specify governing law and pick a non-binding-friendly jurisdiction.</li><li>Detailed price and structure in the LOI: in civil-law systems, makes the LOI look more like a binding contract. Insert an explicit pre-contractual liability waiver, address cost responsibility, and limit reliance language.</li><li>"Subject to contract" disclaimer should be prominent (English law: <em>RTS Flexible Systems v. Molkerei Alois Müller</em> [2010] UKSC 14 &mdash; powerful but conduct can override).</li></ol><p><strong>(d) System-level effects of 10-week auto-renewing exclusivity.</strong></p><ul><li><em>Disclosure Letter timing.</em> 10+ weeks gives the seller time to prepare a fulsome Disclosure Letter (Module 12), narrowing the buyer\'s warranty set. Conversely, BidderA gets more DD time and can mark up the SPA more aggressively.</li><li><em>Closing-accounts vs. locked box (Module 11).</em> Longer exclusivity tends to favour locked-box mechanics; the locked-box accounts can be properly cut.</li><li><em>MAC walk-right (Module 09).</em> Auto-renewing exclusivity stretches the signing-to-closing window where MAC risk lives; the seller should resist auto-renewal precisely because it lengthens the MAC tail.</li><li><em>Topping bid risk.</em> Extended exclusivity on a UK-listed target without a fall-away on Rule 2.4 announcements by other bidders is dangerous &mdash; if a third party wall-crosses major shareholders and emerges with hard irrevocables, TargetCo is locked out by the exclusivity but obligated to recommend the higher offer under Rule 25. Build in a fall-away on competing Rule 2.4 announcements.</li><li><em>Counter-proposal:</em> 6&ndash;8 weeks initial; one extension of 2&ndash;4 weeks by mutual written consent; carve-out for receipt of unsolicited approaches with notification to BidderA; structural protection by the Code rather than a contractual fiduciary out.</li></ul>',
    type: 'clause-spotting'
  },
  {
    id: 'M03-02',
    module: '03',
    title: 'Commitment papers, ECL, Limited Guarantee on a $1bn PE take-private',
    setup: 'BuyerCo (PE sponsor) signs a $1bn cash-only SPA to acquire TargetCo (US public). BidCo is a thin acquisition vehicle. Financing: $400m equity from Fund VII, $600m debt arranged by Lead Bank. Reverse termination fee: $50m for financing failure, $80m for antitrust failure. SPA longstop: 12 months. Debt commitment letter: bridge facility expiring 11 months from signing; "events of default include any change-of-control event in any other Fund VII portfolio company" (drafted by the lender and not yet pushed back); lenders\' MAE definition references "any material adverse change in the financial markets generally."',
    prompt: 'Identify the SunGard-clean problems and the ECL / Limited Guarantee structural questions; quantify the maximum sponsor exposure; describe the partner-level fixes.',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>SunGard-clean failures.</strong></p><ol><li><em>Bridge expiry mismatch.</em> Bridge expires 11 months from signing; SPA longstop is 12 months. One-month financing gap if closing slips to month 11&ndash;12. Fix: extend bridge expiry to longstop + 30-day buffer.</li><li><em>Lender event-of-default condition broader than SPA.</em> "Any change-of-control event in any other Fund VII portfolio company" is incremental conditionality not mirrored in the SPA. Classic SunGard violation. Fix: delete entirely, or scope to BidCo\'s own credit events only, or mirror as a CP in the SPA (the seller will refuse).</li><li><em>MAE definition mismatch.</em> Lenders\' MAE references "financial markets generally"; SPA MAE almost certainly defines MAE narrowly to exclude general market conditions (the "disproportionate impact" carve-out is standard). Result: a market downturn could trigger lenders\' MAE without triggering the SPA MAE. Fix: align MAE definitions or define lenders\' MAE by reference to the SPA.</li><li><em>Specified Representations list missing.</em> The commitment letter does not appear to identify a defined sub-set of SPA representations whose accuracy is a financing condition. Fix: agree the Specified Representations list (corporate existence, authority, capitalisation, no conflict, due execution, sanctions/AML, validity of security) and the separate "Specified Acquisition Agreement Representations" the buyer can refuse to close on.</li></ol><p><strong>ECL and Limited Guarantee structure.</strong></p><ul><li><em>ECL.</em> $400m commitment from Fund VII to BidCo. Capped at $400m. Survives until closing or termination of the SPA. Conditional only on SPA closing conditions and simultaneous funding of debt. Seller is named as express third-party beneficiary (Delaware: explicit designation; UK equivalent: <em>Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999</em>). Common drafting defect: ECL conditional on lenders funding without backstop &mdash; if lenders walk on a non-SunGard-aligned condition, the ECL also walks. Fix: ECL conditioned only on the buyer-side controllable closing conditions, not on lender behaviour.</li><li><em>Limited Guarantee.</em> Separate guarantee by Fund VII of BidCo\'s reverse break-fee obligations: $50m financing-failure RBF + $80m antitrust-failure RBF = $130m aggregate guarantee cap.</li><li><em>Maximum sponsor exposure</em> = ECL cap ($400m, only payable on closing in any event) + LG cap ($130m, payable on a buyer-fault termination). Total <strong>$130m exposure</strong> on failed deal; $400m on consummated deal (which becomes an investment, not a liability).</li><li>Partner shorthand: "what\'s the LG capped at?" &mdash; answer: $130m, equal to the sum of the two RBFs.</li></ul><p><strong>Reverse break-fee asymmetry rationale.</strong></p><p>$50m financing failure (5% of $1bn) vs. $80m antitrust failure (8% of $1bn). The asymmetry reflects the <em>specific identifiable risk</em> being underwritten. Antitrust risk is more concentrated and harder to mitigate ex post, hence the higher fee. Both fees are <em>primary</em> payment obligations triggered on defined events &mdash; not "liquidated damages on breach" &mdash; and therefore sit outside the English-law penalty rule entirely (post-<em>Cavendish</em> [2015] UKSC 67), even on an English-law SPA.</p><p><strong>Partner-level fixes (in order of priority).</strong></p><ol><li>Extend bridge expiry to longstop + 30 days.</li><li>Delete the cross-portfolio change-of-control event of default; tighten Specified Representations list to a closed enumerated set.</li><li>Align MAE definitions (lender MAE by reference to SPA MAE).</li><li>Make the ECL enforceable by seller as named third-party beneficiary, conditioned only on buyer-controllable closing conditions.</li><li>Document Limited Guarantee as a primary payment obligation (not liquidated damages on breach) to sidestep penalty doctrine.</li><li>If the SPA is governed by English law, drop "events of default" boilerplate that maps to penalty-doctrine triggers; structure all RBF mechanics as primary obligations on defined events.</li></ol>',
    type: 'numerical'
  }
];

// Scenarios fragment M04 — Module 04 (Due Diligence)
// Per-module override: REPLACES all module='04' scenarios in scenarios-B.js when build runs.
// Sources: research/B-presigning-dd-valuation.md (B-04-01 retained, refreshed); research/stress-test-04.md (basket / scrape / DD-outputs system view).
window.__SCEN_M04 = [
  {
    id: 'M04-01',
    module: '04',
    title: 'Mapping DD findings to SPA mechanisms — UK private trade buy',
    setup: 'A US trade buyer (BuyerCo) acquires a UK private target (TargetCo) for £450m on a cash-free, debt-free basis. DD findings: (a) €4m pending tax assessment in TargetCo\'s German subsidiary, mid-litigation; (b) €3m unsupported EBITDA add-back claimed for "founder\'s salary normalisation"; (c) TargetCo\'s largest customer (15% of revenue) has issued an RFP for new vendors and the contract expires 8 months post-closing; (d) historical solvent contamination at a leased manufacturing site, with landlord consent required for remediation; (e) the CEO has a £5m change-of-control bonus on signing and is critical to retention.',
    prompt: 'For each finding, identify the most appropriate SPA risk-allocation response, the key drafting points, and where the finding lands in the equity bridge or interim-period mechanics.',
    modelAnswer: '<ul><li><strong>(a) Tax assessment</strong> — <em>specific indemnity</em>, uncapped, no de minimis or basket, €4m escrow for ~7 years (German tax statute of limitations). Add a seller covenant to cooperate on conduct of the proceedings (M13 / M17). W&I will exclude the known matter, so the indemnity is the only recourse.</li><li><strong>(b) EBITDA add-back</strong> — <em>price chip</em>; at 10x EBITDA, €3m disallowed = ~£26m off the price (FX-adjusted). Land it in EV (clean) rather than as a debt-like item, to avoid leaving fresh disputes for completion accounts (<a href="11-purchase-price.html">M11</a>).</li><li><strong>(c) Customer RFP</strong> — too uncertain to indemnify cleanly. Three options: (i) <em>price chip</em>; (ii) <em>earn-out</em> tied to customer-retention milestones (M11); (iii) <em>CP</em> for written customer renewal pre-closing (seller will resist as creating a hold-up). Most common compromise: a portion of price held back for 12 months conditional on customer retention.</li><li><strong>(d) Environmental + landlord</strong> — <em>specific indemnity</em> (capped at price, no de minimis, escrow ~1.5x estimated cost for 5–7 years); <em>CP</em> for landlord consent (binary, clean); optional <em>CP</em> for Phase II intrusive testing with right to renegotiate or walk if results materially worse than estimate.</li><li><strong>(e) CoC bonus</strong> — <em>debt-like item</em> in the equity bridge (deduct from price; M11 / M05); <em>disclosure</em> in the disclosure letter; <em>CP</em> for new CEO contract and bonus waiver/release. If retention is critical, structure a transaction bonus + management equity rollover (M05 / M15) tying the CEO\'s upside to the buyer\'s plan.</li></ul><p><strong>Bring-down implication:</strong> if any of (a)–(d) materially worsens between signing and closing, expect a fight at the bring-down certificate over whether the change qualifies the warranties (UK practice locks the disclosure letter at signing; US practice often allows "Update Disclosures" that cure the bring-down but not indemnification).</p>',
    type: 'clause-spotting'
  },
  {
    id: 'M04-02',
    module: '04',
    title: 'Tightening the basket — partner-meeting negotiation',
    setup: 'You are sitting in a negotiation call between BuyerCo and SellerCo on a $300m US private deal. The current SPA draft has a tipping basket at $1.5m (0.5% of EV) for general warranty claims, a per-claim de minimis of $25k, and a cap of $30m (10% of EV). The seller\'s lead partner says: "let\'s tighten the basket — make it a true deductible at 1%, raise the de minimis to $100k, and bring the cap down to 7.5%." She also says: "we want a single materiality scrape on damages only, not a double scrape, and no knowledge scrape — the W&I underwriting will give you the comfort you need." The buyer\'s deal lead asks you, the junior member of the deal team, to brief them on what is being asked and what to push back on.',
    prompt: 'Brief the buyer\'s deal lead in 5–6 bullets: (i) translate each requested change into its commercial impact; (ii) flag where W&I may or may not deliver "the comfort you need"; (iii) identify the buyer\'s strongest pushback on each item.',
    modelAnswer: '<ul><li><strong>Basket — tipping ($1.5m) → true deductible ($3m).</strong> Three layers of seller win: (1) threshold doubles (0.5% → 1% of EV); (2) the $3m itself becomes unrecoverable (gone forever, not just a trigger); (3) the seller gains a "$3m cushion" before any dollar flows back. Buyer pushback: hold the line on tipping at the increased threshold; if forced to deductible, tie it to a smaller percentage (0.5–0.75%).</li><li><strong>De minimis $25k → $100k per claim.</strong> Filters out individual losses below $100k from even counting toward the basket. Mid-market range is €/£50k–250k; $100k is reasonable. Pushback only if the target has a pattern of small recurring liabilities; otherwise concede.</li><li><strong>Cap 10% → 7.5% of EV.</strong> Reduces total recoverable from $30m to $22.5m. Combined with the deductible move, the seller\'s effective exposure on general warranties drops from $28.5m to $19.5m — a 32% reduction. Buyer pushback: hold cap at 10%, or trade it against the deductible move.</li><li><strong>Single materiality scrape on damages only, no double.</strong> Buyer must still prove a "material" breach (the scrape doesn\'t apply to the breach test); but once breach is proven, damages are calculated without the materiality filter applied a second time. Reasonable compromise — single scrape on damages is in 50%+ of US private deals (ABA Deal Points). Buyer pushback: agree single scrape on damages but lower the cap-erosion.</li><li><strong>No knowledge scrape.</strong> The seller is asking that "to the seller\'s knowledge" qualifiers continue to bite both at the breach test and at damages. Buyer pushback: knowledge scrape on damages-only is reasonable and increasingly common; resist a "no scrape at all" outcome especially on fundamental and tax warranties (which are typically not knowledge-qualified to begin with).</li><li><strong>"W&I underwriting will give you the comfort you need" — qualified true.</strong> RWI / W&I will indemnify under the policy at the policy retention (typically 0.5% of EV at signing, dropping to 0.25%) and within the policy cap. But: (a) W&I excludes <em>known</em> matters disclosed in the DD report or the data room; (b) W&I underwriters apply their own knowledge limits and may follow or impose additional knowledge limits regardless of the SPA scrape; (c) for a basket structured as a true deductible, the policy retention typically sits at the basket level — the seller is removed from the first-loss layer altogether. Verify with the broker before agreeing.</li></ul>',
    type: 'negotiation'
  }
];

