window.__SCEN_M10 = [
  {
    id: 'M10-01',
    module: '10',
    title: 'Cash-conditional UK private-company closing — back-to-back lender release',
    setup: 'You are running closing on a £180m UK private-company acquisition. CP satisfaction came in late on Friday; closing is set for Tuesday morning. On Monday afternoon you discover that the seller\'s institutional lender has refused to release the floating charge over the target\'s assets unless the pay-off amount (£62m) is received in their account by 10:30am Tuesday in cleared funds. Your CHAPS wire takes 30 minutes to process. The seller\'s counsel insists Closing must complete by midday because the seller\'s CFO is flying out at 1pm.',
    prompt: 'What is the closing-day choreography? What are the partner-language phrases that drive the structure?',
    modelAnswer: 'This is a classic <strong>back-to-back lender release</strong> closing. The pay-off amount must be wired before the share transfer is treated as complete, because if you wire after the share transfer, you have transferred title to a company subject to a continuing charge — exposing the buyer. The single-transaction doctrine actually helps here: you build the closing so that (i) at 09:30 you instruct your bank to wire £62m to the lender; (ii) at 10:00 you instruct release of the share transfer documents into escrow with seller\'s counsel "subject to lender\'s confirmation of receipt and release"; (iii) at 10:30 the lender confirms receipt and emails the deed of release of the charge directly to your firm; (iv) you instruct release of the remainder (£118m) to the seller; (v) seller\'s counsel confirms receipt; (vi) you sign the Completion Memorandum. Partner phrases on the call: "Have we got the back-to-back lender release?", "Wire pre-positioned T-1?", "Funds-flow lock?", "Mercury-compliant on the deed of release?". Risks: (a) the wire is delayed in the inter-bank system — mitigation: pre-position funds T-1 in your client account, wire CHAPS by 09:00 to give a one-hour buffer; (b) the lender\'s release deed has unexpected conditions — mitigation: pre-agree the release deed wording on Friday; (c) the CFO\'s flight is irrelevant — closing happens when it happens, but informally manage the seller\'s expectation that closing will be done by 11:30am with buffer.',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'M10-02',
    module: '10',
    title: 'Split closing for an Italian Srl acquisition',
    setup: 'You represent the buyer of an Italian Srl acquired for €85m. Closing requires (a) execution of the share-transfer deed before an Italian notary in Milan, (b) wire transfer of the purchase price, (c) execution of resignation letters from the three current directors, (d) signing of a Transition Services Agreement under English law. The seller and the seller\'s counsel are based in London. The Italian notary insists on physical attendance.',
    prompt: 'How do you structure the closing? Where does the single-transaction doctrine actually do work, given that civil-law title passes at notarisation regardless of escrow?',
    modelAnswer: 'This is a "split closing." (1) Pre-Closing: the seller grants a notarised power of attorney to a representative (often the seller\'s Italian counsel) authorising execution of the share-transfer deed. The POA itself must be notarised either by an Italian notary or, if signed in London, by a UK notary public and apostilled under the Hague Apostille Convention 1961. The POA is delivered to Italian counsel in advance. (2) Closing day morning: in Milan, the buyer\'s representative and the seller\'s POA-holder attend before the Italian notary, who reads aloud the share-transfer deed (in Italian; an English translation is annexed for the parties\' comfort), both sign in the notary\'s presence, and the notary attests. The deed is then filed with the Registro delle Imprese — title passes on filing under Civil Code Art. 2470. (3) Closing day London leg: parallel virtual call. Wire transfer of €85m goes from buyer\'s solicitors\' client account to seller via SEPA Instant or TARGET2. The TSA is executed by exchange of e-signed signature pages (DocuSign or similar). Resignation letters are pre-signed in escrow by seller\'s counsel and released on receipt-of-funds confirmation — taking care to be Mercury-compliant given the deed of release language in the resignation letters. (4) <strong>Single-transaction clause does the bridging work.</strong> Critically, in civil-law systems title passes at notarisation regardless of escrow, so absent a single-transaction clause the buyer ends up with Italian title before the London leg has completed. The SPA-level fiction overrides the unitary moment created by notarisation: nothing in either jurisdiction is deemed to have happened until the totality has. Practical tip: the timing of the wire matters because the Italian notary cannot attest the share transfer until the buyer is funded — funds typically pre-position in client account T-1.',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'M10-03',
    module: '10',
    title: 'Conditional specific performance in a US PE LBO closing failure',
    setup: 'You represent the seller (a strategic) in a $1.2bn sale of a US speciality chemicals business to a private-equity sponsor. The merger agreement was negotiated in heated competitive auction conditions and is "tight" on the buyer\'s side: no financing condition; the buyer must use commercially reasonable efforts to obtain debt financing; specific performance against the buyer is available "if and only if" the buyer\'s debt financing is in fact available; reverse termination fee of $80m (≈6.7% of EV); marketing period of 20 consecutive business days after CP satisfaction. CMA and HSR clearances both came through last week. You are seven business days into the marketing period when the buyer\'s mezzanine lender pulls out, citing concerns about the target\'s most recent quarterly financials. The buyer claims the financing is no longer available; the senior banks confirm this by email.',
    prompt: 'What is the seller\'s position? What partner-grade questions does the seller\'s counsel need to ask in the next 24 hours?',
    modelAnswer: 'The architecture in play is the post-<em>Hexion v Huntsman</em> <strong>conditional / limited specific performance</strong> framework. Headline: the seller is, on the face of the agreement, limited to the $80m reverse termination fee — specific performance against the buyer is barred because financing is not available. But that is not the end of the analysis. <strong>Three partner-grade questions in the next 24 hours.</strong> (1) <em>Has the buyer used commercially reasonable / reasonable best efforts?</em> If the buyer breached its financing covenants — failed to negotiate replacement debt, gave up too easily, refused to pay an increased margin — then under the <strong>prevention doctrine</strong> (per <em>Snow Phipps v KCake</em>, Del. Ch. 30 Apr 2021) the buyer\'s breach is deemed to have caused the financing failure, and the buyer cannot invoke the financing condition. Specific performance becomes available again. Document-discovery analogy: ask immediately for the buyer\'s correspondence with all lenders. (2) <em>Did the marketing-period timing favour the buyer in a way that breached the cooperation obligations?</em> If the seller\'s cooperation was perfect, the buyer cannot claim the financials were inadequate. (3) <em>Is the reverse termination fee a true cap on damages or a floor?</em> The agreement says "if and only if" — that\'s a cap. But explicit wording matters: if the agreement excludes specific performance only "where the financing fails despite the buyer\'s reasonable best efforts", a finding of insufficient effort opens specific performance. Practical move: serve a reservation-of-rights letter, issue document preservation notice, and if facts support, file in Delaware Chancery for specific performance under the prevention-doctrine theory. <em>Channel Medsystems v Boston Scientific</em> (2019) and <em>Snow Phipps v KCake</em> (2021) are the authority. Note: <em>Akorn v Fresenius</em> (2018) and <em>AB Stable v MAPS</em> (2020) are NOT applicable — they are buyer-walk-away precedents (M09), not buyer-compelled-to-close.',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  }
];

/* Module 11 — Purchase Price Mechanisms: per-module scenarios fragment.
 *
 * v3 stress-test fixes (May 2026): replaces D-11-* scenarios from scenarios-D.js (build script
 * drops letter scenarios for any module that has an override).
 *
 * Refresh focus: refresh scenarios with v3 frames (DD->peg link; MAE-vs-Leakage gap; Leakage
 * no-friction asymmetry; W&I retention parallel; Lazard Technology Partners citation; Porton
 * Capital reframed). Add a third scenario covering the no-recourse / synthetic-warranty exit
 * pattern and the mechanism-switch domino chain — both flagged as gaps in stress-test-11.md.
 */
window.__SCEN_M11 = [
  {
    id: 'M11-01',
    module: '11',
    title: 'Bidding into a Locked Box auction for a UK manufacturer',
    setup: 'You represent a UK private-equity sponsor bidding for a profitable mid-market manufacturing business in a competitive auction. The seller (a family-owned business) has run a tight process; the seller\'s M&A adviser has provided full audited accounts to 31 December 2025 plus a sell-side QofE (Big Four) identifying £2.4m of one-off "transaction" and "owner-comp normalisation" add-backs. Bids are due in mid-March 2026 with expected closing in September 2026 (subject to UK CMA clearance). The seller insists on a Locked Box mechanism with Locked Box Date 31 December 2025 and a Permitted Leakage list comprising ordinary-course salary, ordinary-course intercompany trading at arm\'s length, transaction fees up to £2m, and a single £8m dividend approved by the seller\'s board on 28 February 2026.',
    prompt: 'How do you structure your bid? What are the negotiation pressure points, and what protects you against operational deterioration during the 8-month gap?',
    modelAnswer: 'This is textbook European mid-market PE Locked Box practice. Walk the components in order. (1) Accept the Locked Box mechanism — refusing it on a competitive auction signals weakness and may eliminate you from the process. (2) QofE first: stress-test the sell-side QofE\'s £2.4m of add-backs in your buy-side QofE. Each add-back becomes either a contractual definition (Schedule X) or a peg adjustment. Partners say "where\'s the peg landing?" — your buy-side accountants need a defensible answer before the bid lands. (3) Pricing: factor a "ticking fee" into your bid — for an 8-month gap (Jan to Sep) at, say, 5% per annum on £400m enterprise value, that is roughly £13m; you can either bid lower upfront or accept the ticking fee in the SPA. (4) Permitted Leakage: scrutinise each item. Ordinary-course salary is fine but you want a defined ceiling (e.g., capped at run-rate as at the Locked Box Date) — bonuses are NOT salary unless expressly carved; intercompany trading at arm\'s length is fine but you want documentary support; transaction-fee carve-out at £2m is generous but standard; the £8m dividend is critical — verify it was indeed approved by 28 February (board minutes), is consistent with distributable reserves, and ask for a confirmatory legal opinion. (5) No Leakage Covenant: insist on the separate-and-independent-obligation drafting (source-deck Clause 5.1 wording); 18-month claims period; pound-for-pound recovery without basket or de minimis. Note the no-friction asymmetry — Leakage recovers without the friction warranty claims carry; sellers will try to recharacterise items as warranty claims to engage basket / cap, you will resist. (6) The CRITICAL operational protection: Locked Box catches value extraction, NOT value destruction. Lost customers, declining margins, reputational damage in the 8-month gap give you no Leakage claim. Tighten the Conduct of Business / Ordinary Course covenant (Module 09) because you have lost the post-Closing true-up that Closing Accounts buyers rely on. Insist on an MAE condition with a deleted pandemic carve-out and key-customer-loss as a stipulated MAE event. Partners frame this gap as: "Locked Box is a wall against the seller\'s pocket, not a wall against the world." (7) Diligence: shift cash-debt-WC diligence pre-Signing — your accountants should reconstruct the Equity Bridge from the Locked Box accounts and stress-test it against pre-Locked-Box trading (Q4 2025). (8) Concession map: in a competitive auction you will not win every point. The Permitted Leakage cap, the No Leakage drafting, and the Conduct of Business tightening are the critical "must wins"; the ticking fee rate and the audit-expert appointment fallback are the trade-aways.',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'M11-02',
    module: '11',
    title: 'Earn-out dispute on a £120m UK SaaS deal',
    setup: 'Two years post-Closing of a £120m UK SaaS acquisition. The SPA included a 2-year EBITDA-based earn-out: target EBITDA of £15m for FY2027, paying £1.5m for every £1m of EBITDA above target, capped at £30m. The SPA defines EBITDA as "the consolidated earnings of the Target Business before interest, tax, depreciation, and amortisation, calculated in accordance with the accounting policies set out in Schedule 8, on a consistent basis with the management accounts for the 12 months ended 31 December 2025." Schedule 8 lists 14 specific accounting policies. After Closing, the buyer integrated the target into its UK division; both buyer and target now share customer accounts, sales staff, and back-office. The SPA contains no separate-business-unit covenant and no anti-frustration covenant. The buyer reports earn-out EBITDA of £14m (just below target — no earn-out due). The seller\'s analysis suggests the "true" earn-out EBITDA should be £22m if appropriate transfer pricing and cost allocations were applied.',
    prompt: 'What is the seller\'s position? What dispute resolution path applies? What can the seller realistically expect, and what was the drafting failure?',
    modelAnswer: 'Two avenues. (1) Calculation dispute: the SPA almost certainly contains a Closing-Accounts-style mechanism for earn-out determination (Adjustment Notice, Objection Notice, Audit Expert determination). Under English law (Jones v. Sherwood, Barclays v Nylon Capital) the Audit Expert acts as expert (not arbitrator) — final and binding subject to manifest error / departure from instructions. Confirm the SPA\'s arbitration clause expressly carves out the expert determination (otherwise jurisdictional confusion — Module 16). The seller serves an Objection Notice within the SPA\'s window, attaching detailed analysis of cost allocations, transfer pricing, and customer reallocations, and proposing the £22m figure. The Audit Expert (Big Four) will determine items in dispute, applying Schedule 8\'s policies. The seller\'s strongest arguments: (i) Schedule 8 fixes the policies — the buyer cannot apply different policies retrospectively; (ii) revenue and costs allocated to the buyer\'s other UK division but properly attributable to the target should be re-allocated for earn-out purposes (essentially a transfer-pricing argument). The buyer will resist: operational integration was permitted (no separate-business-unit covenant) and the policies were applied as written. Consider negotiating a baseball-arbitration format (each side submits a single number) — increasingly common in US price disputes; deters aggressive Adjustment Notices but requires express SPA drafting (an expert acting in default of clear terms of reference may decline to operate this way). (2) Implied-covenant claim: under English law (Porton Capital v. 3M, EWHC 2895 Comm 2011, Eder J), express obligations bite — the seller in Porton substantially won because 3M had failed an express "diligently seek regulatory approval" obligation. But where the SPA does NOT impose an express obligation (e.g., "operate as a separate business unit", "no customer reallocation", "preserve existing sales structures"), the implied covenant of good faith will not be expanded to fill the gap. Compare Delaware: Lazard Technology Partners v. QinetiQ (Strine C.J., 2015) and Winshall v. Viacom hold the same — the implied covenant cannot expand express drafting. The seller cannot rescue weak drafting. Realistic outcome: an Audit Expert determination probably increases earn-out EBITDA modestly (perhaps to £17–19m, generating £3–6m of earn-out under the £1.5m-per-£1m multiplier) but does not deliver the seller\'s full £22m claim. The seller\'s commercial leverage is also limited because the £30m earn-out cap means upside is bounded. SRS Acquiom 2024 puts realised earn-outs at ~21¢ on the dollar with 28% formally contested — this dispute fits the pattern. The drafting failure: no separate-business-unit covenant, no anti-frustration covenant, no frozen-policies annex covering the integration scenario, no choice between linear and cliff with adequate buyer-side discipline. Lesson: negotiate explicit express obligations on the buyer; do not rely on the implied covenant to fill drafting gaps.',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'M11-03',
    module: '11',
    title: 'Mechanism switch and synthetic-warranty exit on a €600m European PE secondary',
    setup: 'You advise the buyer (a US PE sponsor) on the secondary acquisition of a Continental European software business from a European PE seller for an enterprise value of €600m. The seller is running a tight competitive process and insists on a synthetic / no-recourse SPA: cap = €1, basket = €1, no escrow, the buyer\'s sole recourse for warranty breach is a W&I (RWI) policy. The seller proposes a Locked Box mechanism with Locked Box Date being the 31 December 2025 audited balance sheet; expected closing is November 2026 (11 months after Locked Box Date) with EU foreign-subsidies and FDI clearances. Mid-negotiation the seller pivots to demanding Closing Accounts because their tax advisers have raised concerns about the Locked Box characterisation in one jurisdiction. The W&I underwriter has quoted retention at 0.5% of EV at signing, dropping to 0.25% after 12 months ("stepped retention").',
    prompt: '(a) How does the synthetic / no-recourse structure interact with W&I retention and the Module 11 first-dollar structure? (b) When the seller pivots to Closing Accounts, what is the domino chain of SPA changes? (c) How do you protect against the 11-month operational gap on either mechanism?',
    modelAnswer: '(a) THE FIRST-DOLLAR STRUCTURE. In a synthetic / no-recourse deal the SPA cap is symbolic (€1); the basket is symbolic (€1); there is no escrow. The buyer\'s entire warranty-recovery economics live in the W&I tower. Per Marsh European M&A Insurance Trends 2024, the retention sits at ~0.5% EV at signing — for a €600m deal that is €3m of first-dollar wear. With the stepped reduction to 0.25% after 12 months, the retention drops to €1.5m once the policy seasons. The retention is FUNCTIONALLY IDENTICAL to a working-capital deductible (the source-deck\'s €100,000 deductible was the same conceptual structure) — buyer wears the first dollar; the policy / seller pays above. In this synthetic structure, the SPA basket has been collapsed away, so the policy retention carries the entire first-dollar structure alone. Partner question: "where\'s our first-dollar structure landing — deductible at the WC bridge, basket at the indemnification, or retention at the policy?" — here only the policy retention. R&Ws still need to be tight, accurate and well-disclosed but the audience is the underwriter, not the seller (different drafting calibration); fundamental warranties (title, capacity, authority) typically remain at full uncapped seller liability; fraud carve-outs remain at the SPA level because the policy excludes fraud (~63% of US private deals are now W&I-backed per the 2025 ABA Private Target M&A Deal Points Study; this is the dominant structure in European mid-market PE exits). (b) DOMINO CHAIN. The seller\'s pivot from Locked Box to Closing Accounts cascades through the SPA. ADD: Estimated Closing Statement deliverable; Closing Accounts dispute clause (Adjustment Notice / Objection Notice / Audit Expert appointment mechanic with explicit baseball-vs-free-form choice); Working Capital Escrow (notwithstanding the no-recourse philosophy, the WC escrow is mechanically necessary); a clear definition of the working-capital peg derived from the QofE. REMOVE OR NARROW: No Leakage covenant (Locked Box mechanic — irrelevant on Closing Accounts); Permitted Leakage list (irrelevant); ticking fee (no Locked Box Date to compensate from). TIGHTEN: Ordinary Course / Conduct of Business covenant — wait, on Closing Accounts the buyer arguably has LESS need to tighten (because the post-Closing true-up catches deterioration) — so depending on negotiation the covenant may be loosened modestly. RESTRUCTURE: Funds Flow Memo (Module 10) splits in two — Estimate at Closing, true-up wired post-Closing. CARVE OUT EXPERT DETERMINATION: ensure the SPA arbitration clause expressly carves out the price-mechanism expert determination (Module 16) — sample wording: "Any dispute arising under or in connection with this Agreement shall be referred to arbitration … except any matter expressly referred to the Audit Expert under [Closing Accounts clause], which shall be determined by expert determination as provided therein." Reciting this seven-line domino chain unprompted is the partner-grade move that signals system command. (c) THE 11-MONTH OPERATIONAL GAP. On EITHER mechanism, the No Leakage covenant catches value EXTRACTION but not value DESTRUCTION. On Locked Box this is the central risk; on Closing Accounts the working-capital and EBITDA-style true-up partially absorb deterioration but operational damage (lost customers, declining margins) still cuts through. Protection in BOTH cases: (i) tight Conduct of Business / Ordinary Course covenant (Module 09) — affirmative undertaking to operate consistent with past practice plus a long negative-consent list; (ii) MAE / MAC condition with a key-customer-loss and key-license-loss carve-out (no broad pandemic-style carve-outs); (iii) bring-down condition at Closing requiring the warranties to remain true in all material respects. The W&I policy will typically have a "no-claims declaration" requirement at signing AND closing — the buyer must confirm at both moments that no breach is known. The 11-month gap also means the underwriter will require a "bring-down" of disclosures at Closing — coordinate timing with the FDI / foreign-subsidies clearance schedule.',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  }
];

/* Module 12 (Representations & Warranties) scenarios — v3 fix-agent.
   Replaces all E-12-* scenarios by module-ID match in the build step.

   v3 changes (May 2026):
   - Replaced Daniel v Drew authority with Infiniteland (correctly framed as turning on
     the contractual construction of "Disclosed").
   - Reframed sandbagging discussion to Arwood-as-resolved (not "reignited").
   - New synthetic / no-recourse W&I scenario covering the full system-level domino effect. */
window.__SCEN_M12 = [
  {
    id: 'M12-01', module: '12',
    title: 'BlueRiver Capital — disclosure standard for a 30,000-document data room (UK)',
    setup: '<p>BlueRiver Capital, a UK-based private equity fund, is buying TargetCo (a software business) from its founder for &pound;50 million. The founder will give the warranties personally, and his lawyer is trying to limit exposure. The data room contains 30,000 documents including a folder labelled "miscellaneous" containing an unflagged email chain about a possible patent infringement claim from a competitor. The buyer\'s lawyer is drafting the disclosure standard and the sandbagging stance; the deal is governed by English law.</p>',
    prompt: 'What disclosure standard should the buyer push for, and what is the legal authority? How should the buyer treat the data room contents? What sandbagging stance should the SPA take?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Disclosure standard.</strong> The buyer should push for a "fairly disclosed" standard requiring disclosure to be in such manner and with sufficient particularity to enable a reasonable purchaser to identify the nature and scope of the matter disclosed. <strong>Authority.</strong> <em>Infiniteland Ltd v Artisan Contracting Ltd</em> [2005] EWCA Civ 758 (Chadwick LJ) — properly read, the case turns on the construction of the SPA\'s particular wording: the contractually-agreed disclosure standard governs and that standard generally requires "sufficient detail to enable the purchaser to identify the nature and scope of the matter disclosed". <em>New Hearts Ltd v Cosmopolitan Investments Ltd</em> 1997 SLT 341 (Lord Penrose): "fair, clear and accurate"; no "trickery"; no needle-in-a-haystack.</p><p><strong>Data room treatment.</strong> The buyer should explicitly carve the data room out of "deemed disclosure" — only matters specifically cross-referenced in the Disclosure Letter to a particular warranty should qualify that warranty. This addresses the classic "data dump" pitfall and ensures that the unflagged patent infringement email cannot defeat an IP infringement warranty claim. The Disclosure Letter delivery deadline should be 5 business days before signing so the buyer has time to digest disclosures.</p><p><strong>Sandbagging stance.</strong> Buyer should insist on an explicit pro-sandbagging clause: "The right of any Buyer Indemnitee to indemnification ... shall not be affected by any investigation conducted or knowledge acquired (or capable of being acquired) at any time, whether before or after the date of this Agreement or the Closing Date ...". This neutralises any <em>Eurocopy plc v Teesdale</em> [1992] BCLC 1067 argument (interlocutory dicta — the case settled before final judgment, but has shaped UK practice nonetheless) that the buyer\'s actual knowledge from data-room sources defeats the claim.</p><p><strong>Backwards link.</strong> Every issue surfaced in DD becomes either (a) a specific warranty, (b) a disclosure, (c) a specific indemnity, or (d) a price reduction; the patent infringement email should drive a specific IP indemnity carve-out from the cap if the buyer is unwilling to accept the risk. <strong>Forwards link.</strong> If the deal carries W&I, the underwriter will read the disclosure standard and the data-room treatment as part of underwriting; a buyer-friendly disclosure regime supports negotiating a knowledge scrape at modest premium uplift (typically 10–15%).</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'M12-02', module: '12',
    title: 'HoldCo Inc — narrow knowledge qualifier defeats compliance-with-laws claim (Delaware)',
    setup: '<p>HoldCo Inc. (Delaware) is selling Subsidiary to a strategic buyer for $200 million. The deal is governed by Delaware law and there is no W&I policy. The seller\'s lawyer drafts a warranty: <em>"To the actual knowledge of John Smith (CEO), without any duty of inquiry, Subsidiary is in compliance with all applicable laws."</em> During due diligence the buyer discovered that the company\'s HR director (Ms. Jones) was aware of an ongoing wage-and-hour investigation but had not informed Mr. Smith. The SPA is silent on sandbagging. Three months after Closing, the wage-and-hour investigation results in a $5 million payment.</p>',
    prompt: 'Can the buyer claim for breach of the compliance-with-laws warranty? What drafting changes should the buyer have insisted on? How does the Delaware sandbagging default interact?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>As drafted, the warranty is qualified to John Smith\'s subjective actual knowledge with no duty of inquiry.</strong> If Smith genuinely did not know, the warranty is not breached even though the company was, objectively, not in compliance. The buyer\'s claim therefore fails at the breach stage — there is no breach to claim against, regardless of sandbagging defaults.</p><p><strong>Drafting changes the buyer should have insisted on:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Wider Knowledge Group</strong> — include the HR Director and the COO; informal market norm is 4–6 senior executives (CEO, CFO, GC, COO, head of HR), though the precise composition is deal-specific (informal observation, not a fixed standard).</li><li><strong>Constructive knowledge with duty of inquiry</strong> — "actual or constructive knowledge after reasonable inquiry of Subsidiary\'s senior management and the records they would normally consult".</li><li><strong>Best of all</strong>: remove the knowledge qualifier altogether for compliance-with-laws warranties (they are objectively verifiable; the seller bears the risk of being wrong about its own compliance state).</li><li><strong>Materiality scrape</strong> — even if "compliance" were qualified by "in all material respects", the scrape would mean the buyer does not have to prove materiality before establishing breach. Per ABA 2025, the double materiality scrape now appears in 82% of US private deals.</li></ul><p><strong>Sandbagging interaction.</strong> Delaware default is <strong>pro-sandbagging</strong> per <em>Cobalt Operating, LLC v. James Crystal Enterprises, LLC</em>, 2007 WL 2142926 (Del. Ch. 20 July 2007) (Strine V.C.); resolved by <em>Arwood v. AW Site Services, LLC</em>, C.A. No. 2019-0904-SG (Del. Ch. 9 March 2022) (Slights V.C.: "the sandbagging defense is inconsistent with our profoundly contractarian predisposition"). On this fact pattern, however, the Delaware default does not help — sandbagging speaks to whether the buyer\'s pre-signing knowledge defeats a claim that <em>is otherwise valid</em>. Here, no breach exists in the first place because the warranty is so narrowly qualified. The buyer wins the sandbagging argument but loses the war.</p><p><strong>System-level lesson.</strong> The cap, basket and sandbagging clause are downstream of the warranty\'s breach standard. Negotiating a generous indemnification regime is wasted effort if the warranty itself is drafted so narrowly that no breach can ever be established. The R&W system (M12+M13+M14+disclosure) must be read as a single negotiation object — the warranty content drives whether any of the rest of the regime ever fires.</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'M12-03', module: '12',
    title: 'European PE exit — synthetic / no-recourse W&I package (the system-level domino effect)',
    setup: '<p>Onslow Capital, a European mid-market PE fund, is selling PortfolioCo (a German industrial software business) for &euro;500 million enterprise value to AscendBuyer LLC, a US strategic buyer. Onslow is winding down its 2018 fund and wants a clean exit; AscendBuyer wants real recourse for warranty breach. The proposed structure is a <strong>synthetic / no-recourse W&amp;I package</strong>: SPA cap = &euro;1; SPA basket = &euro;1; W&amp;I policy at 10% of EV (&euro;50m limit); 0.5% retention stepping to 0.25% after 12 months; 1% RoL premium &approx; &euro;500k. AscendBuyer\'s deal lawyer is being asked by AscendBuyer\'s GC: "<em>if we sign this structure, what else has to move and what does AscendBuyer actually own as protection?</em>"</p>',
    prompt: 'Walk through the system-level domino effect of the synthetic / no-recourse structure. What moves at the SPA level, what moves at the policy level, and what is the buyer\'s actual recourse for (a) general warranty breach, (b) fundamental warranty breach, (c) fraud? Where does the W&I underwriter\'s comfort dictate language?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>SPA-level changes.</strong> Cap drops from typical 10–25% of EV to <strong>&euro;1</strong>; basket drops from typical 0.5–1% of purchase price to <strong>&euro;1</strong>; survival becomes symbolic (typically 18 months for general warranties at SPA level); the indemnification clause is a procedural shell. The R&amp;W package is drafted not as real contractual liability but to satisfy the <strong>W&amp;I underwriter</strong> — a different audience from a future claims judge. <em>"No recourse"</em> wording: <em>"Notwithstanding any other provision of this Agreement, the Buyer\'s sole recourse for any breach of the Seller Warranties shall be against the W&amp;I Policy ... except (a) in respect of fraud or wilful concealment by the Seller, or (b) for breach of the Fundamental Warranties up to the Fundamental Cap."</em></p><p><strong>Policy-level changes.</strong> The policy carries the real survival: typically 24–36 months for general warranties; 6–7 years for fundamentals and tax (aligned to the relevant statute of limitations + a buffer). <strong>Materiality scrape</strong> drafted into both SPA and policy in coordinated form (the policy reads through the SPA, so the scrape needs to be effective in both). <strong>Knowledge scrape</strong> negotiated as a typical 10–15% premium uplift; without it, the policy responds only where the seller\'s knowledge defence applies — useless cover.</p><p><strong>Buyer\'s actual recourse.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>(a) General warranty breach</strong>: claim goes to the W&amp;I policy, subject to the retention (&euro;2.5m at signing dropping to &euro;1.25m after 12 months) and the &euro;50m policy limit. Above &euro;50m, no recovery (negotiate excess layers if needed).</li><li><strong>(b) Fundamental warranty breach (title to shares, capacity, authority)</strong>: typically remains at <strong>full uncapped seller liability</strong> at the SPA level — the W&amp;I underwriter will not write adequate cover for fundamentals because the seller is in the best position to know them. AscendBuyer keeps a real claim against Onslow for any fundamental breach.</li><li><strong>(c) Fraud or wilful concealment</strong>: irreducible carve-out remains at the SPA level. The policy <em>excludes</em> fraud (a basic insurance principle), so AscendBuyer keeps a real claim against Onslow for fraud regardless of the otherwise-symbolic &euro;1 cap. UK: <em>Lazarus Estates</em>, <em>HIH Casualty</em>; US: <em>ABRY Partners</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Where the underwriter\'s comfort dictates language.</strong> Disclosure standard (specificity required); knowledge group composition (broader is more conservative for the underwriter); materiality qualifiers (the underwriter may accept higher per-warranty thresholds); identified DD red flags (often subject to specific exclusions or sub-limits in the policy); jurisdiction-specific risks (German labour law, environmental, regulatory). The 2–3-hour <strong>underwriting call</strong> is where the underwriter walks through the SPA, Disclosure Letter and DD reports with insured\'s counsel and refines coverage; coverage is settled by negotiating exclusions down, retention down, and scrapes in.</p><p><strong>Backwards link to DD (M04).</strong> Every issue surfaced in DD that the underwriter is asked to insure must have either (i) been satisfactorily disclosed in the Disclosure Letter and resolved, or (ii) been carved out of the policy with a specific exclusion. The DD-disclosure-policy chain is a single workflow.</p><p><strong>Per ABA 2025</strong>: this synthetic / no-recourse pattern is now mainstream — ~63% of US private deals use W&amp;I, and 41% of deals (up from ~30% in 2023) have R&amp;Ws that do not survive Closing at all. The structure has reshaped how R&amp;W packages are drafted.</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  }
];

/* Scenarios fragment for Module 13 — The Indemnification Regime.
   Per-module override merged into window.SCENARIOS by build step. Replaces all
   E-13-* scenarios from scenarios-E.js. */
window.__SCEN_M13 = [
  {
    id: 'M13-01', module: '13',
    title: 'Steel Corp — HMRC tax assessment, the "tax tail" and the W&I handoff',
    setup: '<p>Steel Corp (UK seller) sold its Subsidiary to Acquirer Inc. for &pound;100 million 14 months ago. The deal was W&amp;I-backed: the SPA cap is &pound;1 (synthetic / no-recourse) for general business warranties; fundamental warranties are uncapped; tax is covered under a separate <em>Tax Deed</em>. The W&amp;I policy provides up to &pound;15 million of cover (15% of EV) with stepped retention 0.5% &rarr; 0.25% after 12 months. Acquirer Inc. has just received a UK HMRC discovery assessment for &pound;4 million in respect of pre-Closing trading profits &mdash; the Subsidiary\'s prior accountant had improperly claimed certain deductions. The matter was not disclosed in the Disclosure Letter or the data room.</p>',
    prompt: 'What can Acquirer Inc. recover, against whom, and what procedural steps must it follow? Walk through the system from the SPA, through the Tax Deed, through the W&I policy, and identify the partner-meeting "tax tail" issue.',
    modelAnswer: '<p>This is a tax matter. The partner shorthand is the <strong>tax tail</strong>: tax claims survive longer than ordinary warranty claims (UK: TMA 1970 ss.34/36/36A as amended by Finance Act 2008 &mdash; up to 20 years for deliberate misstatement, 6 for careless, 4 ordinary), and tax matters typically run under a separate Tax Deed with its own procedure.</p><ol><li><strong>SPA cap &mdash; not the route.</strong> The general SPA cap is &pound;1 (synthetic). The buyer cannot recover the &pound;4m against Steel Corp under the general indemnification regime.</li><li><strong>Tax Deed &mdash; the primary route.</strong> The Tax Deed is a stand-alone pound-for-pound indemnity for pre-Closing tax. Notify Steel Corp under the Tax Deed\'s notice procedure (typically 30&ndash;60 Business Days from becoming aware), with reasonable detail of the basis and quantum. The Tax Deed survives the tax statute + 30&ndash;60 days, well beyond 14 months.</li><li><strong>Third-party-claims procedure (HMRC).</strong> HMRC is a third party; under the Tax Deed (and the SPA\'s third-party-claim mechanic), Steel Corp gets the opportunity to participate in the defence with its own counsel appointed by the Subsidiary. Settlement consent (not unreasonably withheld) with deemed approval if no objection within 7 Business Days.</li><li><strong>W&amp;I tower &mdash; the parallel route.</strong> The tax <em>warranty</em> in the SPA is covered by W&amp;I policy. The <em>tax deed</em>, in UK practice, may or may not be inside the W&amp;I tower &mdash; this is the partner-meeting question. <em>"Is the tax deed inside the tower?"</em> If yes, claim under both routes capped at one full loss (anti-double-recovery). If no, the tax deed is outside the symbolic SPA cap and remains direct seller exposure on tax.</li><li><strong>Anti-double-recovery / deductions.</strong> The Loss is reduced by (i) any tax-benefit actually realised (if the &pound;4m payment creates a deductible expense, the after-tax cost is lower); (ii) any reserve in the Closing Financial Statements for the same matter; (iii) any third-party recoveries; (iv) any closing-accounts true-up that has already taken the matter into account.</li><li><strong>Claims tail freeze.</strong> Once timely notified within the Tax Deed\'s notice period, Steel Corp remains liable until quantum is finally ascertained &mdash; even if the HMRC dispute (audit &rarr; appeal &rarr; tribunal) takes years. Without the freeze the buyer would lose the claim before HMRC concludes.</li></ol><p><strong>Partner-meeting takeaway:</strong> the tax tail dominates the indemnification negotiation on every UK W&amp;I-backed deal; the test question is always "is the tax deed inside the tower?", and the answer drives the seller\'s long-run residual exposure.</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'M13-02', module: '13',
    title: 'No-multiplier vs. recurring EBITDA misstatement &mdash; financial-statements carve-out',
    setup: '<p>A buyer is negotiating an SPA. The Target was acquired at a 10x EBITDA multiple. The seller insists on a "no multiplier" provision in the Loss definition: <em>"For the avoidance of doubt, no Loss shall be calculated by applying a multiplier to any reduction in earnings, profits or cash flow, even if the Buyer purchased the Target on a multiple basis."</em> Three months post-Closing, the buyer discovers an undisclosed customer relationship was wrongly characterised as recurring; the EBITDA was overstated by &euro;500,000 per year (recurring impact, not one-off).</p>',
    prompt: 'What is the buyer\'s argument against the no-multiplier clause? What is the seller\'s argument? What is the typical compromise — and what other lever does the buyer pull in exchange for accepting the clause?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Buyer\'s argument.</strong> The Target was bought on a 10x multiple; the recurring &euro;500,000 EBITDA overstatement reduces enterprise value by &euro;5,000,000 (10 &times; &euro;500,000), so the Loss is the diminution in value at &euro;5,000,000 per <em>Lion Nathan v C-C Bottlers</em> [1996] 1 WLR 1438 (PC) &mdash; the leading authority that diminution in value is the appropriate measure for SPA warranty breach. The buyer paid for &euro;5,000,000 of value it did not get; the cash-only measure undercompensates and gives the seller a windfall by allowing it to retain the inflated price.</p><p><strong>Seller\'s argument.</strong> Contractual freedom: the parties have agreed the measure of damages and the multiplier is a speculative calculation unconnected to actual cash impact. Multipliers reflect future expectations that are inherently uncertain and excessively generous to the buyer; the buyer cannot show it has actually lost &euro;5m in cash. The seller frames the clause as a liquidated allocation of valuation risk.</p><p><strong>Typical compromise.</strong> No-multiplier clause applies generally, <em>but</em> with an exception preserving the multiplier measure for breach of <em>financial-statements</em> warranties (where the multiplier is the proper measure since the buyer paid a multiple of warranted earnings). Alternative: the parties pre-agree a "deemed damages" clause for misstated financial statements at [N]&times; the misstatement.</p><p><strong>Other levers the buyer pulls in exchange.</strong> If the buyer accepts the no-multiplier clause without the financial-statements exception, the partner-level negotiation moves to compensating levers:</p><ul><li><strong>Materiality scrape (double scrape)</strong> &mdash; ABA 2025 prevalence ~82% in US private deals; strips the materiality qualifier so the buyer doesn\'t have to prove each item is "material."</li><li><strong>Knowledge scrape</strong> &mdash; strips the seller\'s knowledge qualifier from the financial-statements warranty.</li><li><strong>Larger cap on financial-statements warranty</strong> &mdash; specifically uncapped or capped at 100% rather than the general cap.</li><li><strong>Specific indemnity</strong> &mdash; for any DD-identified accounting issue, ring-fenced outside the warranty regime.</li></ul><p><strong>Partner-meeting framing.</strong> The buyer frames the conversation as: <em>"we\'ll accept no-multiplier if you give us the financial-statements exception and a double scrape; otherwise the cap on financial-statements goes to 100%."</em> The clause is rarely conceded in isolation; it is traded against three or four compensating levers.</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'M13-03', module: '13',
    title: 'Six-claim portfolio at &euro;200m EV: applying basket, cap, carve-outs, anti-double-recovery and the W&I tower',
    setup: '<p>Deal: &euro;200 million purchase price; W&amp;I-backed. <strong>SPA-level mechanics:</strong> de minimis = &euro;100,000 (0.05%); basket = &euro;1,000,000 (0.5%) <strong>tipping</strong>; SPA cap = &euro;2,000,000 (1%) for general business warranties; fundamental warranties uncapped at SPA level; environmental and tax carved out from cap; specific indemnity for known regulatory issue at &euro;5,000,000 ring-fenced, outside cap and basket. <strong>W&amp;I tower:</strong> retention &euro;1,000,000 (0.5%) stepping to &euro;500,000 (0.25%) after 12 months; primary layer of &euro;20m (10% of EV); two excess layers of &euro;10m each, total tower &euro;40m. <strong>Closing accounts true-up</strong>: &euro;200,000 of working-capital under-accrual already captured as a price reduction.</p><p>Six post-Closing claims arise at month 13:</p><ul><li>Claim A: tax assessment, &euro;3,000,000 (warranty + Tax Deed; under Tax Deed and tax warranty).</li><li>Claim B: undisclosed customer dispute settlement, &euro;450,000 (general business warranty).</li><li>Claim C: minor IP licence renegotiation cost, &euro;75,000 (general business warranty).</li><li>Claim D: environmental clean-up cost, &euro;15,000,000 (environmental warranty &mdash; carved out from cap).</li><li>Claim E: working-capital under-accrual already captured in closing accounts true-up, &euro;200,000 (financial-statements warranty &mdash; double-recovery risk).</li><li>Claim F: fundamental &mdash; title defect, &euro;30,000,000 (uncapped at SPA level).</li><li>Claim G: known regulatory issue (subject to specific indemnity), &euro;5,000,000.</li></ul>',
    prompt: 'Calculate the buyer\'s total recovery and explain how each device (de minimis, tipping basket, cap, carve-outs, anti-double-recovery, W&I tower, specific indemnity) operates. What is the partner-meeting takeaway about how SPA, escrow, W&I and closing-accounts coordinate?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Step 1 &mdash; Apply de minimis (&euro;100,000):</strong> Claim C (&euro;75,000) is below de minimis and is not even counted. The other six survive.</p><p><strong>Step 2 &mdash; Anti-double-recovery on Claim E (&euro;200,000):</strong> the working-capital true-up under the closing-accounts mechanism (M11) has already reduced the price by &euro;200,000. The anti-double-recovery clause excludes this amount from "Loss" under the indemnity. <strong>Recovery on E: &euro;0.</strong> Partner shorthand: <em>"already in the true-up &mdash; not stand-alone."</em></p><p><strong>Step 3 &mdash; Apply tipping basket (&euro;1,000,000):</strong> aggregate of the qualifying general-warranty claims is A + B + D + Claim E = &euro;3,000,000 + &euro;450,000 + &euro;15,000,000 + 0 = &euro;18,450,000. Exceeds the &euro;1m basket &rarr; the basket "tips" and the entire amount is recoverable, subject to subsequent caps and carve-outs. (Claim F is fundamental, outside the basket entirely; Claim G is specific indemnity, outside the basket entirely.)</p><p><strong>Step 4 &mdash; Apply general cap (&euro;2,000,000) respecting carve-outs:</strong> environmental (D) and tax (A) are carved out from the &euro;2m cap. The general cap applies only to Claim B (&euro;450,000) &mdash; well within. Recovery on B: &euro;450,000.</p><p><strong>Step 5 &mdash; Tax (Claim A, &euro;3,000,000):</strong> claimed under the Tax Deed (UK practice; partner question: <em>"is the tax deed inside the tower?"</em>); typically covered by W&amp;I policy. Buyer recovers &euro;3,000,000. Reduced by any tax benefit actually realised.</p><p><strong>Step 6 &mdash; Environmental (Claim D, &euro;15,000,000):</strong> carved out from the SPA cap; covered under the W&amp;I tower if not specifically excluded. Buyer absorbs the retention (&euro;500,000 after 12 months); first &euro;14,500,000 falls within the &euro;20m primary layer. Recovery on D: &euro;14,500,000 from W&amp;I + &euro;500,000 absorbed as retention.</p><p><strong>Step 7 &mdash; Fundamental (Claim F, &euro;30,000,000):</strong> uncapped at SPA level; W&amp;I tower typically does not respond to fundamental warranties (they remain seller exposure). Recovery on F: &euro;30,000,000 from Steel Corp / sellers, subject to solvency / escrow.</p><p><strong>Step 8 &mdash; Specific indemnity (Claim G, &euro;5,000,000):</strong> ring-fenced specific indemnity outside the cap and basket, with its own &euro;5m sub-limit. Recovery on G: &euro;5,000,000.</p><p><strong>Total recovery &mdash; tabulated:</strong></p><ul><li>A (tax): &euro;3,000,000 (Tax Deed + W&amp;I)</li><li>B (customer dispute): &euro;450,000 (SPA general cap)</li><li>C (IP licence): &euro;0 (de minimis)</li><li>D (environmental): &euro;14,500,000 (W&amp;I primary layer above &euro;500k retention)</li><li>E (working capital): &euro;0 (anti-double-recovery)</li><li>F (title): &euro;30,000,000 (sellers, uncapped)</li><li>G (specific indemnity): &euro;5,000,000 (ring-fenced)</li></ul><p><strong>Total: &euro;52,950,000</strong> against a &euro;200m purchase price (with the &euro;500,000 retention absorbed by buyer).</p><p><strong>Partner-meeting takeaway.</strong> The single deal moves money through five distinct mechanisms operating in parallel: (i) the SPA cap and basket for general business claims; (ii) the carve-outs (tax, environmental, fundamental) operating on the cap; (iii) the closing-accounts true-up plus anti-double-recovery (Claim E neutralised); (iv) the W&amp;I tower (retention &rarr; primary &rarr; excess); (v) the ring-fenced specific indemnity for known matters from DD. <em>"If you change one of these &mdash; shorten survival, flip tipping to deductible, drop W&amp;I &mdash; what else has to move?"</em> is the canonical partner question. Cross-reference: M04 (DD identifies known matters &rarr; specific indemnity); M10 (escrow funds the seller exposure on F); M11 (closing-accounts true-up handles E); M14 (W&amp;I tower handles D); M16 (any of these claims becoming a dispute goes to expert determination on quantum or to arbitration on breach).</p>',
    type: 'numerical'
  }
];

/* Per-module scenarios fragment for Module 14 — Warranty & Indemnity Insurance.
 * Replaces letter-fragment items with module === '14' (build-data.sh override behaviour).
 * Refreshed for May 2026 market: 2025 hardening cycle, tax-deed treatment, fronted vs all-domestic,
 * seller-stapled auction mechanics, fundamental cap carve-out, policy dispute forum.
 */
window.__SCEN_M14 = [
  {
    id: 'M14-01', module: '14',
    title: 'TechCo — UK SaaS founder selling to PE, retaining as CEO (May 2026)',
    setup: '<p>TechCo, a mid-market UK SaaS business, is being sold by its founder for &pound;150m to PE buyer Apex Equity. The founder has owned the business for 12 years and personally drafted some of the customer contracts; the diligence team identified a few historic non-compliance issues with UK customer contracts that the founder fixed in the last two years. Apex wants to retain the founder as CEO post-Closing but does not want a sour working relationship if a warranty claim arises. The seller is otherwise a "clean" individual seller without a fund-style overhang. The deal is signing in mid-2026.</p>',
    prompt: 'How should the W&I-aligned SPA be structured for this May 2026 deal? What specific issues need careful drafting, given current market conditions?',
    modelAnswer: '<p>Textbook "departed-seller / continuing-CEO" situation perfectly suited to W&amp;I.</p><p><strong>Recommended structure (May 2026 market):</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Buy-side W&amp;I policy</strong> with policy limit of 10&ndash;15% of EV (&pound;15&ndash;22m). Retention 0.5% of EV (&pound;750k) stepping to 0.25% after 12 months (the 2024-trough "0.25% flat" or "tip-to-nil" structures are no longer the floor post-2025 hardening). UK primary RoL ~<strong>1.0&ndash;1.5%</strong> (post-2025 hardening; was &lt;1% during 2024 trough on competitive auctions). Premium ~&pound;180&ndash;330k.</li><li><strong>SPA caps:</strong> $1 nil-recourse for general business warranties; founder&rsquo;s liability for <strong>fundamental warranties</strong> (title to shares, capacity, no encumbrances) capped at 100% of price (the <em>fundamental cap</em>); founder&rsquo;s liability for fraud uncapped (irreducible per <em>HIH Casualty v Chase Manhattan</em>; <em>Lazarus Estates v Beasley</em>).</li><li><strong>Tax deed.</strong> UK practice: separate tax deed alongside the SPA. Negotiation question: <em>"is the tax deed inside the W&amp;I or are we seller-on-the-hook for it?"</em> Apex should push for the tax deed to be inside the W&amp;I (with appropriate sublimit) so the founder is not personally on the hook pound-for-pound for pre-Closing tax. Insurer typically requires Big 4 tax DD to extend cover.</li><li><strong>Survival:</strong> 18 months for general at SPA level (24&ndash;36 months at policy level); 7 years for fundamental and tax warranties; tax deed survives statute of limitations + 30&ndash;60 days (TMA 1970 ss.34/36/36A &mdash; up to 20 years for deliberate behaviour).</li><li><strong>Knowledge scrape:</strong> warranty knowledge scrape at 10&ndash;15% premium uplift, particularly important given the founder&rsquo;s deep historic knowledge &mdash; the buyer doesn\'t want a seller-knowledge qualifier defeating cover. Add <em>materiality scrape</em> in the policy aligned to any SPA materiality scrape.</li><li><strong>Disclosure:</strong> comprehensive disclosure letter; the historic customer-contract issues identified in DD are <em>known matters</em> excluded from the W&amp;I &mdash; structure as a separate seller indemnity outside the W&amp;I cap (capped at, say, &pound;2m, to maintain founder\'s clean exit).</li><li><strong>Underwriter pressure points</strong> to anticipate: (a) <strong>cyber sublimit</strong> at 10&ndash;25% of policy limit; (b) <strong>GDPR</strong> exclusion for fines (public-policy non-insurability); (c) <strong>pension underfunding</strong> exclusion if any DB scheme legacy; (d) any specific DD-flagged matters added during the underwriting call.</li><li><strong>No-recourse wording:</strong> buyer agrees not to claim against the founder for any breach of insured warranty (except fraud and fundamental warranties up to fundamental cap). Policy disputes go to the policy&rsquo;s forum (likely London arbitration), not the SPA&rsquo;s English High Court.</li><li><strong>Subrogation:</strong> insurer&rsquo;s subrogation rights against the founder waived except for fraud or wilful concealment.</li></ol><p>Net effect: founder&rsquo;s day-to-day exposure is essentially zero (apart from fraud); buyer has meaningful recourse via the insurer; founder remains exposed only for fundamental warranties up to the fundamental cap and for fraud uncapped; tax deed handled inside W&amp;I or via separate seller indemnity.</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'M14-02', module: '14',
    title: 'US strategic buyer acquiring Delaware medtech with carved-out R&D division (post-2025 hardening)',
    setup: '<p>A US strategic buyer is acquiring a Delaware-incorporated medtech business for $400m. The deal is governed by Delaware law. The seller is a sponsor PE fund (Onslow Capital). During the auction, two sponsor bidders offered seller-stapled W&amp;I; the strategic buyer didn\'t take the staple, and is now negotiating its own RWI after winning. The deal includes a complex pre-Closing carve-out of an unrelated R&amp;D division. The deal is signing in mid-2026.</p>',
    prompt: 'What are the principal structural considerations for the RWI in this May 2026 US deal? What underwriter pressure points should you anticipate?',
    modelAnswer: '<ol><li><strong>Naming:</strong> US terminology is <strong>RWI</strong> (Representations &amp; Warranties Insurance); functionally identical to UK / EU W&amp;I.</li><li><strong>Premium (May 2026):</strong> US primary RoL ~<strong>2.3&ndash;3.5%</strong> post-2025 hardening (Marsh recorded a 16% YoY rate increase in 2025 from the 2024 trough of ~2.0&ndash;3.0% per CBIZ). For a $400m EV deal with a $40m policy limit (10% EV), premium ~$0.9&ndash;1.4m. Higher than UK / EU because US claim frequency historically higher and US deal complexity (litigation, class actions, regulatory) greater.</li><li><strong>Retention:</strong> typically 0.5&ndash;1% in US (often higher than UK / EU); for $400m, retention $2&ndash;4m. Stepped retention with drop-down at 12 months is standard.</li><li><strong>Stapled history:</strong> the buyer didn&rsquo;t take the seller-stapled offer in the auction; running its own underwriting now (slower, possibly less favourable terms because the sponsor&rsquo;s broker no longer controls the process). Consequence: tighter timeline; bidder must compress NBI &rarr; underwriting call &rarr; bind into available signing window.</li><li><strong>CFE limit:</strong> $40m policy = primary layer only; one insurer sufficient. If buyer wants to scale up to a $200m tower (50% of EV), expect 4&ndash;5 layers and 4&ndash;5 different insurers, each within its CFE.</li><li><strong>Sandbagging interaction:</strong> Delaware default is pro-sandbagging (<em>Cobalt Operating</em>, 2007); the SPA can be silent on sandbagging and the buyer retains its claim regardless of pre-signing knowledge. The W&amp;I policy will typically still exclude matters known to the insured (regardless of contractual sandbagging position) unless a buyer knowledge scrape is negotiated (rare). Buyer DD reports in the policy file effectively constitute "known matters."</li><li><strong>Carve-out specifics:</strong> the pre-Closing R&amp;D carve-out introduces specific risks (transition services, asset / liability allocation, IP rights) that may need <em>specific contingent risk insurance</em> separately, or a specific seller indemnity outside the W&amp;I cap. The W&amp;I will have an "ordinary course" exclusion that may catch carve-out mechanics.</li><li><strong>Underwriter pressure points</strong> for US medtech:<ul><li><strong>Cyber sublimit</strong> at 10&ndash;25% of policy limit (medtech is data-heavy);</li><li><strong>Wage-and-hour</strong> exclusion or sublimit (state-by-state, esp. California or New York);</li><li><strong>FDA regulatory</strong> exclusion / sublimit (medtech-specific; any open enforcement matter should be carved out);</li><li><strong>Product liability</strong> sublimit (medical devices);</li><li><strong>Specific DD-flagged matters</strong> added during the underwriting call after underwriter reviews DD reports.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Disclosure:</strong> US practice is disclosure schedules attached to SPA, with anti-bundling for cross-references.</li><li><strong>Sole and exclusive remedy:</strong> standard US clause channelling claims into the indemnification regime; carve out fraud (<em>ABRY Partners v F&amp;W Acquisition</em>, Del. Ch. 2006).</li><li><strong>No-recourse:</strong> coordinate with sponsor&rsquo;s request for "no recourse" &mdash; the SPA cap likely $1; fundamental representations carved out at fundamental cap (often 100% of price); fraud uncapped.</li><li><strong>Policy dispute forum:</strong> typically New York state court (or sometimes Delaware) for US RWI &mdash; independent of the SPA&rsquo;s Delaware Chancery / Superior Court forum. If the insurer denies, dispute is in the policy&rsquo;s forum.</li></ol>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'M14-03', module: '14',
    title: 'UK PE sponsor — tax deed inside or outside W&I?',
    setup: '<p>You are advising a UK PE sponsor exiting a &pound;500m EV manufacturing business in mid-2026. The sponsor wants the cleanest possible exit. The Target has a complex international tax structure including: (a) a German subsidiary with active transfer-pricing positions on intra-group financing; (b) a historic UK group reorganisation in 2019 that may have triggered de-grouping charges (currently disputed with HMRC); (c) a Luxembourg holding structure under an OECD Pillar Two GloBE compliant minimum-tax framework. The buyer is a US strategic. The buyer\'s broker has issued a primary NBI at 1.4% RoL on a 10% policy with a 0.5% stepped retention &mdash; with a transfer-pricing carve-out and exclusion for the disputed UK de-grouping matter.</p>',
    prompt: 'How should the tax deed and W&I interact? Map each tax exposure to the right risk-transfer instrument.',
    modelAnswer: '<p>This is a textbook UK-deal question where the partner asks: <em>"is the tax deed inside the W&amp;I, or are we seller-on-the-hook for it?"</em> The answer depends on each tax position individually.</p><p><strong>Tax warranty vs. tax deed (refresher).</strong> The <em>tax warranty</em> in the SPA is a contractual statement that the Target&rsquo;s tax position is as described (returns filed; no audit pending; no undisclosed liabilities) &mdash; covered by W&amp;I in the same way as any other warranty, subject to its exclusions. The <em>tax deed / tax covenant</em> is a separate UK-practice deed under which the seller indemnifies the buyer pound-for-pound for any pre-Closing tax liability emerging post-Closing &mdash; without basket, de minimis or knowledge qualifier. Survives the tax statute of limitations + 30&ndash;60 days (TMA 1970 ss.34/36/36A periods &mdash; up to 20 years for deliberate behaviour).</p><p><strong>Mapping the three tax positions:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>German transfer-pricing.</strong> The W&amp;I primary NBI has carved this out. The buyer should: (a) require a <em>Big 4 transfer-pricing study</em> to support the existing intra-group financing arrangements; (b) push the broker to bring back the cover at sublimit (transfer pricing is improving as a coverage area &mdash; insurers offer cover with proper DD); (c) failing that, take out <em>specific tax insurance</em> for the transfer-pricing position (single-issue policy underwritten by reference to the specific risk; pricing is risk-specific rather than RoL-based). The tax deed should contain the seller&rsquo;s pound-for-pound indemnity for any uninsured transfer-pricing exposure.</li><li><strong>UK 2019 de-grouping (disputed with HMRC).</strong> This is a "known matter" &mdash; explicitly excluded from W&amp;I (the policy excludes matters known to the insured at bind). The buyer should: (a) take out <em>specific contingent risk insurance</em> on the de-grouping issue (the insurer underwrites the litigation outcome); or (b) require a stand-alone seller indemnity in the SPA outside the symbolic cap, with adequate seller backstop (escrow, parent guarantee, or completion accounts retention) given the sponsor will dissolve the SPV. The tax deed survives long enough to cover the matter, but is worthless without seller solvency &mdash; so buyer needs cash backstop.</li><li><strong>Luxembourg / Pillar Two structure.</strong> Pillar Two GloBE rules (in force since 2024) drive a top-up minimum tax. Forward-looking compliance is excluded from W&amp;I (forward-looking statements are universally excluded). Historical Pillar Two compliance for the post-1-Jan-2024 period is in scope of the tax warranty. Buyer should obtain (a) DD comfort on Pillar Two positions; (b) tax deed cover for any pre-Closing minimum-tax shortfall; (c) Pillar Two compliance certifications from the seller.</li></ol><p><strong>Tax deed inside or outside W&amp;I?</strong> The buyer should push for the tax deed to be <em>inside</em> the W&amp;I where possible &mdash; i.e., insurer agrees the policy responds to claims under the tax deed (treating them as warranty-equivalent). This is more controversial than the tax warranty itself; not all insurers offer it; pricing is an extra ~5&ndash;10% RoL uplift. If the insurer won&rsquo;t extend, the tax deed remains a stand-alone seller obligation outside the symbolic cap &mdash; meaning the sponsor cannot truly clean-exit on the tax tail; needs an escrow or parent guarantee for tax matters specifically.</p><p><strong>Partner phrasebook moment:</strong> "we&rsquo;ve carved transfer-pricing back to a sublimit subject to Big 4 DD; the de-grouping is contingent risk &mdash; specific cover or seller-backed escrow; Pillar Two is in scope but flag any post-Closing top-up to a tax-tail covenant; we&rsquo;re pushing the tax deed inside the W&amp;I at +5&ndash;10% RoL &mdash; if the insurer holds firm, we need a tax escrow."</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'M14-04', module: '14',
    title: 'Cross-border auction — seller-stapled W&I and the fronted-policy question',
    setup: '<p>A French sponsor is exiting a Pan-European industrials business at &euro;800m EV. The auction is running with five bidders: two US strategics, two European PE houses, one Asian SWF. The sponsor has run a seller-stapled W&amp;I process and the broker has stapled an NBI to the bid pack: 1.2% primary RoL on a 12% (&euro;96m) policy, 0.5%&rarr;0.25% stepped retention, knowledge scrape included at 12% premium uplift. Three of the bidders ask in their bid clarifications: "is the policy fronted or all-domestic, and does it have a German pension carve-out?" The Target has a legacy German DB pension scheme with c. &euro;30m IFRS deficit and a UK subsidiary.</p>',
    prompt: 'Walk through the partner-meeting answer: explain the fronted-vs-all-domestic question, the pension underfunding treatment, and the seller-stapled mechanic.',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Seller-stapled mechanic.</strong> The sponsor&rsquo;s broker has run the underwriting process upfront, obtained an NBI from a preferred underwriter, and "stapled" the term sheet to the bid pack so all five bidders see the same NBI and can flip the same policy on signing. The sponsor controls choreography &mdash; W&amp;I is no longer a bid differentiator; it&rsquo;s a level-playing-field input. Bidders run their own DD, conduct their own underwriting calls (each bidder has a separate call with the underwriter post-signing of exclusivity), and bind the same broad policy structure. The benefit to the sponsor: compressed timeline; bid-comparable terms; clean-exit certainty regardless of winner.</p><p><strong>Fronted vs all-domestic.</strong> The bidders are asking whether the W&amp;I policy is issued by:</p><ul><li><strong>All-domestic</strong> &mdash; a single insurer (e.g., a London-market specialist) issues and retains the entire risk in one jurisdiction.</li><li><strong>Fronted</strong> &mdash; a locally-authorised insurer (e.g., a French insurer for the French sponsor / the Pan-European target jurisdictions) issues the paper to satisfy domestic regulatory or tax requirements; behind the scenes, the actual risk is reinsured to a London-market specialist underwriter.</li></ul><p>For a Pan-European industrials business with French, German, UK, possibly Italian operations, fronting is common because: (a) French / Italian local-authorisation requirements; (b) premium tax / stamp-duty considerations; (c) the principal underwriter (likely London) doesn&rsquo;t have local-authorisation in every jurisdiction. Adds coordination steps: the fronting insurer&rsquo;s authority to settle claims; the reinsurance treaty&rsquo;s exclusions matching the policy&rsquo;s; settlement coordination. Slightly higher cost (~5&ndash;10% RoL uplift) and a small additional negotiation timeline.</p><p><strong>German pension underfunding carve-out.</strong> The &euro;30m DB deficit is exactly the kind of exposure W&amp;I underwriters near-universally exclude. The carve-out will be in the policy. Bidders should be told:</p><ul><li>Pension underfunding is excluded from the W&amp;I (universal market practice for European DB schemes given liability discount-rate volatility, regulatory clearance risk &mdash; e.g., German <em>Pensions-Sicherungs-Verein</em>; UK Pensions Regulator &mdash; and historic claims activity).</li><li>The sponsor is offering a <strong>specific seller indemnity outside the W&amp;I</strong> capped at the IFRS deficit + buffer (say &euro;40m), backstopped by an escrow of &euro;15m for 24 months and a parent guarantee from the sponsor&rsquo;s management entity for the balance.</li><li>Alternatively, bidders can purchase <em>pension-specific insurance</em> from a specialist insurer (premium ~5&ndash;10% of the deficit per annum). Less common at this size; usually used where the sponsor refuses to provide a backstop.</li></ul><p><strong>Partner-meeting summary:</strong> "seller-stapled at 1.2% primary, 0.5% stepped retention; the policy is fronted through a French insurer, reinsured to London; pension underfunding carved out, &euro;30m specific seller indemnity backstopped by &euro;15m escrow plus parent guarantee; CFE limit on primary is &euro;50m so the &euro;96m tower needs two layers and two insurers; underwriting calls in the next two weeks with bidder-specific DD; bind at SPA signing."</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  }
];

/* Scenarios fragment — Module 15 v3 fix-agent (Ancillary Transaction Documents)
 *
 * Per build-data.sh: __SCEN_M15 fully replaces any letter-fragment items with module === '15'.
 * Two scenarios: (1) refreshed PE rollover scenario emphasising sweet equity / envy / strip;
 * (2) new TSA forcing-mechanics scenario for a carve-out.
 */
window.__SCEN_M15 = [
  {
    id: 'M15-01', module: '15', type: 'pe-rollover-architecture',
    title: 'PE buyout with founder rollover — strip, sweet equity, envy, and the SHA package',
    setup: 'A PE sponsor is acquiring 75% of TechCo for £200m enterprise value. The two founders, Alice and Bob, will each retain 12.5% of the post-deal ordinary equity. The sponsor proposes that its £150m investment be split into: (i) £140m of preferred / loan notes paying 9% PIK (paid in kind), and (ii) £10m of ordinary shares ("the strip ratio"). The founders will roll £4m of cash equity into ordinary shares only (no participation in the preferred element). Both founders will sign new 3-year employment contracts with 12-month notice and 18-month garden leave / non-compete. The sponsor wants the founders locked in, properly incentivised, with appropriate safeguards if a founder exits. The founders want flexibility and meaningful upside if the company performs.',
    prompt: 'Walk through the closing-bible package, the institutional-strip economics (envy ratio), the leaver mechanics, and the two or three most contentious negotiation points an associate should expect.',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Closing-bible package (selection):</strong></p><ol><li><strong>SPA</strong> &mdash; purchase mechanics, R&amp;Ws, indemnities, escrow withholding (now small given W&amp;I).</li><li><strong>Disclosure Letter</strong> (UK) &mdash; qualifying the warranties; bring-down Disclosure Letter at Completion is in scope.</li><li><strong>Tax Deed</strong> &mdash; pre-closing tax indemnification; 12-year limitation under Limitation Act 1980 s. 8; sits next to the W&amp;I policy (general tax warranties inside the policy; specific tax indemnities outside).</li><li><strong>Shareholders Agreement (SHA)</strong> &mdash; governance (sponsor appoints majority of board, founders one each, possibly an independent), reserved matters / consent rights (sponsor veto on M&amp;A, debt above threshold, hiring/firing CEO, capex above threshold), drag-along (sponsor can drag founders on a sale above 50% threshold or a higher founder-friendly threshold of 75%), tag-along, pre-emption rights, leaver provisions, exit triggers including IPO / trade sale.</li><li><strong>New Articles of Association</strong> mirroring SHA share-transfer restrictions (so future shareholders are bound &mdash; the SHA only binds signatories under section 33 Companies Act 2006; the Articles bind everyone).</li><li><strong>Founder Employment Contracts</strong> &mdash; 12-month notice, 18-month garden leave plus 12-month post-employment non-compete restricted to actual geographic markets and competitor list (Tillman v Egon Zehnder [2019] UKSC 32 severance approach).</li><li><strong>Management Incentive Plan (MIP)</strong> documents structuring the strip and sweet-equity instruments &mdash; vesting schedule (4 years with 1-year cliff), good / intermediate / bad leaver mechanics, hurdle and ratchet provisions.</li><li><strong>IP assignments</strong> for founder-held IP.</li><li><strong>Escrow Agreement</strong> &mdash; smaller given W&amp;I (~0.3% median on insured deals per SRS Acquiom 2025).</li><li><strong>W&amp;I policy</strong> &mdash; itself a closing-bible item; with seller-warranty bridge below the retention to fill the cap-to-attachment-point gap.</li><li><strong>Funds Flow Memorandum</strong> &mdash; routing the price, escrow, W&amp;I premium, lender payoffs, founders\' rollover proceeds, and management completion bonuses.</li></ol><p><strong>Institutional strip economics &mdash; envy ratio.</strong> The sponsor invests £150m: £140m preferred (9% PIK; senior; repaid in full with accrued return before any value flows to ordinary) plus £10m ordinary (strip ratio: 14:1 preferred:ordinary). Founders roll £4m into ordinary only. Sponsor effective cost-per-ordinary-share: implied via the £10m sponsor ordinary tranche valuation. Founders\' cost-per-ordinary-share: their £4m / their share count. The envy ratio compares these effective costs &mdash; mid-market UK norms are 3&ndash;5&times;. A 4&times; envy means the sponsor effectively paid four times what founders paid for the same upside-leveraged ordinary share, because founders are not investing in the preferred element that has to be repaid first.</p><p><strong>Hurdle and ratchet.</strong> Sponsor proposes an 8&ndash;12% IRR hurdle on the strip below which sweet equity does not begin to participate; founders push for a lower hurdle (or no hurdle). Above 20&ndash;25% IRR, a one-way ratchet steps founders\' percentage up (e.g., 25% &rarr; 30% of ordinary). Sponsor prefers high hurdles + one-way ratchet; founders prefer low hurdles + two-way symmetry.</p><p><strong>Leaver mechanics interaction with sweet equity.</strong> Bad-leaver (resignation without good reason; dismissal for cause; breach of restrictive covenants): manager forfeits unvested sweet equity AND, in the most aggressive drafting, forfeits the loan-note element. Good leaver (death, disability, dismissal without cause, material employer breach): FMV for vested shares; loan-note return preserved. Intermediate leaver (resignation after 3+ years\' service): weighted price (e.g., between cost and FMV depending on tenure).</p><p><strong>Three most contentious negotiation points:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Bad-leaver definition and clawback scope.</strong> Sponsor wants broad bad-leaver (anything other than dismissal-without-cause = bad leaver) AND clawback of both sweet equity and loan-note element. Founders want narrow bad leaver (only dismissal for gross misconduct or breach of restrictive covenant) and preservation of loan-note return on any leaver category except bad-leaver-for-cause. Compromise: intermediate-leaver category for resignation after 3+ years (FMV with 70% multiplier); bad-leaver only for dismissal-for-cause or breach (cost basis); loan-note return preserved on intermediate leaver but stripped on bad-leaver-for-cause.</li><li><strong>Envy ratio and hurdle.</strong> Founders argue for 5&times; envy and 8% hurdle (low bar so sweet equity participates on moderate exits); sponsor argues for 3&times; envy and 12% hurdle. Compromise often at 4&times; envy and 10% hurdle, with a one-way ratchet rewarding founders for IRR above 20%.</li><li><strong>Drag-along threshold and reserved-matter scope.</strong> Sponsor wants 50% drag (founders can be dragged on any majority-supported sale); founders want 75% drag and a "minimum return" overlay on the founders\' price. Reserved matters: sponsor wants veto on hiring/firing CEO; founders want autonomy on day-to-day operations. Compromise: 50% drag with a minimum-return floor for founders only on a sub-2&times; money sale; veto on M&amp;A, debt, capex, CEO change, but not day-to-day operations.</li></ul>'
  },
  {
    id: 'M15-02', module: '15', type: 'tsa-forcing-mechanics',
    title: 'Carve-out TSA — forcing mechanics and the upstream cascade',
    setup: 'A US public industrial conglomerate ("Parent") is selling its specialty-chemicals division ("Target") to a UK PE sponsor for £400m. Target rides on Parent\'s shared SAP ERP system, payroll provider, treasury, IT helpdesk, and procurement function. A 12-month TSA is contemplated. Target also hosts the regulated payments licence under which Parent\'s North American distribution arm processes customer refunds — Parent therefore needs Target to keep providing that one service back to Parent during a transition period. The first draft TSA from Parent\'s counsel proposes: cost-plus 7%, 12 months term with 6-month renewal at Parent\'s option, no MFN, no exit-services covenant, no reverse TSA, no step-up.',
    prompt: 'As the buyer\'s associate, identify the major TSA negotiation issues, frame the upstream cascade implications for DD and pricing, and propose a marked-up package the senior partner can sign off on.',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Major TSA issues with the first draft:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>No step-up pricing.</strong> Flat cost-plus 7% gives Parent no commercial pressure to enable migration and gives the buyer no incentive to leave on time &mdash; the TSA could drift indefinitely. Counter: step-up schedule, e.g., 100% of cost months 1&ndash;6, 110% months 7&ndash;12, 125% months 13&ndash;18, 150% months 19&ndash;24, with a hard stop at month 24. The 7% mark-up should drop or convert into the step-up.</li><li><strong>Renewal at Parent\'s option is buyer-poison.</strong> A unilateral renewal lets Parent extend the buyer\'s service dependency. Counter: hard stop at month 24, renewable only by mutual agreement; remove Parent\'s unilateral 6-month renewal.</li><li><strong>No MFN clause.</strong> Without MFN, Parent can cross-subsidise its retained business and raise the buyer\'s prices without raising affiliate prices. Counter: MFN limited to "equivalent" services on a defined comparable basis &mdash; the buyer gets the best commercial terms Parent gives any retained affiliate for equivalent services.</li><li><strong>No exit-services / migration-cooperation covenant.</strong> Without it, the hard stop at month 24 is unrealistic &mdash; the buyer cannot leave without data extracts, schema documentation, vendor introductions, knowledge-transfer sessions, and (for SAP / payroll) parallel-run periods. Counter: defined exit-services list with named seller personnel, defined deliverable formats, and parallel-run obligations for IT systems.</li><li><strong>No reverse TSA.</strong> Target hosts Parent\'s payments-licence service &mdash; the buyer holds the leverage. Counter: insist on a reverse TSA at the same pricing, term and SLAs as the forward TSA; the buyer (now operating Target) becomes the TSA service provider for Parent\'s North American distribution. This both compensates the buyer fairly and gives the buyer commercial leverage during the migration period.</li><li><strong>SLA baseline.</strong> Internal "SLAs" Parent uses for itself are often aspirational. Counter: bake in actual historical service levels measured for the prior 12 months as the contractual minimum; cap liability at 12 months of fees per affected service excluding consequential damages.</li><li><strong>Step-up exclusion for buyer-side delay.</strong> Parent will argue step-ups should not apply if the buyer&rsquo;s migration delay is what extended the period. Counter: step-ups apply unless Parent breached the exit-services covenant; otherwise the step-up forcing mechanism is gutted.</li></ol><p><strong>Upstream cascade &mdash; what to flag to the deal team:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Module 04 (DD).</strong> Shared-services DD must surface the cost normalisations: Target&rsquo;s reported P&amp;L includes shared corporate-cost allocations from Parent that will not continue as such. Quantify the additional standalone cost (the run-rate cost-base that emerges when Target is on its own) &mdash; this drives the synergy / dis-synergy bridge in valuation.</li><li><strong>Module 11 (Locked Box vs. Closing Accounts).</strong> Because the cost base will materially change as services migrate during the gap period, locked-box pricing mis-prices the deal. Pivot to closing accounts &mdash; the working-capital and cash adjustments at Completion will reflect the actual TSA-period cost base, not a stale signing-date snapshot.</li><li><strong>Module 17 (Integration / post-closing).</strong> The integration roadmap is constrained by what the TSA actually delivers. The PMI (post-merger integration) plan must align with the step-up schedule &mdash; if the step-up to 125% kicks in at month 13, the migration plan must put the buyer in independent operation by month 12.</li></ul><p><strong>Marked-up TSA package to propose:</strong></p><ul><li>Cost-plus 5% (down from 7%) with step-ups: 100% months 1&ndash;6, 110% months 7&ndash;12, 125% months 13&ndash;18, 150% months 19&ndash;24.</li><li>Hard stop at month 24, renewable only by mutual agreement; remove Parent&rsquo;s unilateral renewal option.</li><li>MFN limited to "equivalent" services.</li><li>Exit-services covenant: defined deliverables (data extracts, schema documentation, vendor introductions, knowledge-transfer sessions, parallel-run periods for SAP / payroll), with named seller personnel and a 30-day cure period for breach.</li><li>Reverse TSA mirroring the forward TSA pricing, term, and SLAs &mdash; for Parent&rsquo;s North American distribution payments-licence service.</li><li>SLA baseline measured against actual historical service levels for the prior 12 months.</li><li>Liability cap: 12 months of fees per affected service, excluding consequential damages; carve-out for Parent&rsquo;s deliberate breach of the exit-services covenant.</li></ul><p><strong>Senior-partner phrases to use:</strong> "is the TSA forcing &mdash; when does the price step?", "are we on a hard stop or just stepped pricing?", "do we have an exit covenant?", "is there a reverse TSA?", "what does the MFN catch?".</p>'
  }
];

/* Per-module scenarios fragment — Module 16 (Governing Law & Dispute Resolution).
 * Per build-data.sh: __SCEN_M16 fully replaces any letter-fragment items with module === '16'.
 * v3 fix-agent: three partner-grade scenarios complementing the M16 glossary, flashcards and quiz.
 *  (01) Cross-document dispute-clause misalignment + post-Brexit anti-suit;
 *  (02) Sovereign buyer with State Immunity Act waiver + parallel BIT/ICSID exposure;
 *  (03) Closing Accounts: expert determination vs LCIA arbitration, Barclays Bank v Nylon Capital
 *       boundary, with cross-link to Module 11.
 */
window.__SCEN_M16 = [
  {
    id: 'M16-01',
    module: '16',
    type: 'fact-pattern',
    title: 'Mismatched dispute clauses across SPA, SHA, TSA and escrow — drawing the litigation map',
    setup: '<p>The buyer (a UK PE sponsor, BidCo Jersey) is acquiring a German-headquartered specialty-chemicals group from a French industrial conglomerate for &euro;650m EV. Closing was nine months ago. A post-closing dispute has emerged over a tax warranty (claim ~&euro;42m), a closing-accounts purchase-price adjustment (~&euro;9m), and seller breaches under a 12-month TSA. The deal documents are: (i) SPA &mdash; English law, ICC arbitration, Paris seat; (ii) Shareholders Agreement (sponsor + two minority management rollover holders) &mdash; English law, LCIA arbitration, London seat; (iii) Tax Deed &mdash; English law, English High Court exclusive jurisdiction; (iv) TSA &mdash; German law, Frankfurt courts exclusive jurisdiction; (v) Escrow Agreement (Luxembourg escrow agent holding &euro;25m) &mdash; Luxembourg law, asymmetric clause permitting the escrow agent to sue anywhere but restricting the parties to Luxembourg courts. The seller has just filed a declaratory action in the Paris Tribunal de commerce seeking a declaration that no warranty has been breached &mdash; clearly a torpedo move to disrupt the SPA arbitration.</p>',
    prompt: 'The senior partner asks: "Draw me the litigation map. Where do each of these claims actually go, what are the parallel-proceedings risks, what are our anti-suit options, and what is the order of attack?"',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Litigation map &mdash; claim by claim.</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Tax warranty (&euro;42m).</strong> The SPA arbitration clause (ICC, Paris seat, English law) ostensibly captures it. But the Tax Deed has its own exclusive English High Court jurisdiction clause; the same factual matrix may sound in both instruments. Under the <em>Fiona Trust</em> [2007] UKHL 40 "one-stop shop" presumption, a rational businessman would not split closely related claims, so the SPA arbitration tribunal will likely take jurisdiction over warranty issues even if the buyer also pleads Tax Deed indemnity. Proper sequencing: ICC arbitration on the warranty; reserve Tax Deed indemnity in the High Court as belt-and-braces, agreeing a stay there pending the arbitration outcome to avoid duplicative findings.</li><li><strong>Closing-accounts adjustment (&euro;9m).</strong> Almost certainly carved out to expert determination under a separate clause in the SPA (standard UK / European market practice). Read the SPA &mdash; if the price-adjustment dispute mechanism puts it to a "Big Four firm acting as expert and not as arbitrator," <em>Barclays Bank plc v Nylon Capital LLP</em> [2011] EWCA Civ 826 (Lord Neuberger MR) governs the boundary &mdash; expert determination is final and binding subject only to manifest error or fraud, not subject to Article V challenges or section 68/69 set-aside. Cross-link to <strong>Module 11</strong>.</li><li><strong>TSA breaches.</strong> German law, Frankfurt courts exclusive. A separate proceeding before the Landgericht Frankfurt am Main. No consolidation possible because the dispute mechanism, governing law, and forum all diverge from the SPA.</li><li><strong>Escrow.</strong> Asymmetric clause permitting escrow agent to sue anywhere; parties restricted to Luxembourg. Under the French <em>Rothschild</em> line (Cass. civ. 1re, 26 September 2012) and as moderated by <em>Apple Sales</em> (Cass. civ. 1re, 7 October 2015), the asymmetric clause is potentially vulnerable in France but the escrow itself is held in Luxembourg, which under English authorities (<em>Mauritius Commercial Bank v Hestia Holdings</em>; <em>Etihad v Fl&ouml;ther</em>) and Brussels Recast Article 25 is enforceable. A claim to release escrow proceeds in line with the SPA outcome would normally be brought before Luxembourg courts.</li></ol><p><strong>Parallel-proceedings risk and the seller&rsquo;s torpedo.</strong> The seller&rsquo;s Paris commercial-court declaratory action is a classic torpedo: launched in the seat of the arbitration to embarrass or delay the arbitration. Under French law, the <em>comp&eacute;tence-comp&eacute;tence</em> doctrine (Article 1448 Code de proc&eacute;dure civile) means the Paris court must defer to the ICC tribunal&rsquo;s own jurisdictional ruling, so the torpedo should be deflected on jurisdictional motion within months. But it forces additional cost and process time. Brussels Recast Article 29 <em>lis pendens</em> does not apply between an EU court and an arbitration tribunal &mdash; <em>Gazprom v Lithuania</em> (CJEU C-536/13) and Recital 12 confirm.</p><p><strong>Anti-suit options.</strong> Post-Brexit, English courts grant anti-suit injunctions against EU member-state proceedings on the same basis as against other foreign proceedings (the <em>Turner v Grovit</em> / <em>West Tankers</em> CJEU constraint no longer binds). Under section 37 Senior Courts Act 1981, supplemented by section 44 Arbitration Act 1996, the test from <em>The Angelic Grace</em> applies: (i) clear and proper case that the foreign proceedings are in breach of the arbitration agreement; (ii) no compelling reasons to refuse. <em>Times Trading Corp v National Bank of Fujairah</em> [2020] EWHC 1078 (Comm) is the post-Brexit illustration. Speed is everything &mdash; apply before the Paris court progresses materially. <strong>Caveat:</strong> the seat is Paris. French courts may issue an anti-anti-suit injunction (the procedural arms race seen in some Russia-related disputes); but France has no general anti-anti-suit doctrine in commercial matters, so the risk is moderate. After <em>Enka v Chubb</em> [2020] UKSC 38, the law of the SPA arbitration agreement should be English (as the substantive contract law is English) &mdash; verify the SPA does not have a separate AA-law clause pointing elsewhere; if it does, the anti-suit availability shifts.</p><p><strong>Order of attack.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Day 1:</strong> File urgent application in the English Commercial Court for an anti-suit injunction against the seller&rsquo;s Paris proceedings on the basis that the SPA arbitration agreement is governed by English law (per <em>Enka</em>) and the seller&rsquo;s declaratory action breaches it. Seek interim anti-suit pending return date.</li><li><strong>Day 1&ndash;7:</strong> File the ICC Request for Arbitration on the tax warranty. This formally seizes the tribunal and allows a competence-competence ruling that supersedes the Paris torpedo regardless of anti-suit outcome.</li><li><strong>Week 2:</strong> Issue the expert-determination notice for the closing-accounts dispute under the SPA mechanism &mdash; this is procedurally independent and should not be delayed.</li><li><strong>Month 1:</strong> Issue Tax Deed High Court proceedings and immediately apply for a stay pending the ICC arbitration. Belt-and-braces preservation of the Tax Deed claim against limitation (Limitation Act 1980 s.8: 12 years for a deed; longer than the 6-year simple-contract period under s.5).</li><li><strong>Month 1&ndash;2:</strong> Initiate German court proceedings on the TSA (statute of limitations under BGB &sect; 195 is 3 years from end of year of knowledge). Coordinate with the ICC arbitration on overlapping witnesses.</li><li><strong>Month 2:</strong> Notice the escrow agent of the disputes and instruct it to retain the &euro;25m pending resolution. Under Luxembourg law, the escrow agent will require a court order or party agreement before releasing.</li></ul><p><strong>What an M&amp;A litigator would actually do here.</strong> Treat this as a coordinated multi-track campaign rather than as one dispute. Run the ICC arbitration as the centre of gravity (the largest claim and the highest-leverage forum). Use the anti-suit application as both a deterrent and a procedural pressure tool against the seller. Sequence the expert determination, Tax Deed claim, and TSA claim around the ICC timetable to avoid inconsistent findings. Most importantly, document on the file that this mess was created by the original drafting (mismatched dispute clauses across SPA, SHA, Tax Deed, TSA, escrow) &mdash; cross-link to <strong>Module 15</strong> on coordinated drafting at signing and to the <em>parallel-proceedings</em> glossary entry. The cost differential between coordinated drafting at signing and unwinding misalignment at the dispute stage is typically 5&ndash;10x.</p>'
  },
  {
    id: 'M16-02',
    module: '16',
    type: 'fact-pattern',
    title: 'Sovereign-fund buyer in a regulated infrastructure deal &mdash; immunity waiver and parallel BIT exposure',
    setup: '<p>A Gulf sovereign wealth fund (acting through a Cayman SPV that is its wholly-owned investment vehicle) is acquiring 80% of a UK regulated water utility from an Australian infrastructure fund for &pound;1.4bn. The target&rsquo;s regulated status means Ofwat consent is required and certain undertakings to the regulator survive closing. The seller&rsquo;s counsel proposes the standard BVCA-style SPA: English law, LCIA London-seated arbitration, no immunity waiver, no carve-out for regulatory matters. A junior partner on the seller side asks: "we can&rsquo;t enforce against an SWF if it walks away from a price-adjustment claim &mdash; we need an immunity waiver." Separately, the buyer&rsquo;s deal team has been told by its policy advisers to "preserve treaty protections" because the host state (the UK) has changing political rhetoric on foreign infrastructure ownership and the Cayman SPV is structured to access a particular BIT.</p>',
    prompt: 'You are advising the seller. Draft the partner-grade response: what immunity waiver is needed, where and why; what is the BIT/ICSID parallel-proceedings risk to the buyer (and what does that mean for the seller&rsquo;s drafting); and how do you reconcile the SPA dispute regime with the regulatory undertakings?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>The immunity waiver question &mdash; why two waivers, not one.</strong> Under the UK State Immunity Act 1978, a state (and certain state-owned entities) is generally immune from the adjudicative jurisdiction of UK courts and from execution against state assets. SIA section 9 treats submission to arbitration as a waiver of <em>adjudicative</em> immunity in respect of court proceedings related to that arbitration &mdash; so an LCIA-seated arbitration agreement is, on its face, sufficient to engage the SWF in the dispute regime. <strong>But</strong> SIA section 13(2)(b) creates a separate <em>execution</em> immunity over state property unless it is in use for commercial purposes. Without an express execution-immunity waiver, an arbitral award against the SWF could be confirmed by the High Court (<em>NML Capital v Argentina</em> [2011] UKSC 31) yet unenforceable against SWF assets. The same two-immunity structure applies under the US Foreign Sovereign Immunities Act 1976 (28 U.S.C. &sect;&sect; 1605, 1610) if any enforcement track touches US assets.</p><p><strong>Threshold question &mdash; is the Cayman SPV a "state" at all?</strong> Under the SIA the test is whether the entity is "a separate entity" of the state and whether the relevant act was done in the exercise of sovereign authority (s.14). Cayman investment SPVs are generally separate legal persons; an arbitral tribunal and the High Court are likely to find they do not enjoy immunity in their own right. <strong>But</strong> if the SWF parent itself is named in the SPA (e.g., as a guarantor of the SPV&rsquo;s payment obligations), that parent must waive separately. And on enforcement, if the buyer side later seeks to claim that SPV assets are in substance state property, immunity returns. Best-practice drafting: name the SWF expressly in a guarantee or undertaking, take the waiver from both the SWF and the SPV, and identify the assets to which the waiver applies.</p><p><strong>Standard waiver wording the seller should insist on.</strong> The waiver should: (i) acknowledge the SWF&rsquo;s and SPV&rsquo;s sovereign / sovereign-related status; (ii) waive immunity from suit, jurisdiction, judgment, set-off, attachment in aid of execution, attachment prior to judgment, execution and any other legal process; (iii) identify the accounts or assets to which the waiver applies (or state that it applies to all property used or held for commercial purposes); (iv) state that the waiver is irrevocable and the SWF will not invoke any immunity at any time; (v) confirm that the arbitration agreement and the ancillary court proceedings do not concern <em>acta jure imperii</em>. Some inalienably immune assets remain (diplomatic mission property; military property; cultural heritage; central bank reserves are debated &mdash; SIA s.14(4) suggests immune save for separate-entity central bank acting commercially).</p><p><strong>BIT/ICSID parallel-proceedings risk.</strong> The buyer&rsquo;s "preserve treaty protections" instruction means: structure the Cayman SPV through (or via a holding-chain step in) a jurisdiction that has a BIT in force with the host state covering the type of investment, so that if the host state takes an action that breaches treaty protections (fair and equitable treatment; expropriation; full protection and security; MFN), the buyer can bring an ICSID or UNCITRAL claim against the host state in parallel to any commercial dispute. Common conduit jurisdictions: Netherlands, Singapore, Luxembourg. The UK has a network of BITs (e.g., UK-UAE BIT) but the Cayman SPV must be eligible under the relevant treaty&rsquo;s "investor" definition. <em>Yukos Universal v Russia</em> (PCA, 2014) is the headline illustration of treaty-claim scale; intra-EU BIT erosion post-<em>Achmea</em> (Case C-284/16) and the May 2020 Termination Agreement do not affect non-EU BITs.</p><p><strong>What that means for the seller&rsquo;s drafting.</strong> The seller cares about the BIT / ICSID track for two reasons: (i) the buyer might try to bring a treaty claim against the UK government on the back of post-closing regulatory action and frame the seller&rsquo;s warranty disclosures as inadequate &mdash; the SPA arbitration must capture that; (ii) the seller does not want to be drawn into ICSID-style proceedings as a non-party witness or interested party. Drafting moves: (a) <strong>broad</strong> arbitration clause covering "any dispute arising out of or in connection with this Agreement" (per <em>Fiona Trust</em>) so warranty-disclosure disputes are arbitrable even if dressed as treaty claims; (b) confidentiality undertakings extending to any related ICSID proceedings; (c) a covenant by the buyer not to bring a treaty claim against the host state on grounds that, if proven, would constitute a warranty breach without first running the SPA dispute mechanism (or at least notifying the seller); (d) keep the SPA arbitration agreement&rsquo;s law expressly English (post-<em>Enka</em>) so anti-suit relief remains available against any forum-shopping.</p><p><strong>Reconciling the SPA dispute regime with the regulatory undertakings.</strong> Ofwat undertakings are between the licensee and the regulator; they are not directly incorporated into the SPA. But the SPA will contain warranties about regulatory compliance and possibly covenants requiring buyer cooperation. Three drafting moves: (i) carve out from the dispute clause anything that requires regulatory consent or determination &mdash; the regulator decides regulatory matters, not an arbitral tribunal; (ii) provide for an interim-relief carve-out enabling either party to seek urgent court relief to prevent breach of the regulatory undertakings (preserves the buyer&rsquo;s and seller&rsquo;s position vis-&agrave;-vis Ofwat without breaching the arbitration agreement); (iii) coordinate with <strong>Module 9 (MAC)</strong> &mdash; if a regulatory action triggers a MAC clause pre-completion, the buyer needs preserved access to court interim relief.</p><p><strong>What an M&amp;A lawyer would actually do here.</strong> The senior partner&rsquo;s mental checklist: <em>"Two waivers, not one. Name the SWF and the SPV separately. Identify commercial-use assets. Carve out regulatory matters. Preserve interim relief. Confirm AA-law is English. Make the buyer covenant not to forum-shop into ICSID without notice."</em> The seller&rsquo;s redline takes 30&ndash;40 minutes of partner time and adds 8&ndash;10 lines to the SPA &mdash; cheap insurance against an unenforceable award. Cross-link to <strong>Module 14 (W&amp;I policy waivers and step-in rights)</strong> and <strong>Module 9 (MAC and interim relief)</strong>.</p>'
  },
  {
    id: 'M16-03',
    module: '16',
    type: 'fact-pattern',
    title: 'Closing Accounts dispute &mdash; expert or arbitrator? The Barclays Bank v Nylon Capital boundary',
    setup: '<p>Twelve months after closing of a &pound;320m UK deal (English law, LCIA London-seated arbitration in the SPA), a closing-accounts purchase-price-adjustment dispute has arisen. Buyer claims a downward adjustment of &pound;7.4m on the basis of (a) inventory write-downs the seller did not reflect (a pure accounting valuation question), (b) provision adequacy for an asbestos litigation claim that crystallised three weeks pre-closing (a mixed legal/accounting judgement), and (c) the proper accounting treatment of an earn-out liability that the seller capitalised but the buyer says should have been expensed (a question of construction of the SPA earn-out clause itself). The SPA dispute mechanism states: "any dispute as to the Closing Accounts shall be referred to an Independent Accountant being one of the Big Four firms acting as expert and not as arbitrator, whose decision shall be final and binding (save in case of manifest error or fraud)." All other disputes go to LCIA. The seller has now filed an LCIA Request for Arbitration alleging the buyer has misclassified items (a) and (c) and that they belong in the LCIA arbitration, not before the expert. The expert (KPMG Forensic) has been appointed and started work on item (b).</p>',
    prompt: 'The senior partner asks: "Where do each of these three issues actually go &mdash; expert or arbitrator? What is the leading authority on the boundary, what are the consequences of getting it wrong, and what should we be doing right now?"',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>The leading authority.</strong> <em>Barclays Bank plc v Nylon Capital LLP</em> [2011] EWCA Civ 826 (Lord Neuberger MR; Thomas LJ) is the controlling English authority on the expert / arbitrator boundary. The test is whether the parties have submitted an actual <em>dispute</em> to a third-party decision-maker (arbitration) or whether they have asked the third party for a <em>determination on a defined issue</em>, typically an issue requiring particular professional skill (expert determination). Critical features:</p><ul><li><strong>Expert determination</strong> &mdash; final and binding subject only to <em>manifest error</em> or <em>fraud</em>; not bound by procedural fairness rules in the same way as an arbitrator; not an "award" within the Arbitration Act 1996; not subject to s.68 (serious irregularity), s.69 (appeal on point of law), or to New York Convention Article V; not enforceable as an arbitral award; expert is contractually liable in negligence (different from arbitral immunity).</li><li><strong>Arbitration</strong> &mdash; a quasi-judicial process; arbitrator bound by procedural fairness; award is an "award" within the Arbitration Act 1996 (or equivalent at the seat); subject to set-aside under section 68/69 (or s.34 of the post-1 January 2025 Arbitration Act 2025 reforms); enforceable internationally under the New York Convention.</li></ul><p><em>Lord Neuberger MR</em> emphasised that the question is one of contractual construction informed by the substance of the function: an "expert" deciding a question that requires a binary determination on legal rights between the parties is in substance an arbitrator, regardless of label.</p><p><strong>Mapping each issue.</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Item (a) &mdash; inventory write-downs (pure accounting valuation).</strong> Squarely within the expert&rsquo;s jurisdiction. This is a classic accounting determination requiring professional skill: applying the consistent accounting policies and the SPA&rsquo;s definition of "Net Assets". <em>Goes to the expert.</em> The LCIA tribunal should decline jurisdiction.</li><li><strong>Item (b) &mdash; asbestos litigation provision (mixed legal / accounting).</strong> Mixed. The legal question (does the asbestos claim need to be provisioned, and at what amount, on the closing date) is partly an accounting judgement (provision-recognition rules under FRS 102 / IAS 37) and partly a legal-merits assessment. Best practice: structure the expert&rsquo;s terms of reference to limit the expert to the accounting framework (probability, measurement, disclosure) while leaving any underlying legal-merits dispute to arbitration if separately raised. In practice, market norm is that closing-accounts experts decide the provision in the round &mdash; <em>goes to the expert</em> &mdash; on the basis that the legal-merits assessment is an input the expert weighs as a professional accountant would. If an arbitral tribunal would reach a different answer to the underlying legal question, that goes to <em>Barclays Bank v Nylon Capital</em> &mdash; the expert is constrained by the SPA terms of reference and cannot rule on legal rights as such.</li><li><strong>Item (c) &mdash; SPA earn-out construction.</strong> This is a pure question of contractual construction of the SPA earn-out clause: does the SPA require the earn-out liability to be capitalised or expensed? The expert is not in a position to construe the SPA &mdash; that is a legal question for the LCIA tribunal under the SPA&rsquo;s general dispute clause. <em>Goes to the LCIA.</em> Critically, <em>Barclays Bank v Nylon Capital</em> warns against construing the expert&rsquo;s mandate too broadly: where the dispute is in substance about the meaning of the SPA itself, the expert is not constituted to decide it.</li></ol><p><strong>Consequences of getting it wrong.</strong> Three-fold and severe:</p><ul><li><strong>Expert decides what should be arbitrated.</strong> Decision is open to challenge as outside the expert&rsquo;s mandate &mdash; potentially void; parties have to start over before the LCIA. Expert may face professional negligence exposure if commercially predictable that a court would set the determination aside.</li><li><strong>Arbitrator decides what should be expert-determined.</strong> Award may be outside the scope of the arbitration agreement (Article V(1)(c) New York Convention; Arbitration Act 1996 s.30, s.67). Award subject to set-aside; counter-party can resist enforcement abroad.</li><li><strong>Parallel proceedings.</strong> Both bodies decide the same issue, producing inconsistent findings &mdash; the expert&rsquo;s decision is final-and-binding subject only to manifest error / fraud; the arbitrator&rsquo;s award is subject to set-aside. The contractual stalemate that follows can take 12&ndash;18 months to unwind.</li></ul><p><strong>What we should be doing right now.</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Within 7 days:</strong> file a robust response to the LCIA Request for Arbitration. Concede that item (c) is properly arbitrable. Argue that items (a) and (b) are properly before the expert under the SPA mechanism. Do <em>not</em> treat the LCIA as a forum to relitigate the accounting issues.</li><li><strong>Within 14 days:</strong> agree with KPMG Forensic and the seller a clear set of <strong>terms of reference</strong> for the expert: (i) confirm that items (a) and (b) are within scope; (ii) state expressly that the expert is not deciding any question of contractual construction of the SPA; (iii) reserve item (c) to the LCIA; (iv) agree that the expert&rsquo;s findings on accounting matters are without prejudice to the LCIA tribunal&rsquo;s findings on contractual construction. Document this in writing.</li><li><strong>Within 30 days:</strong> in the LCIA arbitration, seek a <strong>partial award on jurisdiction</strong> declaring that items (a) and (b) are not arbitrable and that item (c) is arbitrable. This pre-empts later set-aside challenges and accelerates the arbitration on the issue that genuinely belongs there.</li><li><strong>Coordinate timelines:</strong> the expert should not deliver a final determination on item (b) until the LCIA tribunal has ruled on the SPA-construction question in item (c), to avoid the expert assuming a legal answer that the tribunal then reverses. Negotiate a sequencing protocol: tribunal jurisdiction ruling first; expert continues on item (a); item (b) parked until tribunal decision; LCIA tribunal hears item (c) on the merits; expert resumes item (b).</li></ol><p><strong>What an M&amp;A lawyer would actually do here.</strong> Three reflexes the senior partner expects: (i) reach for <em>Barclays Bank v Nylon Capital</em> immediately &mdash; it&rsquo;s the boundary case, and any associate not citing it in the first response loses credibility; (ii) frame the response not as defending the expert&rsquo;s jurisdiction across the board but as <em>conceding</em> item (c) to the LCIA, which signals procedural seriousness and removes the seller&rsquo;s easiest argument; (iii) coordinate the two tracks rather than running them as adversaries &mdash; a pragmatic sequencing protocol with KPMG Forensic and the LCIA tribunal Secretariat is partner-grade work, not a procedural sideshow. Cross-link to <strong>Module 11 (Closing Accounts / Locked Box / Earn-outs)</strong> &mdash; specifically the expert-mechanism drafting that should have anticipated this boundary by carving out SPA-construction questions to arbitration. Cross-link to <strong>Module 17 (Post-Closing Claims Cascade)</strong> on coordinated timelines for parallel claims tracks.</p>'
  }
];

