window.__SCEN_M17 = [
  {
    id: 'M17-01', module: '17', type: 'claim-cascade',
    title: 'Claim cascade — tax-audit discovery on a W&I-backed UK take-private',
    setup: 'BuyerCo is a US-listed strategic that closed an £800m W&I-backed acquisition of UK-headquartered TargetCo eighteen months ago. HMRC has just opened an enquiry into TargetCo\'s pre-closing transfer pricing arrangements with its Irish IP-holding subsidiary, alleging a £45m underpayment plus interest and penalties for periods 2021–2023 (entirely pre-closing). On the same day BuyerCo\'s CFO discovers that TargetCo\'s former finance director (departed 8 months post-closing) sent emails confirming awareness of the transfer-pricing weakness in late 2022. The W&I policy has a £4m retention with £200m of total cover (primary £25m + four £43.75m excess layers); the SPA had a £5m de minimis basket and a £40m cap on general warranty claims (with tax warranties uncapped above the basket). Two of TargetCo\'s former directors are still on BuyerCo\'s acquired-business board.',
    prompt: 'You are the senior associate on the post-closing team. Walk through the integrated 30-day claim cascade you would run on Day 0 of this discovery, with timing, the recipient of each notice, and the consequence of missing each.',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Day 0 — internal investigation and legal hold (privileged).</strong> Open privileged file under Litigation Privilege; brief external counsel; issue legal hold to retained custodians (CFO, controller, tax director, former IP team); commence forensic copy of historical email and accounting systems for the relevant period. <em>Why first:</em> destruction of evidence (auto-deletion policies, retention schedules) is the most common operational failure; once destroyed, no quantum case.</p><p><strong>Day 0–3 — W&I Notification of Circumstances (NOC).</strong> File protective NOC with the W&I primary insurer (and excess layers via the broker), even before the tax position has crystallised. Contents: identify the relevant tax warranty; describe HMRC enquiry; estimate of £45m + interest + penalties; reserve right to supplement. <em>Posture: papering, not substantiating.</em> Consequence of missing: most policies require notification "as soon as reasonably practicable" or within a defined window (typically 30 days from awareness); a late NOC can void cover entirely. Run through the tower so the primary insurer has notice in time to engage its loss adjuster (Crawford / Sedgwick) and reserve its position.</p><p><strong>Day 3–14 — preserve evidence.</strong> Forensic image of the former finance director\'s mailbox and personal device (subject to data-protection / employment law); structured interviews of the controller and tax director; freeze accounting policies and reporting periods affecting the underpayment computation; preserve the contemporaneous pre-closing tax due-diligence file (the disclosure record of what BuyerCo knew at signing is critical to the W&I "fair disclosure" / "deemed disclosure" gap). <em>Why:</em> the most-litigated W&I dispute is whether the matter was disclosed to a sophisticated buyer with sufficient specificity at signing.</p><p><strong>Day 7–21 — SPA notice to seller.</strong> Strict-form notice to seller compliant with the SPA\'s notice provisions: form (signed letter / hand-delivered / registered post); address (the SPA\'s notice address, not the seller\'s commercial address); content (claim under the tax warranty + Tax Deed; reservation of all rights). Tax warranties and the Tax Deed are typically uncapped above the basket; the £45m is comfortably above the £5m basket. <em>Consequence of missing:</em> defective notice can be a complete bar even if substance valid. Run parallel notice under both the SPA and the separate Tax Deed (if used).</p><p><strong>Day 14–30 — D&O notice to former directors\' carrier.</strong> The two TargetCo directors still on BuyerCo\'s acquired-business board, plus the former finance director (8 months departed), face potential personal exposure if HMRC pursues senior officers individually under "deliberate inaccuracy" provisions or under directors\' duty obligations. Notify the post-closing run-off / "tail" D&O policy (typical 6-year run-off in UK practice) of circumstances. <em>Consequence:</em> a formal HMRC penalty notice triggering personal-liability provisions arrives months after the policy notification window has closed if the carrier is not informed early.</p><p><strong>Day 14–30 — lender covenant check.</strong> BuyerCo\'s acquisition finance includes maintenance covenants (typical mid-cap structure) referencing "events that could reasonably be expected to result in a Material Adverse Effect." A £45m HMRC enquiry plus director exposure plausibly meets that test. Notify the agent under the facility agreement; coordinate with treasury on covenant headroom recalculation.</p><p><strong>Day 14–30 — public-disclosure assessment.</strong> BuyerCo is US-listed; assess Form 8-K Item 2.06 (impairment of acquired goodwill if the contingent liability impairs); Item 8.01 (other material events) for any material litigation; SEC Regulation FD (no selective disclosure to analysts). Do <em>not</em> issue a release prematurely — but lock in the team that will be available to advise on a release if the position deteriorates.</p><p><strong>Day 14–30 — integration freeze on the affected workstream.</strong> Pause integration steps that could prejudice the claim or cure the breach inadvertently: do <em>not</em> merge TargetCo into a clean BuyerCo entity; do <em>not</em> change accounting policies on the relevant subsidiaries; do <em>not</em> restructure the Irish IP-holding subsidiary or its inter-company arrangements; preserve all historical contracts, accounting records, and inter-company invoices in their original form.</p><p><strong>Day 30+ — substantiate or contest.</strong> Move from papering to substantiating: upgrade NOC to formal Notification of Claim with quantified loss; engage the W&I insurer\'s loss adjuster on quantification (insurer typically pushes a diminution-in-value standard, insured pushes broader loss); negotiate with seller under the Tax Deed (seller typically has control of the tax dispute given historical knowledge — coordinate joint defence with HMRC).</p><p><strong>Connecting points the partner will want:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Subrogation waiver.</strong> Confirm whether the policy contains a seller-favour subrogation waiver — material to the seller\'s residual exposure once the W&I has paid.</li><li><strong>Eroding retention.</strong> Confirm whether the £4m retention is eroding (consumed dollar-for-dollar by prior payments) or non-eroding (resets each claim). On a deal with multiple potential issues, eroding makes a material difference.</li><li><strong>Soft-staple analysis.</strong> Run the fair-disclosure (W&I) vs. deemed-disclosure (SPA) gap on the tax DD record; identify whether the matter is fairly disclosed to the insurer (excluded from cover but recoverable under the SPA), deemed disclosed under the SPA (excluded from SPA but covered under W&I), or in both / neither.</li><li><strong>Jurat.</strong> Sworn loss-substantiation statement — confirm jurat correctly executed before commissioner for oaths; defective jurat can require re-execution and miss policy deadlines.</li></ul>'
  },
  {
    id: 'M17-02', module: '17', type: 'cross-border-non-compete',
    title: 'Cross-border non-compete enforcement — sale-of-business + executive package post-FTC abandonment',
    setup: 'A US PE sponsor has acquired a UK-headquartered B2B SaaS business (TargetCo) for $400m. The two founders (US citizens, resident in California) and the German country head are key value drivers. The deal documents include: (1) a 36-month sale-of-business non-compete covering global operations; (2) 24-month employee post-termination non-competes for each of the founders and the country head, identical drafting, no compensation provisions; (3) US, UK, and German employment contracts under their respective governing laws. Twelve months post-closing the German country head resigns to join a Berlin-based competitor; the two California-resident founders begin advising a Bay Area startup that the sponsor regards as competitive. The sponsor wants to enforce all three non-competes. The PE deal team has now (May 2026) asked for a memo on enforceability across each jurisdiction.',
    prompt: 'Analyse enforceability of (a) the 36-month sale-of-business non-compete, (b) the founders\' employment non-compete (California), and (c) the country head\'s employment non-compete (Germany). Identify the partner-meeting moves the sponsor should make.',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>(a) 36-month sale-of-business non-compete.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>UK / EU framework.</strong> The European Commission Notice on restrictions directly related and necessary to concentrations (<strong>2005/C 56/03</strong>) treats sale-of-business non-competes as ancillary to the concentration if no longer than 2 years (know-how only) or 3 years (goodwill and know-how). 36 months is within the 3-year envelope where goodwill and know-how are both transferred — defensible as an ancillary restraint not separately reviewed under Article 101 TFEU. Geography (global) and activity scope must be tied to the markets and products in which TargetCo operated; a global tie is sustainable for a global SaaS business. Beyond 3 years requires justification on the specific facts.</li><li><strong>UK common law.</strong> UK courts treat sale-of-business restraints more favourably than employment restraints because the seller is paid for goodwill the buyer must protect (<em>Esso v Harper\'s Garage</em> [1968] AC 269 (HL) is the foundational restraint-of-trade authority; <em>Tillman v Egon Zehnder</em> [2019] UKSC 32 governs the modern severance / blue-pencil approach). 36 months is within the typical 2–5 year range for sale-of-business non-competes.</li><li><strong>US federal posture (May 2026).</strong> The FTC Non-Compete Rule was set aside in <em>Ryan v FTC</em> (N.D. Tex., 20 August 2024); on <strong>5 September 2025</strong> the FTC voted 3-1 to dismiss its own appeal and accede to the vacatur — the Rule is permanently abandoned. The FTC has redirected to case-by-case Section 5 enforcement (inaugural action <em>Gateway Pet Cremation</em>, 4 September 2025), but explicitly carves out (i) <strong>non-competes in the bona fide sale of a business</strong> and (ii) <strong>senior-executive non-competes connected to equity grants</strong>. The sale-of-business non-compete is essentially in the safest zone of US enforcement.</li><li><strong>California carve-out.</strong> Cal. Bus. &amp; Prof. Code <strong>§16601</strong> expressly preserves sale-of-business non-competes from the §16600 prohibition. The 36-month tie on the founders, qua sellers of TargetCo (rather than qua employees), is enforceable in California.</li><li><strong>Verdict:</strong> 36-month sale-of-business non-compete is enforceable in UK, EU, and US (including California). Run.</li></ul><p><strong>(b) Founders\' employment non-compete (California).</strong></p><ul><li>Cal. Bus. &amp; Prof. Code <strong>§16600</strong> voids non-compete agreements in employment except in narrow statutory exceptions (sale of business §16601, partnership dissolution §16602, LLC dissolution §16602.5). California strengthened §16600 in 2024 with SB-699 (extending to out-of-state non-competes that purport to bind California-resident employees) and AB-1076 (requiring employer notice to employees that pre-existing non-competes are void).</li><li>The 24-month employment non-compete on the founders is therefore <strong>void in California qua employment</strong> — even if drafted under New York or Delaware law, §16600 / SB-699 reaches it.</li><li><strong>Tactical move:</strong> the sponsor cannot enforce as an employment non-compete. But the founders also signed the sale-of-business non-compete (point (a)); their advisory work for a competitor breaches that <em>sale-of-business</em> covenant. Sponsor sues under the sale-of-business non-compete and avoids the §16600 trap entirely.</li><li><strong>Senior-executive equity carve-out.</strong> If the founders\' restraints are tied to equity grants (rolled equity, sweet equity, MIP), the FTC\'s case-by-case Section 5 enforcement explicitly carves out senior-executive equity-linked non-competes; this is a reinforcement, not a substitute, for the §16601 sale-of-business analysis at the California level.</li></ul><p><strong>(c) German country head\'s employment non-compete.</strong></p><ul><li>German <em>HGB §74</em> requires <strong>minimum 50% of last salary</strong> (<em>Karenzentschädigung</em>) for the duration of an employee post-termination non-compete; failure to provide compensation either voids the covenant or allows the employee to elect to be bound while recovering the statutory minimum.</li><li>The drafting in the country head\'s contract has <strong>no compensation provision</strong>. Result: the covenant is at best ambiguous and at worst void. The country head\'s move to a Berlin competitor is likely <strong>not enforceable</strong> against without compensation.</li><li><strong>Tactical move:</strong> the sponsor cannot enforce as drafted. Three options: (i) re-paper now with proper compensation provisions, but the country head has already resigned — too late; (ii) offer compensation retrospectively to obtain enforcement, contingent on the country head agreeing to be bound; (iii) accept that the covenant is unenforceable and pursue alternative remedies — sue for breach of post-termination duties of confidentiality (still enforceable) and for misappropriation of trade secrets if facts support it.</li><li><strong>Lessons-learned for DD on continental European acquisitions.</strong> Pre-existing non-competes for senior executives must be DD\'d for proper compensation funding; an unfunded non-compete on a key executive is a hidden integration risk that may surface only on departure. Sponsor budget for non-compete compensation as a recurring post-closing cost.</li></ul><p><strong>Partner-meeting summary.</strong></p><ol><li>Run the sale-of-business non-compete (a) — enforceable in UK, EU (per 2005/C 56/03), US (post-FTC abandonment 5 Sept 2025), and California (§16601).</li><li>Drop the California employment non-compete (b) — §16600 / SB-699 void it as employment but the sale-of-business covenant captures the same conduct.</li><li>Live with the German non-compete being voidable (c) — re-paper future hires with compensation; pursue confidentiality / trade-secret remedies instead.</li><li>Partner question: "<em>Are the founders\' restraints tied to their equity grants?</em>" — if yes, the senior-executive equity-linked carve-out from FTC enforcement adds a reinforcing layer. If no, restructure now via the equity / MIP documents to align with the post-2025 federal posture.</li></ol>'
  }
];

/* Module 18 — v3 fix: per-module scenarios fragment.
 *
 * Replaces the F-18 letter-fragment scenarios. Two scenarios:
 *   M18-01 — UK take-private under post-3 Feb 2025 Code regime (jurisdiction,
 *            Rule 2.4 leak management, wall-cross / hard-soft irrevocables,
 *            certain funds, scheme vs. contractual offer, Rule 21 1% break fee,
 *            MAC under Rule 13.5).
 *   M18-02 — US public-target merger with appraisal-arbitrage threat,
 *            controlling-stockholder MFW structuring, Time-Warner / QVC
 *            framing, deal-protection trilogy, and antitrust-RTF correlation.
 */
window.__SCEN_M18 = [
  {
    id: 'M18-01', module: '18', type: 'public-m&a-uk-take-private-2025',
    title: 'UK take-private under the post-3 February 2025 Code regime — leak, wall-cross, certain funds, scheme',
    setup: 'A US-headquartered private-equity sponsor is contemplating a recommended take-private of a UK-listed industrial-technology company (premium-listed on the London Stock Exchange Main Market; ~£2.4bn equity value; founder-family holds 22% via family trusts and connected nominees; institutional shareholder register otherwise ordinary). The sponsor proposes 60% cash / 40% loan-note alternative for tax-sensitive holders, financed with a $2.0bn TLB / $0.5bn second-lien / $0.4bn revolver / sponsor equity (with a sub-12% rollover from senior management). Due diligence has been ongoing for six weeks under NDA. On Tuesday morning, the Financial Times publishes a "PE Eyeing Industrial-Tech Bid" speculative piece naming the target, the sponsor, and the indicative price. Target shares jump 11% in early trading.',
    prompt: 'Walk through the principal regulatory and structuring decisions the deal team faces, in approximate sequence, with specific reference to (i) the post-3 February 2025 Code regime and Rule 2.4; (ii) wall-cross and hard / soft irrevocables; (iii) certain funds and SunGard-clean financing; (iv) scheme vs. contractual offer; (v) concert party and Rule 9; (vi) Rule 21 deal protection economics; (vii) MAC under Rule 13.5; (viii) antitrust covenant ↔ reverse termination fee.',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>(i) Post-3 February 2025 Code regime and Rule 2.4 (immediate response to the FT leak).</strong></p><ul><li>The target is a UK-registered company premium-listed on a UK regulated market — squarely within the post-RS 2024/1 narrowed jurisdiction. The Code applies; the prior "central management and control" residual debate is irrelevant here.</li><li>The 11% share-price jump combined with the FT piece naming the sponsor will almost certainly trigger a Panel direction (or self-imposed obligation under Rule 2.2 / 2.4) for a Rule 2.4 "possible offer" announcement before the market re-opens. The naming convention applies — the sponsor must be named.</li><li>Once the Rule 2.4 announcement is made and names the sponsor, the <strong>PUSU 28-day clock under Rule 2.6</strong> starts running. By 5pm on day 28, the sponsor must announce a Rule 2.7 firm intention or that it does not intend to bid (triggering a 6-month cooling-off under Rule 35). Extension only on target\'s request with Panel consent.</li><li>Strategic implication: the leak compresses an already-running due diligence into a 28-day window. If the sponsor is not "certain-funds-ready" within 28 days, it must walk away for six months. The deal team\'s first priority is therefore to accelerate financing and irrevocable undertakings.</li></ul><p><strong>(ii) Wall-cross and hard / soft irrevocables.</strong></p><ul><li>Pre-leak the sponsor would normally have run a controlled wall-cross of top institutional holders during weeks 4–6 of due diligence. Post-leak, this is much harder: any institution wall-crossed before announcement is now restricted under MAR.</li><li>The deal team should immediately approach the founder family and management to seek <strong>hard irrevocables</strong> (no fall-away on a competing bid) given clear alignment. Expect 22% founder-family + ~5–10% management = ~25–32% hard irrevocables — a strong signal of deal certainty.</li><li>Institutional irrevocables would normally be <strong>soft</strong> (8–10% uplift threshold, with the irrevocable falling away if a competing offer is announced at that uplift). Post-leak, soft irrevocables can still be sought via wall-cross, but timing is tight.</li><li>Concert-party watch: the founder family holding through trusts / connected nominees is presumptively a concert party under the Code\'s rebuttable presumptions; the deal team must confirm aggregation analysis carefully (see (v)).</li></ul><p><strong>(iii) Certain funds and SunGard-clean financing.</strong></p><ul><li>The Rule 2.7 firm-intention announcement must include a <strong>certain funds confirmation</strong> from the financial adviser. This requires substantially unconditional financing — significantly tighter than typical US "market-out" financing.</li><li>The TLB / second-lien commitment letters must therefore be <strong>certain-funds-clean</strong>: limited specified-event market-MAC; tight specified-representation regime; SunGard-style flow-down so that lender conditionality cannot exceed buyer conditionality; arranger flex tightly capped (no broad market-flex).</li><li>Personal exposure: the financial adviser bears personal liability if the certain-funds confirmation is mis-given. UK financial advisers will not give the certificate without bullet-proof commitments.</li><li>Equity backstop: the sponsor will need to provide an equity-commitment letter from an investment vehicle with sufficient committed-capital headroom; no "best efforts" equity raise is acceptable.</li></ul><p><strong>(iv) Scheme vs. contractual offer.</strong></p><ul><li>For a recommended deal of this size, a <strong>scheme of arrangement</strong> under Companies Act 2006 Part 26 is structurally preferable: 100% acquisition on a single approval (majority in number AND 75% in value at each class meeting, plus court sanction); avoids the 0.5% UK SDRT on share transfers (significant on £2.4bn equity = ~£12m saving); court-blessed certainty.</li><li>Tradeoffs: the scheme is target-controlled (the target convenes the meetings) and adds a court timetable. Hostile schemes are unworkable, so this is a recommended-deal-only structure.</li><li>If the deal becomes contested (e.g., a competing offer emerges during the PUSU window), the sponsor may need to switch to a contractual offer to retain control of the timetable.</li></ul><p><strong>(v) Concert party and Rule 9.</strong></p><ul><li>The 22% founder-family holding spread across trusts and connected nominees needs a careful concert-party analysis. The Code\'s rebuttable presumptions (company / 20%+ shareholders; family-and-trust connections) are likely engaged.</li><li>If the family is treated as a single concert party at 22%, no Rule 9 issue (below 30%). But adding management rollover and any sponsor-affiliated participation could push the post-deal aggregate concert party above 30% of the post-deal entity — though this is typically managed by structuring (e.g., the sponsor retains majority of voting rights without aggregating with management for Rule 9 purposes).</li><li>The deal team should obtain advance Panel guidance on the concert-party position before announcing.</li></ul><p><strong>(vi) Rule 21 deal protection economics post-3 February 2025.</strong></p><ul><li>Pre-3 February 2025: target-side break fees were near-categorically prohibited under the 2011 Rule 21.2 regime, with very narrow exceptions (e.g., a "white knight" exception for a target responding to a hostile bid).</li><li>Post-3 February 2025 (RS 2024/1 / Instrument 2024/3): a <strong>1% aggregate target-side break fee</strong> is permissible in Rule 21.1 transactions. On a £2.4bn deal, this enables a £24m break fee — modest but useful for deal certainty.</li><li>UK target-side deal protection economics remain substantially thinner than US (where 2.5–4% is standard). The sponsor should not expect matching rights or perpetual no-shops; the Code\'s policy is to keep the auction open.</li><li>The deal team should also consider the new "relevant period" concept defining Rule 21\'s temporal reach, the clarified ordinary-course carve-out, and the narrower "restricted action" definition — all relevant to target-board permitted actions during the offer period.</li></ul><p><strong>(vii) MAC invocation under Rule 13.5.</strong></p><ul><li>Public-deal MAC invocation requires Panel consent and only where the circumstance is of "material significance to the offeror in the context of the offer". The bar is exceptionally high — WPP / Tempus (2001), Brait / Iceland Foods (2014), and the Drax line illustrate that successful invocation is the rare exception.</li><li>For a US-trained sponsor familiar with Akorn-style MAC litigation, this is a significant calibration shift. The deal team should counsel the sponsor that the MAC is essentially unavailable absent dramatic material change beyond the existing offer.</li></ul><p><strong>(viii) Antitrust covenant ↔ reverse termination fee.</strong></p><ul><li>Antitrust risk on this deal: industrial-technology with potential overlap; UK CMA Phase 1 / Phase 2 risk; possibly EU merger control if EU revenue thresholds are met; HSR if US revenues are sufficient.</li><li>The buyer covenant on antitrust is correlated with the size of the reverse termination fee (RTF). Stronger buyer obligation (hell-or-high-water — committing to defined divestitures) → smaller RTF; weaker obligation (reasonable best efforts) → bigger RTF.</li><li>The Code\'s offer-related-arrangement rule (Rule 21.2) does not prohibit RTFs (paid by offeror to offeree) in the same way it prohibits target-side break fees. A 4–8% RTF on regulatory failure is achievable; the negotiation focus is on the antitrust covenant strength and the trigger for RTF payment.</li></ul><p><strong>Sequencing summary (28-day timetable):</strong></p><ol><li>Day 0 (FT leak): Rule 2.4 possible-offer announcement before market re-open; financial adviser engaged; Panel notified.</li><li>Days 1–7: founder-family and management hard irrevocables locked; certain-funds-clean commitment letters finalised; CMA Phase 1 strategy agreed; HSR filing prepared.</li><li>Days 7–14: institutional wall-cross under MAR; soft irrevocables sought; scheme vs. contractual offer decided; antitrust covenant / RTF negotiated.</li><li>Days 14–21: scheme-document drafting; fairness opinion obtained; Panel approval of structure; certain-funds confirmation finalised.</li><li>Days 21–28: Rule 2.7 firm-intention announcement; Panel and FCA filings; antitrust filings.</li></ol>'
  },
  {
    id: 'M18-02', module: '18', type: 'public-m&a-us-controlling-stockholder',
    title: 'US public-target merger — Time-Warner / QVC framing, MFW structuring, deal-protection trilogy under Omnicare, antitrust RTF',
    setup: 'A NYSE-listed US life-sciences company (~$8bn equity value; one major pipeline drug awaiting FDA Phase 3 readout; founder-controller family holds 35% through a family-controlled GP; remaining 65% institutional and public float) is in late-stage merger discussions with a strategic acquirer headquartered in Switzerland. The proposed deal: $90 cash + 0.5 acquirer shares (currently $40) per target share = ~$110 / share blended consideration; market price pre-rumour was $78. The controller would receive identical merger consideration as other shareholders (no rollover; no side deal; no continuing role). The target board has formed a special committee. The deal lawyer\'s associate asks: "Are we in MFW territory? Do we need Time-Warner cover? What\'s our deal-protection package, and what RTF are we proposing on antitrust risk?"',
    prompt: 'Diagnose the Delaware doctrinal posture: (i) is this a controlling-stockholder transaction triggering MFW analysis, and what would a fully-MFW-compliant structure look like (under Match Group)? (ii) is the deal in "Revlon mode" — apply Time-Warner / QVC; (iii) what deal-protection trilogy is appropriate, with specific reference to Omnicare cumulative-restrictiveness limits; (iv) what is the appraisal-arbitrage exposure post-Dell / DFC / Aruba; (v) what antitrust covenant and RTF size are appropriate?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>(i) Controlling-stockholder analysis and MFW structuring.</strong></p><ul><li>The 35% founder-controller family is a controlling stockholder under Delaware law (the threshold is functional control, not 50% — a 35% holding combined with a controlled GP is enough on these facts). However, the controller is receiving <em>identical</em> merger consideration as other shareholders, with no rollover, no side deal, no continuing role.</li><li>Under the Match Group Holding 1 (Del. 4 April 2024), MFW applies to ALL controlling-stockholder transactions in which the controller receives a NON-RATABLE benefit. On these facts, the controller does not receive a non-ratable benefit — the merger consideration is pro-rata. The threshold question is therefore: does this controlling-stockholder transaction trigger MFW analysis even without a non-ratable benefit?</li><li>The conservative position: implement MFW protections anyway, in case a court later identifies a non-ratable benefit (e.g., differential timing of cash receipt, post-merger employment arrangements, indemnification, control of the target\'s residual entities). MFW is a safe-harbour; the cost of building it ab initio is low.</li><li><strong>Full MFW-compliant structure (six prerequisites)</strong>: (1) special committee with EVERY member independent of the controller (Match Group Holding 2 — a single non-independent member contaminates the committee; deal team must scrutinise each member\'s historical relationships, financial ties, and dealings with the controller); (2) full authority delegated to the committee, including authority to say "no"; (3) free choice of legal and financial advisers (e.g., independent counsel and a financial adviser with no recent controller engagement); (4) sufficient information provided (projections, due diligence outputs, strategic alternatives analysis); (5) non-waivable majority-of-the-minority shareholder vote (excluding the controller and affiliates); (6) no coercion of unaffiliated stockholders. ALL six in place AB INITIO — before the controller engages on substantive economic terms.</li><li>Failure to satisfy any prerequisite restores entire-fairness review (Weinberger v. UOP), with the defendants bearing the burden to prove fair dealing and fair price — a much harder bar.</li></ul><p><strong>(ii) Time-Warner / QVC and Revlon mode.</strong></p><ul><li>The deal is part-cash, part-stock ($90 cash + 0.5 acquirer shares = ~$110 blended). Mixed consideration is the classic Revlon-trigger case under QVC if it results in shareholders losing the protection of a public-market fiduciary majority.</li><li>Apply QVC: are target shareholders losing the protection of a fiduciary public-market majority? They are receiving stock in the Swiss strategic acquirer. If the acquirer has a controlling stockholder (or a public-market fluid majority), the analysis differs.</li><li>If the acquirer has a fluid public-market majority (no controlling stockholder), target shareholders\' fiduciary protection is roughly preserved — Time-Warner cover may be available. The transaction can be defended as a strategic merger of equals (or near-equals) preserving fluid governance, not a sale of control.</li><li>If the acquirer has a controlling stockholder, Revlon clearly triggers — shareholders are losing fluid-majority protection and the board owes the auctioneer duty (best price reasonably available; market check; auction-style process).</li><li>Practitioner question to ask Swiss-acquirer counsel: "what\'s the post-deal control structure? Is there a controlling stockholder?"</li><li>Hybrid Time / QVC analysis: even if not strictly Revlon-mode, the special committee should run a market-check process to insulate the deal from Revlon attack — the cost is low and the litigation risk is real.</li></ul><p><strong>(iii) Deal-protection trilogy and Omnicare cumulative-restrictiveness.</strong></p><ul><li>Standard package for a US public-target merger: (1) no-shop with window-shop carve-out (target may respond to unsolicited bona fide written proposals reasonably likely to lead to a superior proposal, post-Phelps Dodge; (2) matching rights (3–5 business days; perpetual but each material change triggers a fresh notice period); (3) force-the-vote provision under DGCL §146 / §251(c) (required vote even if board recommendation changes); (4) break fee at 2.5–4% of equity value (here: ~$200–320m on $8bn); (5) voting agreements with the controller covering 35% of the voting power.</li><li>Omnicare cumulative-restrictiveness check: the package must not be PRECLUSIVE (foreclosing any other transaction) and COERCIVE (depriving stockholders of meaningful choice). On these facts: (a) 35% voting agreement is significant but not majority-control; (b) force-the-vote alone is permissible standing alone; (c) 3.5% break fee is mid-market; (d) matching rights with 4-day window are standard; (e) window-shop carve-out preserves the fiduciary out. The combined package looks defensible — distinguishable from Omnicare\'s 65% voting agreement combined with force-the-vote and absent fiduciary out.</li><li>Pitfall: if the controller\'s 35% voting agreement is irrevocable (no fall-away on superior proposal), the combined package is closer to Omnicare. Modern best practice: voting agreements terminate on a board recommendation change in favour of a superior proposal — preserving the fiduciary out\'s teeth.</li></ul><p><strong>(iv) Appraisal exposure post-Dell / DFC / Aruba.</strong></p><ul><li>DGCL §262 appraisal is available in any Delaware merger that meets the technical requirements. Post-Dell (2017) / DFC Global (2017) / Aruba (2019), the Court of Chancery now heavily weights deal price as fair value in arm\'s-length transactions with adequate process — appraisal arbitrage volume has fallen sharply.</li><li>Real risk on these facts: the controlling stockholder participation could push the deal into a pre-Dell-style appraisal posture — controlling-stockholder freeze-outs (especially absent MFW) are not protected by the Dell deal-price presumption to the same degree.</li><li>If MFW is fully implemented and the deal is approved by a clean MOM vote, appraisal exposure is materially mitigated — but not eliminated. Hedge funds may still file appraisal petitions seeking the Aruba deal-price-minus-synergies value plus statutory interest (5% over the Federal Reserve discount rate, compounded monthly).</li><li>Mitigation: (a) extended market check (go-shop or robust pre-signing process); (b) clear bid-protection package not so onerous as to deter interlopers; (c) adequate disclosure to defeat process-defect arguments; (d) MFW MOM-vote approval. Appraisal and MFW do different work — MFW gives BJR review on the merits (defending against breach-of-duty claims), appraisal is a price-only revaluation. Both pressure points require attention.</li></ul><p><strong>(v) Antitrust covenant and reverse termination fee.</strong></p><ul><li>Cross-border life-sciences M&A: HSR filing in the US; potential EU merger control filings if EU revenue thresholds met; UK CMA Phase 1 if UK revenues / share-of-supply test triggered; Swiss merger control; potentially other jurisdictions (China SAMR, Brazil CADE, etc.).</li><li>Pipeline-drug overlap is a typical antitrust pinch-point — divestitures of one of the parties\' competing assets may be required.</li><li>Buyer covenant negotiation: a "hell-or-high-water" obligation requires the buyer to commit to all required divestitures up to a defined dollar threshold; "reasonable best efforts" with carve-outs (no obligation to divest above defined revenue / EBITDA threshold) is buyer-friendly.</li><li>RTF correlation: stronger buyer covenant → smaller RTF; weaker buyer covenant → bigger RTF. Typical US RTF on antitrust failure: 4–8% of equity value (here: $320m–$640m); occasionally 10–15% on antitrust-risky deals (up to $1.2bn).</li><li>For these facts (pipeline-drug overlap as the principal antitrust risk): a "hell-or-high-water up to a defined divestiture threshold of $X revenue" plus a 5–6% RTF would be a typical landing zone. The negotiation should treat the antitrust covenant and RTF as a single connected package — "what\'s our hell-or-high-water and what\'s the RTF?".</li></ul><p><strong>Bottom line for the partner-meeting moment.</strong> The deal is a mixed-consideration controlling-stockholder transaction with material antitrust risk and a pipeline-drug binary. The structural answer is: MFW-compliant ab initio under Match Group; Time-Warner cover dependent on acquirer post-deal control structure (running market check regardless); standard deal-protection package sized below Omnicare\'s cumulative-restrictiveness threshold; appraisal exposure controlled via MFW MOM vote and process robustness; antitrust covenant calibrated against RTF in the 4–8% band. The user should be able to follow each of these axes in a meeting — and to ask the sharp question: "are we in Revlon mode or do we have Time-Warner cover, and either way is our protection package below the Omnicare line?"</p>'
  }
];

/* Module P1 (Drafting Decoder) scenarios fragment.
   Per-module partner-meeting scenarios P1-01..P1-04, each putting the user
   in a senior-associate seat reading a drafted clause word-by-word.
*/
window.__SCEN_P1 = [
  {
    id: 'P1-01', module: 'P1', type: 'drafting-decoder-warranty-package',
    title: 'Compliance-with-laws warranty drafting — buyer-side mark-up',
    setup: 'A US private-equity sponsor is the buyer in a UK-law SPA for a mid-market industrial-services target (~£250m enterprise value). The seller\'s first draft of the Compliance-with-Laws warranty reads: "<em>The Target and its directors, representatives and officials have always complied and comply with all applicable laws, as well as with all orders, authorizations, licenses and decrees issued by any competent authority with respect to the Target.</em>" The buyer-side senior associate is preparing a mark-up. The deal team\'s diligence has surfaced one minor disclosure (a planning-permission technicality at one site, value impact ~£200k) and one open question (a competition-law inquiry into the industry that has not named the target). General-warranty cap is 15% of equity value (£37.5m); basket is tipping at 0.5% (£1.25m); de minimis is £25k.',
    prompt: 'Walk through the buyer-side mark-up (i) phrase-by-phrase amendments to the warranty itself; (ii) qualifier strategy across the warranty package; (iii) the indemnification regime (knowledge scrape, materiality scrape, double scrape); (iv) the fair-disclosure vs specifically-disclosed standard for the Disclosure Letter; (v) how the diligence findings translate into specific drafting moves; (vi) the senior partner\'s likely directional input.',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>(i) Phrase-by-phrase amendments to the Compliance-with-Laws warranty.</strong></p><ul><li><em>"The Target and its directors, representatives and officials"</em> — the rep extends to individual directors and officers. The buyer is comfortable with this; it forecloses the seller from arguing that a director\'s personal non-compliance falls outside the rep. Keep as drafted.</li><li><em>"have always complied and comply"</em> — buyer mark-up: ACCEPT (this is buyer-friendly temporal scope; the seller will likely propose narrowing to "during the [3] years prior to the date hereof"; buyer should resist or accept narrowing only in exchange for compensating concessions on knowledge / materiality).</li><li><em>"with all applicable laws"</em> — buyer accepts the universality. The seller will likely add "in all material respects" — buyer\'s response: accept the materiality qualifier into the rep itself in exchange for a materiality scrape that strips it for indemnification purposes (so the rep is qualified for breach but unqualified for damages).</li><li><em>"as well as with all orders, authorizations, licenses and decrees issued by any competent authority"</em> — broad regulatory net. Acceptable; the seller may try to enumerate specific licenses or limit to "material" ones — buyer should resist categorical limits, accept materiality qualifier.</li></ul><p><strong>(ii) Qualifier strategy across the warranty package.</strong></p><ul><li>The buyer\'s opening posture should be: <strong>NO</strong> knowledge qualifier on Compliance-with-Laws (it is too broad and important a rep to be limited by what named individuals subjectively knew). The seller will fight hard for one. Compromise: accept "to the Seller\'s Knowledge" with a CONSTRUCTIVE-knowledge formulation ("after due and careful inquiry of the Target\'s senior management and review of the books and records of the Target Companies in the ordinary course") and a knowledge group of CEO + CFO + General Counsel + Chief Compliance Officer.</li><li>"In all material respects" — accept into the rep itself in exchange for a materiality scrape.</li><li>"Other than as fairly disclosed in the Disclosure Letter" — see (iv) below; this is the biggest disclosure-standard fight.</li></ul><p><strong>(iii) Indemnification regime — scrape strategy.</strong></p><ul><li>Push for a <strong>DOUBLE SCRAPE</strong>: strip both materiality and knowledge qualifiers from the rep schedule for indemnification purposes only. Standard form: <em>"For purposes of determining (i) whether any of the Sellers\' Representations and Warranties have been breached, and (ii) the amount of any Loss that is the subject of an indemnification claim under Clause 7, in each case any qualification as to materiality (including the words \'material\', \'in all material respects\', \'Material Adverse Effect\' or any similar qualifier) and any qualification as to knowledge (including the words \'to the Seller\'s Knowledge\', \'to the best knowledge of the Seller\' or any similar qualifier) contained in such Representations and Warranties shall be disregarded."</em></li><li>The CRITICAL CARVE-BACK: preserve MAE where it functions as a defined trigger of a closing condition (otherwise the bring-down condition becomes unqualified — unacceptable to seller).</li><li>Likely seller resistance: scrape limited to (ii) only (so qualifiers gate the breach test but not damages), or scrape only on the materiality side (knowledge qualifiers preserved). Buyer\'s fallback: accept materiality scrape only, fight harder for knowledge scrape on Compliance-with-Laws specifically.</li></ul><p><strong>(iv) Fair-disclosure vs specifically-disclosed standard.</strong></p><ul><li>The seller\'s opening will likely be a fair-disclosure standard: matters identifiable from the Disclosure Letter on a reasonable reading qualify as disclosed against any warranty. Whole letter is the universe.</li><li>The buyer\'s opening should be a specifically-disclosed standard: the Disclosure Letter must identify each matter against the specific warranty it disclaims. One-to-one matching. Seller cannot bury a disclosure deep in the letter and expect it to qualify a different warranty.</li><li>Compromise positions: (a) fair disclosure for general warranties, specific disclosure for fundamental reps; (b) fair disclosure but with the New Hearts v Cosmopolitan standard codified — "fair disclosure requires sufficient detail to identify the nature and scope of the matter disclosed"; (c) specific disclosure for indemnification carve-out, fair disclosure for breach-test purposes.</li></ul><p><strong>(v) Diligence findings &rarr; drafting moves.</strong></p><ul><li>The planning-permission technicality (£200k) — disclose specifically against the Compliance-with-Laws warranty in the Disclosure Letter. Below the de minimis (£25k) so it is not actionable individually anyway, but disclosure ensures clean carve-out and avoids any "fraud" or "wilful misconduct" argument later. Pre-closing remediation desirable.</li><li>The unnamed competition-law inquiry — the buyer should push for a SPECIFIC INDEMNITY (outside the cap and basket) covering any future enforcement action arising from the inquiry, with a long survival (5+ years). The seller will resist; compromise positions include (a) a capped specific indemnity (say, £10m); (b) a price reduction reflecting the risk; (c) acceptance into the cap/basket but with a longer survival on the antitrust warranty specifically.</li></ul><p><strong>(vi) Senior partner\'s likely directional input.</strong></p><ul><li>"<em>This rep is too broad — let\'s add a constructive-knowledge qualifier and a fair-disclosure standard, but with a double scrape on indemnification</em>." Translation: accept knowledge and materiality qualifiers into the rep schedule for the breach test; strip them entirely for damages calculation. Net: seller gets the rep schedule looking conventional; buyer gets the indemnification math unqualified.</li><li>"<em>Specific indemnity on the antitrust inquiry, outside the cap, basket and de minimis, with a five-year survival.</em>" Translation: the inquiry is a known unknown that should not be priced into the general-warranty regime.</li><li>"<em>The Permitted Encumbrances list is too thin</em>." Translation: walk through every item on Schedule [X] and ask what is disclosed; if a real impairment is hidden in there, raise it.</li></ul><p><strong>What the user should be able to follow in the meeting.</strong> When the partner says "<em>let\'s tighten the rep — knowledge qualifier with constructive knowledge and a fair-disclosure standard, but double scrape on indemnification</em>", the user should hear: (a) the rep itself is moving from buyer-friendly unqualified to seller-friendly qualified; (b) the indemnification regime is moving from balanced to buyer-friendly via the scrape; (c) the disclosure standard is moving from buyer-friendly specific to seller-friendly fair; (d) the net is a negotiated middle that preserves the rep\'s commercial bite for damages but allows the seller to disclose around it for breach. Each phrase points at specific drafting on the page.</p>'
  },
  {
    id: 'P1-02', module: 'P1', type: 'drafting-decoder-mac-clause',
    title: 'MAC/MAE drafting — buyer- and seller-side moves on a regulated-industry deal',
    setup: 'A European strategic acquirer is buying a US life-sciences target (~$1.2bn equity value) with a single key pipeline drug awaiting an FDA Phase 3 readout expected in the interim period (signing to closing: 6–9 months for antitrust and FDA-related clearances). The seller\'s draft MAC definition includes the standard carve-outs (economic conditions, war, pandemics, industry-wide changes, deal announcement, changes in Laws) and a disproportionate-impact carve-back, and includes the word "<em>prospects</em>" in the MAE\'s reach ("<em>any effect that is, or could reasonably be expected to be, material and adverse to the results and/or financial condition of the Target or its assets, operations, affairs, prospects, future financial performance</em>"). The buyer\'s lead has flagged: (i) Phase 3 binary risk — drug fails or succeeds, large value swing; (ii) FDA enforcement risk — non-pipeline regulatory action; (iii) rate-of-change in regulatory environment for orphan drugs (US Congress).',
    prompt: 'Walk through both sides\' drafting moves: (i) the buyer\'s posture on "prospects"; (ii) the pandemic carve-out and disproportionate-impact carve-back; (iii) the changes-in-laws carve-out and its specific relevance to regulated industries; (iv) whether to add a quantitative MAE threshold; (v) interaction between MAC and the Phase 3 binary; (vi) the senior partner\'s likely sequencing of priorities.',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>(i) The buyer\'s posture on "prospects".</strong></p><ul><li>The buyer wants "<em>prospects</em>" IN. With "prospects", a downward forecast revision (a major customer signalling non-renewal, a regulator signalling enforcement, a binary readout missing) can constitute a MAC even before P&amp;L impact materialises. Without it, the buyer must wait for actual results to deteriorate before walking — making the MAC nearly impossible to invoke for a forward-looking risk.</li><li>The seller will fight hard to delete "prospects". Standard seller posture: "<em>this is a backward-looking measure; we don\'t guarantee future performance</em>." Compromise: keep "prospects" but cap the look-forward to a defined period (e.g., "12 months") and require the prospects-based MAC to be supported by quantifiable evidence (a major contract terminating; a regulator initiating formal enforcement).</li><li>Strategic implication for this deal: the Phase 3 readout is the buyer\'s biggest concern. With "prospects" in, a negative readout (or even pre-readout signals from FDA) could ground a MAC walk. Without "prospects", the buyer is contractually locked unless and until the negative readout materialises.</li></ul><p><strong>(ii) Pandemic carve-out and disproportionate-impact carve-back.</strong></p><ul><li>Post-COVID, "pandemics, epidemics and public-health emergencies" is a near-universal carve-out. The buyer accepts this. The leverage is in the disproportionate-impact carve-back: even within the pandemic carve-out, the buyer can invoke MAC if the Target was hit "<em>materially disproportionately</em>" relative to peers in the industry.</li><li>The disproportionate-impact carve-back is the buyer\'s claw-back. Standard form: "<em>except in the case of clauses (i), (ii), (iii) and (v) above, to the extent it does not have a materially disproportionate effect on the Target relative to other comparable entities operating in the industry</em>." Note: the deal-announcement carve-out (clause iv) is conspicuously NOT in the carve-back — buyer cannot ever invoke deal-announcement effects.</li><li>Negotiation: how to define "materially disproportionate" (qualitative or quantitative threshold like "twice the industry average"); how to define "comparable entities" (peer group of regulated drug developers? broader pharma?); whether the carve-back applies to "changes in Laws" (clause v) — specifically relevant here given congressional regulatory turbulence on orphan drugs.</li></ul><p><strong>(iii) Changes-in-Laws carve-out and regulated industries.</strong></p><ul><li>For a regulated-industry target (pharma), the changes-in-Laws carve-out is particularly tricky. A regulatory regime change (orphan-drug pricing reform, accelerated-approval pathway changes) can wipe out the target\'s value.</li><li>Buyer counter-move: carve back regulatory-change risk for INDUSTRY-SPECIFIC changes only, leaving generic regulatory shifts on the buyer. Drafting: "<em>changes in Laws, except to the extent that such changes specifically and disproportionately affect the [pharmaceutical / orphan-drug] industry</em>." This rebalances the carve-out.</li><li>Alternative: a SPECIFIC INDEMNITY for any value diminution caused by orphan-drug pricing reform during the interim period — converts the MAC walk-away right into a damages claim outside the cap. Better for deal certainty (closing happens) but worse for the buyer if the value diminution exceeds the indemnity cap.</li></ul><p><strong>(iv) Quantitative MAE threshold.</strong></p><ul><li>Standard MAE definitions are qualitative ("material and adverse"). Some sophisticated drafting adds quantitative thresholds: e.g., "an effect that has caused or would reasonably be expected to cause a 30% reduction in the Target\'s annual EBITDA over a 12-month period" — gives certainty to the standard.</li><li>Quantitative thresholds are unusual but not unprecedented. They are buyer-unfriendly in the sense that they raise the bar (the buyer must hit a defined number). They are seller-unfriendly in the sense that they remove ambiguity (the seller cannot argue "well, it\'s not really material").</li><li>For this deal, a quantitative threshold could be useful for the orphan-drug-pricing-reform risk: "any change in Laws causing or reasonably expected to cause a [X]% reduction in the Target\'s pricing for [Drug Y]". But the parties might prefer to handle this via a specific indemnity instead (see (iii)).</li></ul><p><strong>(v) Interaction with the Phase 3 binary.</strong></p><ul><li>The Phase 3 readout is the deal\'s defining risk. Three structural approaches: (a) MAC walk on negative readout (buyer\'s preference) — broad MAC + "prospects" preserved; (b) PRICE adjustment based on readout (negotiated split) — readout outcome determines a contingent purchase-price installment; (c) WALK-AWAY RIGHT specific to readout — a CP that Phase 3 results have not "<em>negatively materially deviated from agreed clinical endpoints</em>" — converts the MAC question into a defined CP test.</li><li>Option (c) is cleanest from a litigation-risk perspective: the parties agree ex ante what counts as a negative readout, avoiding the post hoc MAC litigation that plagued Akorn-style cases.</li><li>The buyer should push for (a) or (c); the seller will push for none of the above (close the deal regardless, pricing the readout into the headline).</li></ul><p><strong>(vi) Senior partner\'s sequencing of priorities.</strong></p><ul><li>"<em>First, keep \'prospects\' in. Second, get the disproportionate-impact carve-back tightened — qualitative is fine but make sure \'comparable entities\' is well defined. Third, on Phase 3, push for a separate CP rather than relying on MAC. Fourth, on regulatory change, get a specific indemnity outside the cap covering orphan-drug pricing reform. Fifth, on changes in Laws, carve back industry-specific changes.</em>" That is the priority list.</li><li>Why this order: "prospects" is the highest-leverage word in the entire definition; the disproportionate-impact carve-back is the next most important; Phase 3 deserves its own structural treatment (MAC litigation is uncertain); regulatory change is best handled via indemnity; the Laws carve-out is a residual.</li></ul><p><strong>What the user should be able to follow.</strong> When the partner says "<em>keep \'prospects\' in, tighten the carve-back, separate CP for Phase 3, specific indemnity on orphan-drug pricing</em>", the user should hear five distinct drafting moves on the page, each calibrated to a specific risk in the deal.</p>'
  },
  {
    id: 'P1-03', module: 'P1', type: 'drafting-decoder-locked-box-leakage',
    title: 'Locked-box deal — Permitted Leakage and no-leakage covenant negotiation',
    setup: 'A European trade buyer is acquiring an Italian family-owned manufacturing target (~€80m enterprise value) under a locked-box pricing structure. Reference Date is 31 December 2025; signing is 15 March 2026; closing is expected ~30 June 2026 after antitrust clearance. The seller has historically extracted dividends (€2m annually), pays the founder-CEO a €600k salary plus performance bonus, and has been considering a one-off €5m dividend in early 2026 as part of pre-deal cash management. The seller has also engaged advisors (financial: a Big Four firm; legal: a top-tier Italian firm) — fees at signing-and-closing are estimated at €1.2m. The buyer\'s draft no-leakage covenant is in standard form (covenant + indemnity + no cap/basket/de minimis + 12-month claims period). The buyer has asked for a draft Permitted Leakage list.',
    prompt: 'Walk through (i) the buyer\'s leverage in the no-leakage architecture; (ii) Permitted Leakage drafting — what to include, what to exclude, with specific reference to the dividend, the founder-CEO compensation, advisor fees, and the proposed €5m one-off dividend; (iii) the ticker / interest-equivalent issue; (iv) the claims period; (v) the senior partner\'s likely position on each item.',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>(i) Buyer\'s leverage in the no-leakage architecture.</strong></p><ul><li>In a locked-box deal, the buyer\'s ENTIRE protection between Reference Date (31 Dec 2025) and Closing (30 Jun 2026) — six months of seller-controlled operations — is the no-leakage covenant. Without it, the seller could strip €X of cash, dividends, related-party payments, etc.</li><li>The covenant operates as: (a) the seller covenants no Leakage during the period; (b) the seller indemnifies on a full-indemnity basis any Leakage that does occur; (c) NO threshold, de minimis, basket or cap (every euro recoverable from euro one); (d) short claims period (typically 12&ndash;18 months); (e) characterised as Purchase Price reduction for tax purposes.</li><li>The buyer\'s leverage: any extraction not on the Permitted Leakage list is breach. So the FIGHT is the Permitted Leakage list — every line.</li></ul><p><strong>(ii) Permitted Leakage drafting.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>The €2m annual dividend pattern.</strong> The seller will ask for "ordinary-course dividends consistent with past practice" on the Permitted Leakage list. The buyer\'s response: <em>capped</em> at the historical level (€2m), <em>pro-rated</em> for the part-year (Jan&ndash;Jun 2026 = €1m), and <em>specifically scheduled</em> in the deal documents (so the seller cannot argue retroactively that the dividend was ordinary).</li><li><strong>The proposed €5m one-off dividend.</strong> This is the highest-leverage item. The seller will want it on Permitted Leakage; the buyer will resist, because the price is fixed at 31 Dec 2025 and pulling €5m post-Reference-Date directly impairs the buyer. Likely outcomes: (a) buyer rejects entirely &mdash; the seller does not get the dividend; (b) buyer accepts, but the headline price is reduced by €5m to compensate; (c) the seller does the dividend BEFORE the Reference Date (re-set the locked box to a later date) so the cash extraction is reflected in the price-fixing balance sheet. Senior associate should flag (c) as a structural alternative.</li><li><strong>The founder-CEO\'s compensation.</strong> "<em>Ordinary-course salary, bonus, benefits and pension contributions to officers and employees consistent with past practice</em>" is a standard Permitted Leakage item. Buyer accepts but: (a) caps the bonus at historical levels (no above-budget discretionary bonuses); (b) excludes any change-of-control payment, retention bonus or transaction-related compensation (those should be specifically disclosed as a separate Permitted Leakage line, with a defined cap).</li><li><strong>Advisor fees (€1.2m).</strong> Two structural choices. (a) Permitted Leakage line: seller and target may bear up to €X of advisor fees. (b) Specific exclusion: advisor fees are NOT a target obligation; the seller pays out of its own pocket (so the target\'s value is preserved). Modern practice for buyer-friendly drafting is (b) &mdash; seller absorbs fees personally. If accepted on (a), cap tightly at the budgeted amount; any excess is leakage.</li><li><strong>Other standard inclusions:</strong> intra-group payments at arm\'s-length pricing in the ordinary course; ordinary-course tax payments; statutory employee benefits required by law.</li></ul><p><strong>(iii) Ticker / interest-equivalent.</strong></p><ul><li>In a locked-box deal, the seller is "running the business for the buyer\'s account" between Reference Date and Closing. Standard practice: a "<strong>ticker</strong>" or "interest-equivalent" payment compensates the seller for the time value of money on the locked-box equity value during the gap.</li><li>Calculation: typically a defined annual rate (e.g. 5&ndash;7%) on the locked-box equity value, accruing daily, paid at closing as part of the purchase price.</li><li>The ticker is NOT leakage &mdash; it is a price component. But it should be carefully drafted to ensure the seller cannot double-extract via dividends + ticker (i.e. the daily ticker rate compensates the seller for not extracting cash; if the seller does extract cash via permitted dividend, the buyer should arguably get a credit).</li><li>Negotiation: rate level (low end 4%, high end 7%); whether the rate is fixed or floating; whether it accrues simple or compound; whether it stops on a buyer-side delay (so the seller cannot benefit from sitting on a slow regulatory clearance).</li></ul><p><strong>(iv) Claims period.</strong></p><ul><li>Standard 12 months from Effective Date (Closing). Some deals stretch to 18 or even 24 months, especially where the buyer needs an audit cycle to discover hidden leakage.</li><li>Recommended for this deal: 12&ndash;18 months. The target is family-owned with limited audit history &mdash; buyer should err on longer side to allow first post-closing audit to surface any hidden related-party payments.</li><li>The claims period is short for a reason: locked-box leakage is an immediate, identifiable cash-extraction issue; it should be discoverable quickly. Long claims periods make the seller liable indefinitely, which conflicts with the locked-box\'s "clean exit" promise.</li></ul><p><strong>(v) Senior partner\'s likely position.</strong></p><ul><li>"<em>The €5m dividend is not on Permitted Leakage. Either the seller skips it, or we re-set the locked-box date to capture it. Either way, the buyer doesn\'t pay for cash that\'s being extracted post-Reference-Date.</em>"</li><li>"<em>Founder-CEO comp is fine at historical levels. Cap discretionary bonuses. Any retention/transaction bonus is a separate disclosed line.</em>"</li><li>"<em>Advisor fees: seller pays out-of-pocket. They\'re not the target\'s burden.</em>"</li><li>"<em>Ticker: 5% per annum, simple, on the locked-box equity value, accruing daily, payable at closing. Stops if buyer-side delay.</em>"</li><li>"<em>Claims period: 18 months. Family company, limited audit history, we want one full cycle plus a margin.</em>"</li></ul><p><strong>What the user should hear in the meeting.</strong> Each line of the Permitted Leakage schedule is a euro of price &mdash; either a euro the buyer is paying for (so the seller can extract it without breaching) or a euro the buyer is keeping (so any extraction is leakage and recoverable). The ticker is a separate price component compensating the seller for the gap; it is not leakage. The claims period is the buyer\'s discovery window. Read every line of the schedule. The €5m one-off dividend is the headline negotiation; do not let it sit ambiguously on the page.</p>'
  },
  {
    id: 'P1-04', module: 'P1', type: 'drafting-decoder-rtf-limited-guarantee',
    title: 'Reverse termination fee, limited guarantee and exclusive remedy in a PE deal — sponsor protection',
    setup: 'A US PE sponsor is the buyer in a $2.5bn cross-border take-private (US target, UK and EU operations). The deal has substantial regulatory risk (HSR, EU Merger Regulation, UK CMA Phase 1, Chinese SAMR; the parties have estimated 8–12 month timeline). The financing comprises a $1.5bn TLB, $0.5bn second lien, $0.4bn revolver and $0.6bn sponsor equity. The seller proposes: (i) RTF of 8% of equity value ($200m); (ii) Limited Guarantee Cap = RTF + reimbursable expenses up to $20m; (iii) RTF triggers on Buyer regulatory failure OR Buyer financing failure; (iv) seller retains specific performance as an alternative remedy at its option. The sponsor has asked the deal team for a counter-position.',
    prompt: 'Walk through the sponsor-side counter-position: (i) RTF size; (ii) the trigger events (regulatory only, financing, both); (iii) the Limited Guarantee Cap composition; (iv) the no-recourse-beyond-cap language and protected-persons list; (v) specific performance vs RTF (mutually exclusive or parallel); (vi) the senior partner\'s likely framing of the trade-offs.',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>(i) RTF size.</strong></p><ul><li>8% on a $2.5bn deal is $200m. Market range for regulatory-risky cross-border deals: 6&ndash;10%. 8% is within range but at the high end; sponsor counter-position is 5&ndash;6% ($125&ndash;150m).</li><li>The RTF is the seller\'s economic protection against the buyer walking; the higher it is, the more deal certainty the seller perceives, but also the more sponsor risk. The negotiation is calibrated against the regulatory risk &mdash; here, four jurisdictions with material risk (especially EU under the foreign-subsidies regulation; SAMR for any China-touching deal).</li><li>Sponsor opening: 5%, with a comprehensive "hell-or-high-water" antitrust covenant up to a defined divestiture threshold. The HHW commitment substitutes for some of the RTF protection &mdash; if the buyer is committing to take all-and-any actions, the seller doesn\'t need as large a financial backstop.</li><li>Likely landing zone: 6&ndash;7% with a moderately strong (but not pure HHW) antitrust covenant.</li></ul><p><strong>(ii) Trigger events.</strong></p><ul><li>The seller proposes triggers on (a) Buyer regulatory failure; (b) Buyer financing failure. The sponsor must FIRMLY resist (b) financing failure as a trigger. Reasons:</li><li>The sponsor will provide an equity-commitment letter and the lenders will provide debt commitments. Financing failure post-signing is rare and typically means a market dislocation or lender default. Putting financing failure on the RTF trigger means the sponsor pays $200m if the credit markets seize up &mdash; an exposure the sponsor does not want.</li><li>Sponsor counter: trigger ONLY on (a) Buyer regulatory failure (or, more narrowly, where the only outstanding CPs at Long-Stop Date are regulatory). Financing failure is handled separately via the financing commitment letters and the standard "specific performance with respect to the equity contribution" mechanic.</li><li>Other trigger considerations: (c) Buyer breach of conduct-of-business covenants causing closing failure &mdash; sponsor accepts this; (d) Buyer voluntary termination &mdash; sponsor accepts this with a higher penalty (often the same as regulatory RTF or sometimes higher); (e) Long-Stop Date expiration without specified outstanding CPs &mdash; sponsor wants this scoped narrowly.</li></ul><p><strong>(iii) Limited Guarantee Cap composition.</strong></p><ul><li>Standard composition: RTF amount + reimbursable expenses up to a defined sub-cap.</li><li>The seller proposes RTF + $20m. Sponsor counter: $5&ndash;10m on expenses (more typical for a deal of this size); cap reimbursable expenses to actual out-of-pocket costs incurred by the seller, with documentation.</li><li>"Reimbursable expenses" definition: legal, financial-advisor, regulatory-filing fees actually incurred. EXCLUDES: costs of the target\'s ordinary-course operations; opportunity costs; loss of bargain (those should not creep in via expenses).</li><li>Recommend explicit drafting: "<em>Reimbursable Expenses means out-of-pocket fees and expenses actually incurred by the Seller in connection with the Transaction, including (i) reasonable legal fees, (ii) reasonable financial advisor fees, and (iii) reasonable regulatory filing fees, in each case supported by reasonable documentation, up to a cap of $[5,000,000] in aggregate.</em>"</li></ul><p><strong>(iv) No-recourse-beyond-cap and protected-persons list.</strong></p><ul><li>The Limited Guarantee should expressly state: "<em>beyond the Limited Guarantee Cap, neither [Sponsor] nor any of its Affiliates, including any portfolio company, fund, partner, member, shareholder, director, officer or employee, shall have any liability whatsoever in connection with this Agreement or the transactions contemplated hereby.</em>"</li><li>The PROTECTED-PERSONS list in the SPA\'s exclusive-remedy clause should mirror this: "<em>The Reverse Termination Fee shall constitute the Seller\'s sole and exclusive remedy against the Buyer and any of its Affiliates, including the Sponsor and its Affiliates, any financing source, any equity investor, and any director, officer, employee or representative of any of the foregoing, in respect of [defined trigger events].</em>"</li><li>Each name on the list is a bullet dodged. Without "Affiliates", the seller could go after a portfolio company; without "financing source", the seller could sue the lenders for tortious interference; without "equity investor", the seller could sue the LPs.</li></ul><p><strong>(v) Specific performance vs RTF — mutually exclusive or parallel.</strong></p><ul><li>The seller proposes retaining specific performance as an alternative remedy at its option. The sponsor must firmly resist this. Two reasons:</li><li>(a) The whole point of the RTF is to substitute monetary certainty for specific performance. If the seller can choose either, the sponsor faces two cumulative risks: pay the RTF AND face an SP order forcing a deal it doesn\'t want.</li><li>(b) Specific performance against a sponsor / fund is a complex remedy that may push capital flows the sponsor cannot accommodate (e.g., calling capital from LPs to fund a deal the sponsor has decided to walk from).</li><li>Sponsor position: SP is available ONLY for the equity contribution mechanic (so seller can compel the sponsor to fund the equity if the only outstanding issue is the equity flow), AND only if the seller waives the RTF. Mutually exclusive: seller picks one path or the other.</li><li>Standard drafting: "<em>The Seller\'s sole and exclusive remedies in respect of [trigger events] shall be either (a) specific performance pursuant to Clause [X] (limited to the Buyer\'s obligation to fund the Equity Contribution) OR (b) payment of the Reverse Termination Fee pursuant to this Clause, but not both. The Seller shall make an irrevocable election within [10] Business Days of [trigger event] failing which the Reverse Termination Fee shall apply.</em>"</li></ul><p><strong>(vi) Senior partner\'s likely framing of trade-offs.</strong></p><ul><li>"<em>RTF at 6%, regulatory only as trigger. Limited Guarantee Cap at RTF + $5m expenses, hard cap. No-recourse-beyond-cap to all sponsor affiliates, financing sources, equity investors. Specific performance only on the equity, mutually exclusive with RTF.</em>"</li><li>The trade-off is: more aggressive sponsor protections require either (a) a stronger antitrust covenant (full HHW vs partial), or (b) acceptance that the seller has weaker downside protection. The sponsor\'s leverage is its alternative to do the deal &mdash; if it\'s competitive auction, sponsor has less leverage; if a one-bidder situation, sponsor has more leverage.</li><li>The user should be able to follow the exchange when the partner says "<em>regulatory-only RTF at 6%, hard cap on the limited guarantee, mutually-exclusive SP&mdash;not parallel</em>" and recognise three distinct drafting moves: (a) trigger scope; (b) cap composition; (c) remedy interaction.</li></ul><p><strong>What the user should hear in the meeting.</strong> The RTF + Limited Guarantee architecture is a single negotiated package. The sponsor cares about: protecting the rest of the fund (no-recourse-beyond-cap), capping the maximum exposure (RTF + small expense cap), narrowing the trigger (regulatory-only), and converting the seller\'s remedy menu into a single binding choice (mutually-exclusive SP or RTF). The seller cares about: economic protection if the deal fails (high RTF), broad triggers (so financing or other failures also pay), parallel remedies (so SP is also available). The negotiation is the calibration between these two views, with the antitrust covenant strength as the secondary lever.</p>'
  }
];

/* Per-module scenarios fragment for Module P2 — Industry Deep-Dives (Financial Services & Tech).
 * Practitioner-complement module. IDs P2-01..P2-04. Calibrated for partner-meeting fluency:
 * UK PRA controller change-of-control + US BHC Act/Bank Merger Act with Riegle-Neal +
 * Solvency II / Form A multi-state insurance + AGPL SaaS loophole + AI Act Annex III + Schrems II / DPF
 * + post-2022 antitrust pattern (Microsoft/Activision; Adobe/Figma; Meta/Giphy; Amazon/iRobot; Booking/eTraveli).
 */
window.__SCEN_P2 = [
  {
    id: 'P2-01', module: 'P2',
    title: 'UK retail bank acquisition — sponsor PE bidder; PRA Section 178 timetable',
    setup: '<p>A US private equity sponsor (Ardent Capital) is bidding for an 80% holding in NorthBank, a UK retail bank with &pound;45bn of total assets, defined-benefit pension scheme with a &pound;180m IFRS deficit, and an open FCA conduct enforcement matter relating to historic mis-selling. The sponsor has a complex LP stack including two sovereign-wealth funds and several family offices. The deal is announced on 10 March 2026; the parties want a 9-month longstop.</p>',
    prompt: 'Walk through the regulatory CP architecture, the principal red flags, and the realistic longstop calibration. What specific carve-outs would you negotiate on the regulatory effort standard?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Regulatory CP architecture.</strong></p><ol><li><strong>PRA / FCA Section 178 controller-change.</strong> Ardent crosses the 50% band &mdash; a single notification covering the largest band crossing. <strong>Decision-maker:</strong> PRA, assessed jointly with the FCA (NorthBank is dual-regulated). <strong>Statutory clock:</strong> 60 working days from acknowledgement of completeness, with one 30-working-day pause &mdash; effectively 90 working days realistic, ~5 calendar months including pre-application dialogue.</li><li><strong>UK CMA antitrust.</strong> UK voluntary regime under Enterprise Act 2002; share-of-supply test under section 23 (25%+ in UK or substantial part). For NorthBank as a regional UK retail bank, share-of-supply is unlikely to be triggered unless Ardent already owns a UK bank &mdash; check the portfolio.</li><li><strong>UK National Security and Investment Act 2021.</strong> Banking is largely <em>not</em> a notifiable sector under the NSI 17 sectors, but check the data-infrastructure carve-out and any critical-infrastructure designation. Voluntary notification possible if uncertainty.</li><li><strong>FDI in source LP jurisdictions</strong> &mdash; not a UK CP but a structuring constraint on Ardent&rsquo;s LP stack disclosure.</li></ol><p><strong>Principal red flags.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pension deficit (&pound;180m).</strong> The PRA / FCA controller assessment will likely be conditioned on a pension-covenant strengthening commitment from Ardent &mdash; typically a Type B or Type C contingent-asset arrangement under the UK Pensions Regulator&rsquo;s clearance regime (parent guarantee or escrow account). The buyer&rsquo;s submission must commit to specifics. Expect a separate pre-announcement engagement with the Pensions Regulator and a clearance application running parallel.</li><li><strong>Open FCA conduct enforcement.</strong> The fifth FSMA controller test (suspicion of money laundering / terrorist financing) is the regulator&rsquo;s discretionary lever; an open enforcement matter automatically tightens the controller assessment. Expect specific corrective-action commitments (root-cause analysis; remediation programme; possibly a Skilled Person review under FSMA s.166).</li><li><strong>LP-stack opacity.</strong> Two sovereign-wealth funds + family offices means deep beneficial-ownership review. The PRA will expect Ardent to deliver a complete ultimate-beneficial-ownership map, AML-history confirmations on all named individuals, and financial-soundness commitments from the sponsor&rsquo;s management entity.</li><li><strong>Resolvability.</strong> NorthBank may be a D-SIB; the post-acquisition group must remain resolvable. Bank of England Resolution Directorate engagement is part of the controller-change submission.</li><li><strong>Senior-managers fitness.</strong> Ardent&rsquo;s representative on the NorthBank board will need SMF7 approval (controller-controller function) post-Phase-1 SMCR reforms (April 2026). Phase 1 narrows SMF7 application for minority NEDs &mdash; but Ardent at 80% is clearly significant influence, so SMF7 applies.</li></ul><p><strong>Realistic longstop calibration.</strong> 9 months is tight. Realistic timeline: <strong>5 months on PRA / FCA</strong> (90 working days including pause + pre-application dialogue + integration-plan finalisation); <strong>parallel 8&ndash;12 weeks on Pensions Regulator clearance</strong>; <strong>parallel 4&ndash;8 weeks on CMA review</strong> (if triggered); <strong>buffer for integration-plan iteration</strong>. <strong>Recommend: 12-month longstop</strong> with a 6-month longstop-extension right exercisable by either party if the PRA / FCA process is "reasonably likely to be satisfied within X further days" (standard wording). 9 months is feasible only if the parties have pre-engaged the PRA before announcement and have an agreed pension-covenant package.</p><p><strong>Regulatory-CP effort standard &mdash; specific carve-outs.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>"Reasonable best efforts" rather than hell-or-high-water</strong>, because the regulator&rsquo;s primary lever is not divestiture; it is capital-injection and governance commitments that re-shape balance sheet and governance.</li><li><strong>Specific carve-out on capital injection:</strong> Ardent commits to inject up to &pound;X (cap) of additional CET1 capital if the PRA conditions approval on it &mdash; above &pound;X, Ardent has the right to walk and pay the reverse-termination fee.</li><li><strong>Specific carve-out on pension covenant:</strong> Ardent commits to a parent guarantee of up to &pound;Y (cap) plus an escrow account of up to &pound;Z; above those levels, Ardent has the right to walk.</li><li><strong>Specific carve-out on governance:</strong> Ardent commits to specific board-composition arrangements (chairman; SID; risk-committee chair) and AML / financial-crime systems commitments.</li><li><strong>Specific carve-out on remediation:</strong> Ardent commits to fund up to &pound;A of remediation programme costs for the open FCA enforcement matter.</li><li><strong>Buyer-side regulatory MAC carve-out:</strong> a "regulator imposes conditions outside the carve-out caps above" trigger that lets Ardent walk without paying the reverse-termination fee.</li><li><strong>Reverse-termination fee:</strong> 1.5&ndash;3% of equity value if Ardent fails to obtain regulator approval not attributable to seller breach; structured as the buyer&rsquo;s exclusive remedy except for fraud or wilful breach.</li></ul><p><strong>Partner-meeting summary:</strong> "PRA Section 178, 90 working days realistic plus 4&ndash;6 weeks pre-application; pension covenant via Type B contingent asset, capped at &pound;Y guarantee plus &pound;Z escrow; FCA enforcement remediation funded up to &pound;A; SMF7 for the controller representative; reasonable best efforts effort standard with specific commitment caps and a regulator-condition MAC carve-out; 12-month longstop with 6-month extension; 2.5% reverse-termination fee."</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'P2-02', module: 'P2',
    title: 'US tech target — AI scoring model in EU credit market; Schrems II + AI Act + NYT v OpenAI overhang',
    setup: '<p>A UK PE buyer (Wessex Capital) is acquiring CredAI, a Delaware-incorporated AI software business that licenses an AI-driven credit-scoring model to consumer lenders in the UK, France, Germany and the Netherlands. CredAI&rsquo;s training data was scraped from public web sources without express licences from publishers; it has not been sued, but two related AI providers (one in the US, one in the UK) have received cease-and-desist notices in the prior 12 months. CredAI&rsquo;s US parent transfers personal data of 350,000 EU borrowers to a US data-processing vendor that is not DPF-certified. The deal is signing in mid-2026.</p>',
    prompt: 'Identify the principal regulatory and IP risks, and design the SPA warranty / indemnity package and the W&I architecture to handle them.',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Principal regulatory and IP risks.</strong></p><ol><li><strong>EU AI Act Annex III high-risk classification.</strong> CredAI&rsquo;s product falls in Annex III category 5 (essential public and private services, including credit scoring). <strong>Compliance deadline: 2 August 2026.</strong> Obligations: conformity assessment; registration in the EU AI database; risk-management system; data governance (training, validation and testing data must be relevant, representative, free of errors, and complete); technical documentation; record-keeping; transparency to deployers; human oversight; accuracy, robustness and cybersecurity; quality-management system; post-market monitoring. <strong>Penalties from 2 August 2025:</strong> up to &euro;15m or 3% of worldwide annual turnover for high-risk obligations. <strong>The buyer&rsquo;s integration plan must build to August 2026 conformity assessment.</strong></li><li><strong>Training-data provenance.</strong> Web-scraped data without express licences from publishers exposes CredAI to: (a) US copyright-infringement risk on the <em>NYT v OpenAI</em>-style fair-use defence (17 USC &sect;107; four factors; doctrine unsettled); (b) EU TDM exception risk &mdash; Article 4 EU Copyright Directive 2019/790 permits text-and-data-mining subject to lawful access and to a rights-holder opt-out (typically expressed via robots.txt); the buyer must verify that opt-outs were respected at training time. Cease-and-desist notices to two related providers signal market-level escalation; possible litigation cluster. <strong>The risk is unquantified but real.</strong></li><li><strong>Cross-border data transfers Schrems II / DPF.</strong> The US data-processing vendor is not DPF-certified; the transfer therefore relies on SCCs plus a documented TIA covering FISA s.702 and EO 12333 access regimes, with supplementary technical (encryption, pseudonymisation) and contractual (warrant-canary, transparency, government-access notification) measures. UK transfers also need a TRA. <strong>Verify the DPIA and TIA documentation in DD; if missing or stale, this is a remediation milestone.</strong></li><li><strong>GDPR / UK GDPR compliance generally.</strong> Article 30 RoPA must be complete and accurate. Article 22 GDPR (automated decision-making) applies to credit-scoring; data subjects have the right to object and to obtain human review &mdash; integration plan must support this in CredAI&rsquo;s product.</li><li><strong>UK CMA share-of-supply analysis.</strong> CredAI&rsquo;s UK customer base may not trigger 25% share of supply, but post-Brexit the CMA has flexibility; assess the AI-credit-scoring market for the UK specifically.</li></ol><p><strong>SPA warranty / indemnity package.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>IP warranty package.</strong> Title to all material IP; chain of title from founders and contractors; "hereby assigns" language in PIIA; pre-existing-inventions schedules complete; confirmatory assignments at signing for any gaps.</li><li><strong>AI-specific warranty package.</strong> Training data was obtained lawfully (no scraping in breach of website terms; rights-holder opt-outs respected at training time); for Annex III high-risk AI, conformity-assessment path established and documented; no AI Act prohibited-use systems deployed; no outstanding copyright-infringement claim or notice in respect of any training dataset.</li><li><strong>Data warranty package.</strong> Article 30 RoPA complete and accurate; cross-border transfers conducted under SCCs + TIA, DPF, BCRs, or another lawful mechanism; no Privacy Shield reliance; no personal-data breach within prior X years not remediated and reported; no outstanding ICO / DPC enforcement; data-room itself complies with personal-data protection requirements.</li><li><strong>Specific indemnity for training-data provenance gaps.</strong> Outside the cap; survival 7 years; tipping basket from dollar one. Covers: copyright-infringement claims relating to training data; DMCA &sect;1202 takedown / mis-attribution claims; any settlement or enforcement matter arising from the cease-and-desist cluster.</li><li><strong>Specific indemnity for AI Act conformity-assessment gap.</strong> Outside the cap; survival to 2 August 2026 + 3 years; covers any regulatory penalty if conformity assessment is not in place by 2 August 2026.</li><li><strong>Specific indemnity for Schrems II / DPF non-compliance.</strong> Outside the cap; survival 5 years; covers ICO / DPC fines for unlawful transfer.</li></ul><p><strong>W&amp;I architecture.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Buy-side W&amp;I policy</strong> with policy limit 10&ndash;15% of EV. <strong>Specific exclusions:</strong> (i) all training-data-provenance matters and any copyright-litigation claims (insurer will exclude); (ii) AI Act conformity-assessment compliance gaps known at bind; (iii) GDPR fines (public-policy non-insurability).</li><li><strong>Cyber sublimit at 10&ndash;25% of policy limit.</strong></li><li><strong>AI / model-liability sublimit</strong> &mdash; rapidly evolving; underwriters offering sublimits in 2025&ndash;2026 specifically for AI-product-liability and model-output liability matters.</li><li><strong>Possible specific contingent-risk insurance</strong> for the training-data-provenance exposure if the underwriter will write it &mdash; likely premium-heavy at 5&ndash;10% of policy limit per annum given the unsettled state of fair-use doctrine.</li><li><strong>Seller-backed escrow</strong> of 5&ndash;10% of EV for 24 months to fund the specific indemnities above the W&amp;I; parent guarantee from Wessex&rsquo;s management entity for the balance.</li></ul><p><strong>Partner-meeting summary:</strong> "Annex III credit scoring; conformity-assessment by August 2026; training-data scrapes generated cease-and-desist notices to peers, so material risk in fair-use posture &mdash; specific indemnity outside cap, with seller-backed escrow plus parent guarantee; non-DPF US vendor remediation milestone; W&amp;I excludes training-data and AI Act gaps; cyber sublimit at 15%; specific contingent-risk insurance for training-data only if underwriter writes at acceptable RoL."</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'P2-03', module: 'P2',
    title: 'Multi-state US insurance acquisition — life insurer with annuity back-book and matching adjustment',
    setup: '<p>An EU strategic insurer (Aurora Versicherung AG, a German group) is acquiring AmericasLife, a Connecticut-domiciled life insurer with subsidiaries domiciled in New York, Florida, Texas, and California. AmericasLife has a $40bn annuity back-book with assets cash-flow-matched under a portfolio that would qualify for a Solvency II Matching Adjustment in the EU. The Aurora group is supervised by BaFin and the ECB as a Significant Institution under the SSM (insurance group thresholds). The deal is signing on 1 May 2026; longstop preferred 9 months.</p>',
    prompt: 'Walk through the regulatory CP architecture and the longstop realism. What is the integration plan for the annuity back-book under EU regulatory rules?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Regulatory CP architecture.</strong></p><ol><li><strong>US state Form A filings in each state of domicile</strong> &mdash; Connecticut (lead state for AmericasLife as the parent holding company is domiciled there), New York, Florida, Texas, California. Filed under each state&rsquo;s adoption of the NAIC Insurance Holding Company System Regulatory Act. <strong>Contents:</strong> identity and biographical affidavits; pre-acquisition financials; post-acquisition organisational chart; 5-year business plan; sources and amount of consideration. <strong>Per-state clock:</strong> 60 days from completeness; deficiency letters reset; California and New York are the slowest reviewers historically. <strong>Multi-state total elapsed time: 6&ndash;12 months</strong> realistic.</li><li><strong>NAIC Form E pre-acquisition market-share notification</strong> &mdash; in any state where the combined entity would exceed market-share thresholds.</li><li><strong>EU side &mdash; ECB qualifying-holding procedure for Aurora&rsquo;s incremental holding.</strong> Even though Aurora is acquiring a US insurer, if Aurora&rsquo;s own SSM significance is affected (e.g., the acquisition pushes consolidated assets through the &euro;30bn threshold or changes group structure), a notification under SSM Article 15 may apply. Verify with BaFin and the ECB JST early.</li><li><strong>BaFin notification under VAG (Insurance Supervision Act)</strong> for the German parent &mdash; cross-border insurance-group changes notified to BaFin.</li><li><strong>HSR antitrust filing</strong> under Hart-Scott-Rodino if size thresholds met (2025 base size-of-transaction $126.4m).</li><li><strong>CFIUS</strong> &mdash; insurance with US person sensitive personal-data exposure could fall in TID US business; assess and consider voluntary notification given Aurora&rsquo;s German parentage and possible Russian-or-Chinese-government-LP scrutiny in the Aurora ownership chain.</li><li><strong>Bring-down warranties at closing</strong> on (a) RBC ratios across the US subsidiaries; (b) absence of new regulatory enforcement; (c) MAC.</li></ol><p><strong>Longstop realism.</strong> 9 months is tight. <strong>Realistic timeline:</strong> Form A filings 6&ndash;12 months realistic; HSR 30 days standard, possibly second request +30 days; CFIUS 45-day Phase I + possible 45-day Phase II; ECB notification (if triggered) 60 working days. <strong>Recommend: 12&ndash;15 month longstop</strong> with a 6-month extension right. The binding constraint is California Form A or New York Form A &mdash; whichever is slowest.</p><p><strong>Annuity back-book integration plan under EU rules.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Solvency II Matching Adjustment in the EU.</strong> Aurora may want to bring the AmericasLife annuity back-book onto the EU group balance sheet to benefit from MA at the EU level. Two routes: (a) <strong>direct portfolio transfer</strong> from US to an EU subsidiary &mdash; not commonly done given regulatory complexity; (b) <strong>reinsurance into an EU subsidiary</strong> &mdash; the more usual route, where the AmericasLife liabilities are reinsured to an Aurora EU subsidiary that holds the matching assets and benefits from MA.</li><li><strong>EU Solvency II eligibility.</strong> EU Solvency II MA has narrower eligibility than UK Solvency UK MA (post 30 June 2024 PS10/24 reform) &mdash; UK now permits "highly predictable" cashflow assets up to 10% of MA benefit, EU still narrower. If Aurora wants UK Solvency UK MA treatment, it should consider routing through a UK subsidiary instead of a continental EU subsidiary.</li><li><strong>EU portfolio-transfer mechanism.</strong> If a portfolio is to be transferred between EU member-state-domiciled subsidiaries, each member state has its own statutory mechanism &mdash; France <em>portefeuille transfer</em> under L.324-1 Code des assurances; Germany <em>Bestandsübertragung</em> under &sect;13 VAG; Italy Article 198 Codice. All require regulatory approval and most require policyholder notification. Cross-border EU portfolio transfers under Solvency II Article 39 add home-state / host-state coordination.</li><li><strong>SCR coverage post-integration.</strong> Aurora must demonstrate to BaFin and the ECB that post-integration SCR coverage at the group level remains above the targeted operating level (typically 150&ndash;180% for a life group). The annuity back-book&rsquo;s SCR depends on interest-rate and longevity stresses; Aurora&rsquo;s MA application can offset.</li><li><strong>US-side approval.</strong> Any portfolio transfer or reinsurance arrangement that materially changes the AmericasLife risk profile may itself require Form D filings (prior-notice transactions between affiliates) in each state.</li></ul><p><strong>Partner-meeting summary:</strong> "Multi-state Form A 6&ndash;12 months realistic with CT lead state, CA / NY binding constraint; 12&ndash;15 month longstop with 6-month extension; HSR + CFIUS in parallel; ECB notification on Aurora group consolidated impact; annuity back-book reinsured to an EU or UK subsidiary post-closing for MA benefit, with Solvency UK preferred over EU Solvency II for broader MA eligibility post 2024 reform; SCR coverage commitment in the BaFin / ECB submission at 150&ndash;180% group-level."</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'P2-04', module: 'P2',
    title: 'European tech buyer acquiring US SaaS target — AGPL exposure and CMA / EU antitrust planning',
    setup: '<p>A French strategic technology buyer (Altair Software SA) is acquiring NeuroPath, a Delaware-incorporated SaaS company providing workflow software to enterprise customers in the US, UK, EU, Canada and Australia. NeuroPath has $80m ARR, 320 employees, and a complex software stack. DD has discovered: (i) the core authentication module is AGPL-3.0; (ii) several core data-processing libraries are GPL-2.0; (iii) the founder PIIA uses "agrees to assign" language without "hereby assigns"; (iv) NeuroPath has 1.2m active enterprise users in the EU/UK including substantial customer-employee personal data; (v) NeuroPath has an "ecosystem"-flavoured market position adjacent to a Booking-style hotel-OTA segment via a partnership integration. The deal is signing in mid-2026; deal value &euro;420m.</p>',
    prompt: 'Walk through the four principal DD red flags, the antitrust planning given the post-2022 precedents, and the SPA / W&I architecture you would design.',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>The four principal DD red flags.</strong></p><ol><li><strong>AGPL-3.0 in the core authentication module &mdash; SaaS loophole closed.</strong> Classic GPL is triggered by distribution; AGPL section 13 extends the obligation to network interaction. NeuroPath users interacting with the AGPL authentication module over the network must be offered the corresponding source. <strong>Replacement is the only clean answer; engineering project of unknown duration.</strong> Remediation milestone: replace the AGPL component within 12&ndash;18 months post-closing; specific indemnity outside the cap for any source-disclosure obligations or claims that arise before replacement; W&amp;I underwriter excludes the matter entirely. <strong>Price reduction</strong> reflecting the engineering cost and the residual exposure during the remediation period.</li><li><strong>GPL-2.0 in core data-processing libraries.</strong> Less severe than AGPL because the trigger is distribution rather than network use. NeuroPath as a SaaS may not be distributing the binary, but if any on-premise version exists or any binary is distributed to a customer, GPL is triggered. Remediation menu: (i) replace the GPL components; (ii) comply with the source-disclosure obligation (not commercially viable for a proprietary SaaS); (iii) negotiate a commercial alternative licence with the GPL author (sometimes possible for individual contributors, not for community-licensed libraries). Remediation milestone within 18&ndash;24 months; specific indemnity outside the cap.</li><li><strong>Founder PIIA "agrees to assign" only.</strong> Per <em>Stanford v Roche</em>, 563 U.S. 776 (2011), "agrees to assign" is a future promise that does not transfer the patent automatically; "hereby assigns" effects an immediate transfer. <strong>Confirmatory assignments executed at signing</strong> &mdash; short-form documents from each founder, employee and contractor expressly assigning all rights in any IP created before the date of the confirmatory assignment that was used in the company&rsquo;s business. If founders have left and are uncooperative, structural risk priced into the deal or covered by specific indemnity outside the cap with seller-backed escrow.</li><li><strong>Personal-data scale (1.2m EU/UK enterprise users).</strong> Article 30 RoPA must be complete; cross-border transfer mechanism for any US data processors (DPF certification or SCCs + TIA); data-room day-one protocol with clean-team and redaction of customer-level personal data. UK transfers also require TRA. Multi-jurisdictional data-protection compliance review &mdash; UK ICO; French CNIL; German LfDIs; Italian Garante; Irish DPC for any EU-wide processing led from Ireland.</li></ol><p><strong>Antitrust planning given the post-2022 precedents.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>EU EUMR notification</strong> if turnover thresholds met. Altair (French) acquires US tech with EU activities; assess at the deal-team kick-off whether the &euro;420m deal-value-based threshold under EUMR Article 1(3) is engaged via cumulative turnover.</li><li><strong>UK CMA share-of-supply</strong> &mdash; voluntary regime under UK Enterprise Act 2002 s.23 (25%+ share of supply in UK or substantial part). Run the share-of-supply analysis pre-signing; <strong>Meta / Giphy precedent</strong> shows closing without UK CMA clearance does not protect the buyer.</li><li><strong>EU "ecosystem" theory of harm risk.</strong> NeuroPath&rsquo;s ecosystem-flavoured market position adjacent to a Booking-style hotel-OTA segment puts the deal in scope of <strong>Booking / eTraveli</strong>-style ecosystem theories. The Commission&rsquo;s theory in that case (2023 prohibition) was that strengthening Booking&rsquo;s position as the leading hotel-OTA ecosystem in the EEA would harm competition. <strong>Pre-filing dialogue must address ecosystem theories</strong>; commitments package design should include access / interoperability undertakings on the ecosystem-adjacent functionality.</li><li><strong>EU DMA Article 14 notification</strong> &mdash; only applies if Altair is a designated gatekeeper (it is not, on these facts); not engaged. But if NeuroPath is acquired by a future gatekeeper, the obligation kicks in.</li><li><strong>Article 22 EUMR referrals limited post-<em>Illumina/Grail</em></strong> (CJEU Case C-611/22 P, 3 September 2024) &mdash; the Commission cannot accept Article 22 referrals from member states for transactions that do not meet the referring member state&rsquo;s national thresholds. So below-threshold "killer acquisition" risk reduced but not eliminated.</li><li><strong>HSR (US)</strong> if size thresholds met (2025 base $126.4m); 2025 expanded HSR rules require list of all current and prospective competitors.</li><li><strong>CFIUS</strong> &mdash; non-US acquirer of US tech business with substantial US person sensitive data (1.2m users); voluntary notification recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>SPA / W&amp;I architecture.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>IP warranty package.</strong> Title to all material IP; "hereby assigns" / present-assignment language verified; chain-of-title evidence; no breach of any open-source licence; no GPL contamination of proprietary code; specific carve-outs for the AGPL and GPL findings (acknowledged risks managed via specific indemnity and remediation milestones).</li><li><strong>Specific indemnity for AGPL remediation.</strong> Outside the cap; survival 5 years; covers (i) cost of replacement engineering work above an agreed budget; (ii) any claim or settlement relating to AGPL section 13 source-disclosure obligations; (iii) regulatory penalties or customer damages arising from non-compliance pending replacement.</li><li><strong>Specific indemnity for GPL remediation.</strong> Outside the cap; survival 5 years; covers similar matters for the GPL libraries.</li><li><strong>Specific indemnity for founder PIIA chain.</strong> Outside the cap; survival 7 years; covers any claim by a founder, employee or contractor asserting non-assignment of IP.</li><li><strong>Data-protection warranty package + specific indemnity</strong> for any GDPR / UK GDPR enforcement matter discovered post-closing, with EU-wide DPC fine-cap exposure.</li><li><strong>Antitrust covenant package.</strong> "Reasonable best efforts" on EU and UK merger control; specific carve-outs identifying remedies the buyer will accept up to a defined ceiling (interoperability commitments; data-portability undertakings); reverse-termination fee at 5% of deal value (~&euro;21m) reflecting the ecosystem-theory risk profile per Adobe / Figma break-fee benchmark.</li><li><strong>Buy-side W&amp;I policy</strong> with policy limit 10&ndash;15% of EV. <strong>Specific exclusions:</strong> (i) AGPL and GPL remediation matters (handled via specific indemnity); (ii) founder PIIA chain matters (handled via specific indemnity); (iii) GDPR fines (public-policy non-insurability); (iv) cyber sublimit at 15% of policy limit; (v) AI / model-liability sublimit if any AI components.</li><li><strong>Seller-backed escrow</strong> of 7.5&ndash;10% of EV for 24 months to fund the specific indemnities; parent guarantee from the seller&rsquo;s management entity for the balance.</li><li><strong>Longstop calibration:</strong> 12-month longstop with 6-month extension right; reflects EU / UK merger-control timing if Phase II opens, plus CFIUS Phase II potential.</li></ul><p><strong>Partner-meeting summary:</strong> "AGPL in core authentication = replacement project, specific indemnity outside cap with seller-backed escrow, W&amp;I excludes; GPL in libraries similar; founder PIIA fixed via confirmatory assignments at signing plus specific indemnity for any non-cooperators; ecosystem-theory antitrust risk handled via reasonable best efforts with capped commitments and 5% reverse-termination fee per Adobe/Figma; 12-month longstop with extension; CFIUS voluntary plus EU DMA monitoring."</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  }
];

