window.__SCEN_P3 = [
  {
    id: 'P3-01', module: 'P3',
    title: 'Friday-night deadlock &mdash; Cap, basket, survival, scrape, retention',
    setup: '<p>It is 7pm on a Friday. You are external counsel for the buyer (a private-equity sponsor) on a &euro;180m carve-out from a strategic. Signing is targeted Sunday night. Five SPA points remain open after a 6-hour session:</p><ol><li><strong>Cap</strong>: buyer at 12% of EV (&euro;21.6m); seller at 8% (&euro;14.4m).</li><li><strong>Basket</strong>: buyer wants tipping at &euro;900,000 (0.5%); seller wants true deductible at &euro;1.8m (1%).</li><li><strong>Survival on general warranties</strong>: buyer at 24 months; seller at 18.</li><li><strong>Materiality scrape</strong>: buyer wants double scrape; seller wants damages-only scrape.</li><li><strong>W&amp;I retention</strong>: buyer at 0.25% stepping to nil at 12 months; seller at 0.5% flat (the seller has authority over the policy).</li></ol><p>The seller\'s counsel says: <em>"We\'re miles apart on cap. Either we close the gap tonight or my client wants to walk."</em></p>',
    prompt: 'Walk through the next 90 minutes. What is your turn-by-turn response choreography? Which trades do you propose, and in what order? What objective criteria do you cite? Where do you use "subject to package", and where do you concede outright? What letter do you draft if the deal does walk?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Step 1 &mdash; Defuse the anchor (Subramanian).</strong> <em>"We\'re not actually miles apart. Let me show you the math: 12% to 8% on cap is 4 points; ABA 2025 puts non-RWI caps for our deal size at 8&ndash;12%, with median around 10%. We\'re both inside the market. The package matters more than any single point."</em></p><p><strong>Step 2 &mdash; Build the issues list and propose the package.</strong> Five open points; propose the following structured landing using cross-clause trades:</p><ul><li><strong>Cap</strong>: 10% (move from 12 to 10) <em>in exchange for</em> tipping basket. <em>Trade 1.</em></li><li><strong>Basket type</strong>: tipping at 0.5% (move seller from true deductible at 1% to tipping at 0.5%) <em>as part of</em> Trade 1.</li><li><strong>Survival</strong>: 22 months (move from 24 to 22) <em>in exchange for</em> a partial knowledge scrape on operational warranties (financial statements, IP, contracts), keeping knowledge qualifier on catch-all warranties (compliance with laws, litigation). <em>Trade 2 + variant.</em></li><li><strong>Materiality scrape</strong>: double scrape (move seller from damages-only) <em>in exchange for</em> de minimis up from &euro;75k to &euro;100k. <em>Trade 4.</em></li><li><strong>W&amp;I retention</strong>: 0.25% stepping to nil at 12 months (move seller from 0.5% flat) <em>in exchange for</em> the buyer absorbing 100% of the W&amp;I premium. <em>Trade 7.</em></li></ul><p>State explicitly: <em>"This is subject to package. If you can\'t land on all five, we\'re back to square one."</em></p><p><strong>Step 3 &mdash; Cite objective criteria for each move.</strong></p><ul><li>Cap: ABA 2025 Private Target Deal Points Study, median ~10% for non-RWI deals at this size.</li><li>Basket: ABA 2025, deductibles 46% / tipping 33%; the trade (cap down for tipping) is the standard cross-clause structure.</li><li>Survival: ABA 2025, 12 months 23% / 18 months 19% / 24 months 26%; 22 months is at the median.</li><li>Materiality scrape: ABA 2025, double scrapes in 82% (up from 69% in 2023). This is market.</li><li>W&amp;I retention: Marsh EMEA Transactional Risk 2024 confirms 0.5% stepping to 0.25% / nil at 12 months is the post-2024 standard for deals &gt;&euro;100m.</li></ul><p><strong>Step 4 &mdash; The phrasebook: agent-principal mechanics.</strong> If counterparty hesitates, deploy: <em>"Give me something to take to the client &mdash; this package gets us both home."</em> If they push back on a single point, use <em>"we\'ve moved twice &mdash; your turn"</em> as reciprocity enforcement. If they say <em>"we need to caucus"</em>, accept and document the open list in writing within 24 hours.</p><p><strong>Step 5 &mdash; The fallback if the deal walks.</strong> Draft Template 6 (termination notice). Critical inclusions: (i) reference to the long-stop clause and unsatisfied CPs (or, if pre-signing walk, no termination notice required &mdash; just confirm withdrawal); (ii) reverse-termination-fee provisions if applicable; (iii) surviving provisions list (confidentiality, governing law, costs); (iv) reservation of rights. Send by email and courier per the SPA notices clause &mdash; many courts enforce strictly.</p><p><strong>Step 6 &mdash; The post-mortem.</strong> If the deal walks, internal memo (Template 2 variant) to the client documenting (i) where the package landed; (ii) whose move it was; (iii) what the negotiating dynamic was; (iv) what to do differently next time. The relationship with seller\'s counsel needs preserving for the next deal &mdash; multi-shot game discipline.</p><p><strong>Partner-meeting takeaway.</strong> The choreography is not single-issue debate; it is package construction. Five linked trades with empirical anchors solve five linked positions. <em>"Subject to package"</em> is the contractual placeholder that holds it all together until signing. The polite phrasebook (<em>"give me something to take to the client"</em>, <em>"we\'d be in the same shoes"</em>) is the relational lubricant; the empirical citations are the substantive backbone.</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'P3-02', module: 'P3',
    title: 'Antitrust deadlock &mdash; HOHW, RTF, long-stop on a $5bn deal',
    setup: '<p>Buyer (a US strategic) is acquiring Seller (a US public company) for $5.0bn cash. The deal will be reviewed by the US DOJ and the European Commission; a Phase II decision is plausible in both jurisdictions. The merger agreement is being negotiated. The remaining open points are:</p><ol><li><strong>Antitrust efforts standard</strong>: seller wants full hell-or-high-water (HOHW); buyer wants commercially reasonable efforts.</li><li><strong>Reverse termination fee</strong>: seller wants 5% of equity value flat ($250m); buyer wants 3% flat ($150m).</li><li><strong>Long-stop date</strong>: seller wants 12 months flat; buyer wants 9 months with mutual-extension language.</li><li><strong>Burdensome conditions carve-out</strong>: seller wants no carve-out; buyer wants carve-out for divestitures &gt;25% of target revenue.</li></ol>',
    prompt: 'Construct the negotiation choreography. What is the structured landing on each point? Which empirical anchors do you cite? How do the four points trade against each other (cross-clause trades)? What is the partner-meeting framing for each move?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Structured landing &mdash; the integrated package.</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Antitrust efforts standard</strong>: capped HOHW. Buyer commits to all remedies including divestitures up to a cap of <strong>15% of target revenue</strong>. Beyond that, the burdensome-conditions carve-out applies. The label "commercially reasonable efforts" or "reasonable best efforts" matters less than the operational obligations attached (Skadden 2024 analysis; Mayer Brown 2023). Frame: <em>"the standard is fine; the obligations attached are what drive outcomes."</em></li><li><strong>Reverse termination fee</strong>: tiered at <strong>2% / 4% / 5%</strong>. Tier 1 (2% = $100m): termination pre-Phase II initiation. Tier 2 (4% = $200m): termination post-Phase II in either jurisdiction. Tier 3 (5% = $250m): buyer fails to close after clearance is obtained. Per Houlihan Lokey 2023 Termination Fee Study: mean 4.3% / median 4.2% across the sample. Microsoft / Activision (2022) is the canonical tiered RTF precedent ($2.0bn / $2.5bn / $3.0bn = 4.4% top tier on $68.7bn). Frame: <em>"flat 5% punishes the buyer in scenarios where both sides did their best; tiered respects the actual escalation in commitment."</em></li><li><strong>Long-stop date</strong>: <strong>12 months initial</strong>, with multi-trigger automatic extensions: <strong>+3 months if EU Commission initiates Phase II</strong>; <strong>+3 months if DOJ issues a Second Request</strong>; <strong>+3 months if UK CMA initiates Phase 2</strong>. Maximum long-stop 21 months. Per Goodwin\'s 2025 deal-delays research, EU Phase II timelines are 18&ndash;24 months &mdash; flat 12 months is unrealistic for an antitrust-sensitive deal of this size. Frame: <em>"12 months gets us through ordinary clearance; the multi-trigger extensions handle the realistic Phase II scenarios. Without extensions we have a forced walk on a deal both sides want."</em></li><li><strong>Burdensome conditions carve-out</strong>: divestitures &gt;<strong>15% of target revenue</strong> (matching the HOHW cap), plus a defined list: (i) divestitures of core IP identified in a schedule; (ii) mandatory licensing of trade secrets; (iii) divestitures that materially impair the strategic rationale (loss of access to defined geographic markets); (iv) behavioural remedies that materially impair the operation of the combined business. Frame: <em>"the carve-out is the buyer\'s walk-right when remedies become destructive. Without it, capped HOHW is meaningless. With it, the cap and the carve-out together define a workable risk envelope."</em></li></ol><p><strong>Cross-clause trade structure.</strong> The four points are tightly linked:</p><ul><li><em>Trade 3 (HOHW &harr; RTF size)</em>: capped HOHW at 15% with carve-outs <em>in exchange for</em> tiered RTF up to 5% top tier. Both sides move from extreme (no HOHW / no RTF; full HOHW / 0% RTF) to a realistic middle.</li><li><em>Long-stop and multi-trigger extensions</em> are the calendar architecture supporting the HOHW commitment &mdash; without time, no commitment is meaningful. Module 08 develops the mechanics.</li><li><em>Burdensome-conditions carve-out</em> is the substantive boundary on the HOHW commitment.</li></ul><p><strong>Empirical anchors to cite.</strong></p><ul><li>Houlihan Lokey 2023 Termination Fee Study: mean RTF 4.3% / median 4.2%.</li><li>Microsoft / Activision (2022): $2.0bn / $2.5bn / $3.0bn tiered RTF (4.4% top tier on $68.7bn).</li><li>Mayer Brown 2023 analysis: capped HOHW with carve-outs is the 2023&ndash;2025 standard structure.</li><li>Goodwin 2025 deal-delays research: EU Phase II timelines 18&ndash;24 months; multi-trigger extensions standard.</li><li>Skadden 2024: efforts standard label is less important than operational obligations attached.</li></ul><p><strong>Partner-meeting framing.</strong> <em>"This is the post-2022 antitrust playbook: capped HOHW with burdensome-conditions carve-out, tiered RTF, multi-trigger long-stop extensions. The structure handles the realistic regulatory scenarios at our deal size. We\'re not asking for less than market; we\'re asking for the structure that\'s actually built into market."</em> Subject-to-package framing: <em>"these four points stand or fall together &mdash; if we can\'t land on the package, we\'re back to where we started."</em> The seller\'s lawyer either signs the package or escalates to principals; either way the substantive position is supported by current empirical anchors.</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'P3-03', module: 'P3',
    title: 'MAC reservation &mdash; the no-walk, no-waive notice',
    setup: '<p>You are external counsel for the buyer of a UK target. Signing was 5 months ago; closing is 4 weeks away. Two CPs remain (antitrust clearance, CFIUS). Yesterday, the target\'s largest customer (28% of revenue) gave 12 months\' notice of non-renewal of its master services agreement, citing a competitor offering. The seller has updated the disclosure letter with the development. The buyer\'s investment committee is split: half want to walk under the MAC; half want to close (the price is good, the customer relationship may be salvageable, the underlying business is sound).</p>',
    prompt: 'Draft a MAC reservation-of-rights notice (Template 4) for the partner\'s review. Then walk through the strategic considerations: what does the notice preserve, what does it not commit to, and what is the partner-meeting framing of the buyer\'s options? What other levers (price reduction; specific indemnity; restructuring of escrow) might be on the table without invoking MAC?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Part 1 &mdash; The MAC reservation-of-rights notice (Template 4 applied).</strong></p><p><em>To: [Seller]<br>From: [Buyer]<br>Re: [Target] acquisition &mdash; reservation of rights / non-invocation notice<br>Date: [today]</em></p><p>1. We refer to the Sale and Purchase Agreement dated [date] between Buyer and Seller (the "<strong>SPA</strong>") and to clause [X] thereof (the "<strong>Material Adverse Change clause</strong>" or "<strong>MAC</strong>").</p><p>2. Buyer has been notified by the Seller in the Disclosure Letter update of [date], and otherwise has become aware, of the following development at the Target:</p><ul><li>On [date], [Customer] (representing approximately 28% of the Target\'s revenue for the financial year ended [date]) gave notice of non-renewal of its Master Services Agreement dated [date], with effect from [date 12 months hence]. The notice cites the customer\'s acceptance of a competitor offering.</li></ul><p>3. <strong>Reservation of rights.</strong> This notice is given expressly to preserve Buyer\'s rights under the Material Adverse Change clause and any other provision of the SPA. This notice does <em>not</em> constitute: (i) a waiver of any rights of Buyer; (ii) a determination that a Material Adverse Change has occurred; (iii) an election to terminate the SPA, to proceed to closing, or otherwise. Buyer expressly reserves all rights, remedies, claims, defences, and elections under the SPA and at law.</p><p>4. <strong>Continuing disclosure.</strong> Seller is reminded of its obligations under clauses [Y] and [Z] of the SPA. Buyer requests immediate disclosure of any further developments bearing on the Material Adverse Change determination, including any further customer notifications, competitive responses, or remedial actions taken or contemplated by the Target.</p><p>5. Nothing in this notice prejudices or limits any other notice, claim or right of Buyer.</p><p>Yours faithfully,<br>[Buyer Counsel]</p><p><strong>Part 2 &mdash; What the notice preserves and what it does not commit to.</strong></p><ul><li>Preserves: the right to invoke MAC if the situation worsens (further customer attrition, other adverse developments).</li><li>Preserves: the right to negotiate price reduction, specific indemnity, or escrow restructuring as alternatives to walk.</li><li>Does not commit to: walking; closing; waiving the breach; converting the matter to an indemnity claim post-closing.</li><li>Avoids: estoppel by conduct (continuing to perform after a known potential MAC without reservation may be construed as waiver). The notice prevents that argument.</li></ul><p><strong>Part 3 &mdash; The strategic options short of MAC invocation.</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Price reduction</strong>: negotiate a downward adjustment of the headline price reflecting the lost customer revenue. At 28% of revenue and a 10x EBITDA multiple, the headline reduction could be substantial. Frame as <em>"the value the buyer paid for is no longer there; we\'re asking for the value back, not for a windfall."</em> Seller may resist on the basis that the customer hasn\'t actually left yet.</li><li><strong>Specific indemnity</strong>: ring-fenced indemnity for actual customer attrition, capped at the lost-revenue impact, surviving 24&ndash;36 months. Trade against general cap reduction. Trade 6 in operation.</li><li><strong>Escrow restructuring</strong>: increase the escrow size and / or extend the escrow release schedule to give the buyer security if customer attrition materialises post-closing. Frame as <em>"protecting the buyer\'s interim risk without renegotiating the deal."</em></li><li><strong>Earnout</strong>: convert part of the headline price to an earnout pegged to customer retention metrics. Per ABA 2025 earnouts have dropped to 18% of deals; this would be an unusual structure but available.</li><li><strong>Walk under MAC</strong>: nuclear option. UK MAC bar is very high (<em>Akorn v Fresenius</em> 2018, US Delaware applied; UK courts have not endorsed Akorn but the standard is similarly stringent). A single 28%-customer loss arguably qualifies <em>if</em> long-term durability of the lost revenue is shown. <em>Litigation risk</em>: seller will challenge MAC invocation. Test of "durability" requires forward-looking evidence (Akorn). The default outcome of MAC litigation is the buyer is forced to close at original price.</li></ol><p><strong>Part 4 &mdash; Partner-meeting framing.</strong> <em>"We\'re not in MAC territory yet &mdash; one customer notice with 12 months\' notice doesn\'t establish durability of impact under Akorn / similar standards. But we are in renegotiation territory: the value the buyer paid for has been impaired. The right move is the reservation notice today (preserves rights), then a substantive discussion next week on price reduction, specific indemnity, and escrow restructuring. Walk is the last resort because the litigation risk on MAC invocation is asymmetric &mdash; UK courts have rarely endorsed MAC walks; a forced-close scenario is unattractive."</em></p><p><strong>Part 5 &mdash; The cross-references.</strong> Module 09 (Interim Period &amp; MAC) develops the doctrine; <em>Akorn v Fresenius</em> (Del. Ch. 2018) is the leading MAC-walk case. Module 12 (R&amp;Ws) covers the bring-down standard at closing and how the customer development interacts with the financial-statements warranty. Module 13 (Indemnification) covers the specific indemnity / cap / escrow architecture. Module 14 (W&amp;I) covers whether the customer development is a sign-to-close breach covered under the policy.</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'P3-04', module: 'P3',
    title: 'The first markup &mdash; setting the anchor',
    setup: '<p>You are external counsel for a private-equity buyer on a &euro;120m bilateral acquisition. The seller is a strategic divesting a non-core business. The non-binding term sheet is signed (price &euro;120m, locked-box pricing, W&amp;I-backed, customary representations and warranties, indemnification "in line with market practice"). You have been asked to send the first SPA markup to the seller\'s counsel. The deal is bilateral &mdash; no auction pressure &mdash; but the seller has signalled it wants signing within 8 weeks.</p>',
    prompt: 'How do you set the first-markup anchor? What positions do you take on cap, basket type, basket size, de minimis, survival, materiality scrape, knowledge scrape, hell-or-high-water, RTF, and W&I retention &mdash; and why those positions specifically? What objective criteria do you cite in the cover memo? What is the partner-meeting framing of the first markup?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>The first-markup discipline (Subramanian).</strong> Whoever sends the first SPA markup anchors the entire negotiation grid. The buyer\'s first markup should: (i) be at the buyer-friendly end of the empirical range; (ii) be defensible (cite ABA / Marsh / Houlihan); (iii) not be so extreme that the seller dismisses it as posturing &mdash; the credible anchor is the one that defines the negotiation, the absurd anchor is dismissed.</p><p><strong>The buyer\'s first-markup positions and rationales.</strong></p><table><thead><tr><th>Issue</th><th>Buyer position</th><th>Rationale / anchor</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Cap (general warranties)</strong></td><td>15% of EV (= &euro;18m) at SPA level (with W&amp;I tower stacked above; total cover up to 20% EV)</td><td>ABA 2025: 8&ndash;12% median for non-RWI; we\'re anchoring above market for negotiating room. With W&amp;I, SPA cap typically collapses to 0.25&ndash;0.5% (retention level) &mdash; we\'re asking for the buyer-friendly architecture before the broker call.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Basket type</strong></td><td>Tipping basket</td><td>ABA 2025: tipping in 33% / deductible in 46%. Buyer-friendly position; trades against cap (Trade 1).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Basket size</strong></td><td>0.5% of EV (= &euro;600,000)</td><td>ABA 2025: 45% of deals at &le;0.5%, 95% at &le;1%. We\'re at the median.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>De minimis</strong></td><td>&euro;50,000 (0.04%)</td><td>Low de minimis; allows aggregation. Sub-de-minimis claims sweep into the basket cumulatively.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Survival (general warranties)</strong></td><td>24 months</td><td>ABA 2025: 24 months in 26% of deals (above median 18 months). Buyer position.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Survival (tax)</strong></td><td>7 years from closing</td><td>UK TMA 1970 ss.34/36/36A: ordinary 4y / careless 6y / deliberate 20y. 7 years covers careless plus buffer.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Survival (fundamentals)</strong></td><td>10 years from closing (or perpetual on title)</td><td>Standard buyer position; protects against title / capacity issues with long discovery cycles.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Materiality scrape</strong></td><td>Double scrape (breach + damages)</td><td>ABA 2025: double scrapes in 82% of US private deals. Now the market default; not even buyer-aggressive.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Knowledge scrape</strong></td><td>Full scrape on operational warranties</td><td>Buyer position. Trade 2 fallback: partial knowledge scrape (operational only) in exchange for shorter survival.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sandbagging</strong></td><td>Pro-sandbagging explicit</td><td>Delaware default per <em>Cobalt</em> 2017; we\'re entrenching the default. UK deal: explicit clause needed (default is anti).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>HOHW (antitrust)</strong></td><td>Reasonable best efforts; no HOHW; burdensome-conditions carve-out for divestitures &gt;5% of target revenue</td><td>Light-touch antitrust because no overlap; we\'re anchoring buyer-friendly with cap and carve-out. Trade 3 fallback: capped HOHW at 15% with tiered RTF.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>RTF</strong></td><td>2% flat (= &euro;2.4m)</td><td>Below Houlihan Lokey 2023 mean (4.3%); buyer-friendly anchor. Trade 3 fallback: tiered up to 4&ndash;5% top tier in exchange for HOHW commitment.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Long-stop date</strong></td><td>9 months with mutual-extension language</td><td>Light-touch antitrust; 9 months is sufficient. Multi-trigger extensions kept available as fallback if seller pushes.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>W&amp;I retention</strong></td><td>0.25% stepping to nil at 12 months</td><td>Marsh EMEA 2024: 0.5% stepping to 0.25% is post-2024 standard at deals &gt;&euro;100m. We\'re at the buyer-friendly end.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>W&amp;I premium</strong></td><td>100% buyer-paid</td><td>Standard for buyer-side policy. Trade 7 fallback: 50/50 split if seller wants the lower retention.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Specific indemnity</strong></td><td>For 3 identified DD findings: tax exposure (&euro;5m sub-limit); environmental remediation at site X (&euro;3m); IP litigation matter (&euro;2m)</td><td>Specific indemnities for known matters; ring-fenced outside cap and basket. Trade 6 in operation.</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>The cover memo accompanying the markup.</strong></p><p><em>"Dear [Counsel],</em></p><p><em>Attached is our first markup of the Sale and Purchase Agreement, reflecting our client\'s positions on the open commercial points. We have benchmarked each material position against the most recent empirical sources:</em></p><ul><li><em>Per the ABA 2025 Private Target M&amp;A Deal Points Study (139-deal sample, transactions 2024&ndash;Q1 2025), our positions on cap, basket size, survival, materiality scrape and de minimis are all within the market range.</em></li><li><em>Per Marsh EMEA Transactional Risk 2024, our W&amp;I retention and premium positions reflect the post-2024 standard.</em></li><li><em>Per Houlihan Lokey 2023 Termination Fee Study, our RTF position is appropriate for the regulatory profile of this deal.</em></li><li><em>Per Goodwin 2025 deal-delays research, the long-stop and extension architecture is appropriate for the modest antitrust risk at this deal size.</em></li></ul><p><em>We have included specific indemnities for three matters identified in our DD: the [tax / environmental / IP] matters discussed at our session of [date]. These are ring-fenced outside the general cap and basket, with the sub-limits indicated.</em></p><p><em>We propose a working session next week to walk through the package. We are running a tight signing timetable but are open to flexibility on individual issues subject to the overall package agreement.</em></p><p><em>Best regards,<br>[Counsel]"</em></p><p><strong>Partner-meeting framing of the first markup.</strong> <em>"The first markup is the anchor for everything that follows. It has to be buyer-friendly enough to give us room to move, defensible enough to be taken seriously, and structured enough to make the cross-clause trades visible to the seller. Every material position cites an empirical source. The seller\'s lawyer will redline this in 5 days; we\'ll have a session 7 days after that. By then we\'ll know which trades they\'re willing to make, which they\'re holding the line on, and where the package will land. The first markup is two-thirds of the negotiation."</em></p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  }
];

/* Scenario fragment — Module P4 (Origination & Business Development).
   Each scenario rehearses a partner conversation the user must be able to
   have credibly to convert the boutique pitch. */
window.__SCEN_P4 = [
  {
    id: 'P4-01', module: 'P4',
    title: 'The lateral conversation — a Kirkland partner running the maths',
    setup: '<p>You are sitting across from <strong>Sarah Chen</strong>, a senior M&amp;A partner at Kirkland &amp; Ellis (London). She runs a sponsor-led mid-market private equity practice. Stated book of business: <strong>$7m</strong>. 70% of her book is mid-market PE sponsors with $1–8bn AuM (sticky-to-her-personally); 30% is two large institutional PE platforms (sticky-to-firm). Comp this year roughly $5m.</p><p>You are pitching her to co-found an AI-augmented M&amp;A boutique under UK ABS. Twenty minutes in, she leans back and says quietly:</p><p style="margin-left:1em;"><em>"Look — I get the AI thesis. It\'s the right call. But Kirkland just put me on a three-year guarantee at $5.5m and I\'ve got $4m of deferred comp accruing. What\'s the actual offer? And what happens to my book in the move? Be specific."</em></p>',
    prompt: 'What does she actually need to hear? Walk through (a) the quantitative offer she\'s asking for, (b) the book-of-business analysis she\'s already running in her head, and (c) the one or two things you can offer that Kirkland structurally cannot.',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>(a) The quantitative offer.</strong> She is asking for a structured package, not a single number. The credible offer:</p><ul><li><strong>Three-year compensation guarantee at 1.0×–1.2× current pay</strong> = $5–6m base. Below current is a non-starter; above 1.2× signals desperation.</li><li><strong>Make-whole on forfeited deferred comp</strong> = $2–3m one-off sign-on (covers ~50–75% of the $4m deferred). The boutique cannot fund the full $4m because it would crater first-year cash; the partner accepts a discount because the forfeiture is partly notional anyway (she would not collect it for years even if she stayed).</li><li><strong>Founding equity grant</strong> = 8–12% of the boutique on a four-year vest, accelerated on change of control. Her current PEP run cannot generate this.</li><li><strong>No MMP-style clawback in Year 1</strong> — she has just lost six months of garden leave and reset her client relationships. Years 2–3, MMP targets are reasonable (e.g. $3.5m / $4.5m of originations).</li><li><strong>Capital contribution</strong> — funded by firm-arranged bank loan over 24 months, structured to fall away if she leaves involuntarily before vest.</li></ul><p>Total cost to extract: roughly $7–9m of cash + equity over Year 1, against an underwriteable book of $4–5m.</p><p><strong>(b) The book-of-business analysis she is running in her head.</strong> Garden leave at Kirkland for senior partners is typically 3–6 months. During that period, her clients are reassigned to colleagues at Kirkland, who deepen those relationships. By the time she emerges, 20–30% of her stated book has eroded. Then she has 12–18 months of marketing period to land what remains at the boutique.</p><ul><li><em>The 70% mid-market PE portion</em> ($4.9m of her stated book): probably 70–85% portable. Mid-market PE sponsors care about the partner more than the platform. Realistic capture: $3.4–4.2m.</li><li><em>The 30% institutional PE portion</em> ($2.1m of her stated book): probably 20–40% portable. Megafunds are panel-driven; the boutique is unlikely to make their panel in Year 1 even with her relationship. Realistic capture: $0.4–0.8m.</li><li><em>Total realistic Year-1 book at the boutique:</em> $3.8–5.0m. Against the underwriting target.</li></ul><p>She is also running the equity calculation: if the boutique scales to $50–150m revenue at 30–40% margin in 5–7 years, her 8–12% equity is worth $1.2–7.2m on a discounted basis. Below her PEP run, but the upside scenario is meaningful.</p><p><strong>(c) What you can offer that Kirkland structurally cannot.</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Conflicts narrowing.</strong> Kirkland\'s anchor PE relationships likely conflict her out of two or three sub-sectors she would otherwise serve. At the boutique with a clean conflict map (and pre-cleared recruitment of co-founders), she takes those mandates. Quantify them — name two or three sub-sector clients she could pitch on Day 1 at the boutique.</li><li><strong>Pricing power.</strong> She can quote fixed fees and lower blended rates that Kirkland\'s cost structure cannot match — and her mid-market PE clients (her sticky 70%) are the segment that values exactly this. The pricing edge is a partner-level competitive weapon, not just a firm-level marketing claim.</li><li><strong>Equity in a scarce asset.</strong> Founding partner of a credible early M&amp;A AI boutique is a one-time-only opportunity. Kirkland cannot offer founder equity. The bet is risky; the asymmetry is real.</li></ol><p><strong>The closing move.</strong> "Sarah, I\'m not asking you to take less than Kirkland on cash. I\'m asking you to take roughly the same cash plus equity in a scarce asset, in exchange for going through 18 months of pain (garden leave + marketing period + book consolidation) that you\'ll be paid for. Show me where the numbers don\'t work and I\'ll come back next week." That answer respects her intelligence and signals you\'ve actually run her maths. The wrong answer is "we\'ll match your guarantee plus equity" — too vague, too generous, and signals you don\'t understand the structural retention forces.</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'P4-02', module: 'P4',
    title: 'Beauty parade and fee structure — pitching a $400m sponsor-led mid-market deal',
    setup: '<p>The boutique has been invited to a four-way beauty parade for a $400m sponsor-led mid-market PE acquisition. The competing firms are <strong>Latham &amp; Watkins, Skadden, and Goodwin Procter</strong>. The PE sponsor has $4bn AuM, runs ~10 deals a year, and the GC has signalled that AI-augmented service delivery is a deciding factor at the margin.</p><p>Latham\'s pitched fee is a $4.8m capped fee with a 1.5× success multiplier on closing ($7.2m). Skadden and Goodwin will likely come in around $4.5–5m. Your boutique partner-team is two M&amp;A founders + one regulatory partner + four senior associates.</p>',
    prompt: 'What fee structure should the boutique propose, and what are the pitch points that win the mandate from this PE sponsor?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>The fee structure.</strong> Three credible options, in increasing order of risk-share:</p><ol><li><strong>Capped fee + reduced success multiplier.</strong> Match Latham\'s $4.8m cap but quote a 1.25× multiplier on closing ($6.0m vs. Latham\'s $7.2m). Total potential fee at closing: $6.0m vs. $7.2m. The cap is the same; the discount is on the success premium. Buyer-friendly framing: "we don\'t need the same upside as a 600-partner platform."</li><li><strong>Lower blended fee structure.</strong> Quote a phased fee: $3.6m through signing (capped); $1.0m signing-to-closing (capped); $1.0m success multiplier on closing. Total at closing $5.6m vs. Latham\'s $7.2m. Partner-density commitment: 60% of hours on the deal are partner-level.</li><li><strong>Risk-share — diligence-driven price-chip rebate.</strong> Quote a $4.5m capped fee + 5% of any DD-driven price reduction we identify (capped at $1.5m of additional fee). If our DD finds an issue worth a $5m price chip, we earn $0.25m of additional fee; if we find $20m, we earn $1m additional. Aligns our incentive with the buyer\'s value capture. <em>Caveat — must be drafted carefully to comply with SRA Code / champerty doctrine in the UK; for US sponsors this kind of risk-share is regulatory grey but increasingly seen at boutiques. Internal compliance review required before pitching.</em></li></ol><p><strong>The pitch points that win.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Senior-partner density.</strong> "Our two M&amp;A founding partners will personally handle 60% of the hours on this deal. Latham will give you 15–20% partner hours and a senior associate running it day-to-day." This is the single sharpest commercial differentiation the boutique has. Quantify the hours.</li><li><strong>AI tooling architecture.</strong> Specific roadmap: contract review automation (Crosby-style), DD memo automation, redline reconciliation, regulatory filing assembly, real-time market intelligence (Mergermarket / PitchBook integration). <em>Critical:</em> data confidentiality regime (no third-party model training; on-premises or VPC deployment; client data segregation; SOC 2 / ISO 27001 in process). The PE sponsor\'s deal team has been burned by AI tools that leak — your data architecture is the decision pivot.</li><li><strong>Speed commitments.</strong> Specific contractual commitments on turnaround (DD memo within 5 business days of full data room access; SPA mark-up within 48 hours of receipt; regulatory filing within 10 business days). Quantified speed is something a 600-partner platform structurally cannot match because of internal review chains. Boutique can.</li><li><strong>Conflicts.</strong> Pre-cleared. Show the slide before they ask. Specific cleared sub-sector positioning relative to the sponsor\'s portfolio.</li><li><strong>Fee transparency.</strong> Show the working — partner hours × rate, associate hours × rate, AFA premium, success multiplier — in a single table. Sponsors who run carry calcs respect financial transparency more than headline lowness.</li><li><strong>Cross-sell story.</strong> The portfolio-AFA pitch: "if this goes well, we want a 3-year retainer-plus-overage on three of your portfolio companies for ongoing GC work, at $X/month flat." Sponsor cross-portfolio leverage is a relationship-builder.</li></ul><p><strong>What kills the pitch (avoid).</strong></p><ul><li><em>Loss-leader pricing.</em> Quoting $3m flat reads as desperate or as not understanding the deal complexity. Sophisticated PE GCs prefer accurate pricing with capped overage to artificially low headline fees that everyone knows will overrun.</li><li><em>Generic AI deck.</em> "We use AI to make our lawyers more productive" — this is what every other firm is saying. The credible answer is data-flow architecture and substitution-rate metrics ("our DD memo automation reduces partner review time by X% on a 200-document data room").</li><li><em>Wrong partner in the room.</em> The two founding M&amp;A partners must be in the pitch meeting, not a junior partner with a deck. PE GCs decide on chemistry as much as on credentials.</li><li><em>No phone call before the meeting.</em> Per BTI Consulting longitudinal data, firms that contact the client by phone before the pitch win at substantially higher rates. Find a way to talk to the GC for 15 minutes before the formal pitch — even just to ask about their current AI / data confidentiality posture.</li></ul>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'P4-03', module: 'P4',
    title: 'The conflict — target\'s CFO was the firm\'s former M&A client',
    setup: '<p>The boutique has been engaged by <strong>BlueSky Capital</strong> (US PE sponsor) for a buy-side M&amp;A on a $250m mid-market target. Two weeks into the engagement, conflicts review surfaces an issue: the target\'s CFO, James Patel, was personally a client of one of the boutique\'s founding partners <em>before</em> the boutique was formed, when she was at Linklaters. She advised him on the sale of his prior business in 2022 — a separate, unrelated transaction that closed cleanly. Patel signed a customary engagement-letter waiver of the firm\'s right to act adversely to him in the future, but the waiver was drafted in standard Linklaters-2022 form.</p>',
    prompt: 'Walk through the conflicts analysis under both Rule 1.9 (US) and the SRA framework (UK). What does the boutique need to do, and how does the analysis change if the founding partner brought Patel\'s file (or knowledge of his finances) into the boutique?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>(1) The formal conflict analysis.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Rule 1.9 (US) — duties to former clients.</strong> The relevant test is "same or substantially related matter." The 2022 sale of Patel\'s prior business is a different transaction from the 2025 BlueSky acquisition where Patel is now CFO of the target. The two are not the same matter. The "substantially related" test asks whether confidential information from the former representation would be useful in the new representation. Here, the 2022 deal involved Patel\'s prior business (different industry, different counterparty), not the current target. <strong>Likely conclusion under Rule 1.9:</strong> not "substantially related" on the formal test. <em>But</em> personal financial information about Patel (compensation, equity holdings, family wealth) acquired in the 2022 representation could plausibly be material to the negotiation of his employment / retention package as a continuing CFO post-closing — that creates a genuine information-overlap concern even if the formal "substantially related" test is not triggered.</li><li><strong>Rule 1.10(a)(2) (US imputation).</strong> The founding partner\'s individual conflict (if any) is imputed to the boutique. A screen + apportionment of no fee + written notice to Patel can cure if the conflict is imputed lateral; but a screen does not cure a Rule 1.7 (current-client) issue if it would be characterised that way.</li><li><strong>SRA Code / Principles (UK).</strong> The duty to act in the best interests of each client + the duty of confidentiality. The boutique\'s duty of confidentiality to Patel under the 2022 representation survives termination. The 2022 waiver — if drafted in standard form — typically waives prospective adverse representations <em>but does not waive confidentiality</em>. So even with the waiver, the founding partner cannot use Patel\'s confidential information for BlueSky\'s benefit. An information barrier inside the boutique is the standard SRA-compliant response.</li></ul><p><strong>(2) What the boutique needs to do.</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Read the 2022 waiver carefully.</strong> Specifically: does it waive prospective adverse representations? Does it carve out matters where confidential information from the prior representation would be material? Standard Linklaters form likely includes both a prospective consent and an information-barrier requirement.</li><li><strong>Notify Patel and BlueSky.</strong> Both clients should receive written notice that the conflict has been identified, that the founding partner will be screened from the BlueSky engagement, and that confidentiality will be maintained. Both clients should reaffirm consent in writing — even if the 2022 waiver covers the prospective adverse-representation point, fresh affirmative consent is the safer move.</li><li><strong>Erect an information barrier inside the boutique.</strong> The founding partner is screened from the BlueSky engagement: separate file access, no participation in deal calls, no financial credit on the matter, no access to deal documents. Written certifications from the screened partner. Fee apportionment excludes her.</li><li><strong>Document the screen.</strong> Standard components: timing of screen establishment (must be early in the engagement); IT-system access blocks; separate billing reviewer; written certifications; no shared support staff or internal meetings.</li><li><strong>Decline the engagement if the screen cannot work.</strong> If the boutique\'s small headcount means an effective screen is impossible (e.g. shared paralegal, shared file system that cannot be partitioned, shared deal-management software), the right answer is to decline BlueSky and refer to another firm. The hot-potato rule does not apply here because BlueSky is the new client, not an existing one being dropped.</li></ol><p><strong>(3) How the analysis changes if the founding partner brought Patel\'s file (or financial knowledge) into the boutique.</strong></p><p>This makes everything materially worse. If the partner physically transferred Patel\'s file (paper or electronic) to the boutique:</p><ul><li><strong>Conflicts and duty of confidentiality compound.</strong> The boutique now holds confidential information about a person who is now negotiating against a current client. Even with a screen, the structural risk to confidentiality is higher.</li><li><strong>The 2022 Linklaters waiver may not cover the boutique.</strong> The waiver was given to Linklaters; it is not automatically transferred to the boutique. Patel could plausibly assert that the boutique is a "new firm" and that no waiver covers this engagement — which would be a Rule 1.9 problem the boutique has to cure with fresh consent (and Patel may decline).</li><li><strong>Disclosure obligation to BlueSky becomes immediate.</strong> The boutique cannot run a screen without telling BlueSky why, and BlueSky may decline to consent to a screened representation if it materially limits the team available.</li><li><strong>Practical answer.</strong> If the partner brought the file, the boutique should: (i) immediately segregate Patel\'s file in encrypted form with no internal access; (ii) seek fresh waivers from both Patel and BlueSky; (iii) consider whether the founding partner needs to step down from the boutique\'s management committee on this matter to make the screen credible. If those steps cannot be cleanly executed, the boutique declines.</li></ul><p><strong>(4) The boutique-formation lesson.</strong> Founding partners bringing client conflicts is the single largest unsexy operational risk in boutique formation. Pre-formation conflicts mapping — running every founding partner\'s former-client universe against the proposed business plan — is mandatory. Conflicts that surface six months in are deal-killers. The "clean conflict map" advantage of a boutique is real but only if it has been built deliberately.</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'P4-04', module: 'P4',
    title: 'AI boutique formation — UK ABS vs. US Arizona ABS structural choice',
    setup: '<p>You have $8m of seed funding and three founding partners committed: a UK Magic Circle M&amp;A partner (London-based, $6m book, mostly UK / EU PE); a Kirkland US M&amp;A partner (NY-based, $4m book, mostly US PE / strategic); and you (founder/CEO, non-lawyer, US-based). You need to choose a regulatory structure for the boutique.</p><p>Three real options on the table:</p><ol><li>UK Alternative Business Structure (ABS) under the Legal Services Act 2007.</li><li>Arizona Alternative Business Structure under Arizona\'s post-2020 ABS regime.</li><li>Hybrid: UK ABS as the primary regulated entity + a small US-licensed partnership operating in Arizona ABS.</li></ol>',
    prompt: 'Walk through the regulatory analysis. Which structure is the right call for this team, and what are the trade-offs the user must explain to each founding partner?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>(1) The regulatory analysis.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>UK ABS (Legal Services Act 2007).</strong> The easiest route in the world for a venture-backed AI law firm. Mature regime (over 1,500 ABSs licensed since 2012); recent precedent for AI-driven service delivery (Garfield.Law authorised May 2025); accommodates non-lawyer ownership up to 100%; SOC for VC-funded structure is well-trodden. SRA review takes ~6–9 months. Reserved-legal-activity coverage is straightforward for M&amp;A advisory. <strong>Capability constraint:</strong> UK ABS only authorises the firm to provide reserved legal activities in England and Wales; cross-border M&amp;A involving US-law SPAs requires US-licensed lawyers operating under US-state authority.</li><li><strong>Arizona ABS (post-2020).</strong> Arizona repealed Rule 5.4 in August 2020. KPMG Law approved as ABS February 2025 — the first Big Four legal-services US firm. The regime is commercially viable. <strong>Constraint:</strong> Arizona ABS authorises practice <em>in Arizona</em>; multi-state practice triggers the unauthorised-practice-of-law (UPL) analysis under each other state\'s rules. NY-anchored sponsor M&amp;A (where the Kirkland partner\'s book lives) cannot be served by an Arizona-only firm without admitting attorneys in NY who in turn cannot be employed by a non-lawyer-owned firm under NY Rule 5.4. The structure works for Arizona-anchored business; it does not solve the NY-centric M&amp;A problem.</li><li><strong>Hybrid (UK ABS + US Arizona partnership).</strong> The realistic structure. UK ABS as the primary regulated entity holding the IP, the technology platform, and the venture capital. US-licensed partnership operating from Arizona ABS for matters where US-state-licensed attorneys are required. The partners in the US entity are admitted in NY / DE / etc; the firm holds Arizona ABS authorisation. Cross-border deals run through co-counsel arrangements between the two entities. Most early-stage AI law firms use a variant of this structure.</li></ul><p><strong>(2) The right call for this team.</strong></p><p><strong>Hybrid (option 3) is the correct choice.</strong> Reasoning:</p><ul><li><em>The UK Magic Circle partner serves UK / EU PE</em> — UK ABS authorisation is the right framework for her practice. She bills out of London under SRA authorisation, charges in GBP, and her book is UK-authorised business. UK ABS is the sole right answer for her practice.</li><li><em>The Kirkland US partner serves US PE / strategic</em> — Arizona ABS authorises her firm but not her practice in NY where her clients sit. She needs US-state-licensed practice under a structure that survives Rule 5.4. The hybrid approach gives her a US-licensed partnership entity (potentially Arizona-incorporated, with founding partners admitted in NY) that operates with Rule 5.4-compatible structure under Arizona ABS, plus co-counsel arrangements with the UK ABS entity for cross-border matters.</li><li><em>The non-lawyer founder/CEO</em> can hold equity in the UK ABS entity at 100% if desired (subject to the LSA\'s "licensable body" 10% non-authorised-person voting threshold). She cannot hold equity in a NY law firm directly. The capital concentrates in the UK entity; the US entity is a downstream wholly-owned (or majority-owned) operating subsidiary structured to be Rule 5.4-compatible.</li></ul><p><strong>(3) The trade-offs to explain to each founding partner.</strong></p><p><strong>To the UK Magic Circle partner.</strong> "Your practice is fully accommodated under UK ABS. Your conflicts and your client base map onto the UK regulator. The downside is that you\'ll work alongside a US-licensed entity for cross-border matters, which adds co-counsel-fee complexity and intra-firm allocation negotiations. We\'ll standardise originator-vs-working credit splits between the entities to avoid the cross-border fights."</p><p><strong>To the Kirkland US partner.</strong> "Your practice runs through the US-licensed entity. You\'re not employed directly by a non-lawyer; you\'re a partner of a US partnership that is contractually integrated with a UK ABS that holds the platform and the capital. This is structurally identical to how multidisciplinary firms work in jurisdictions where Rule 5.4 still applies. The downside is that you\'ll need to be physically based in or admitted in a state where the Arizona ABS authorisation does not face automatic UPL challenge. You\'ll bill out of NY but the firm\'s ownership sits in the UK entity. Practical answer: most NY M&amp;A boutiques run this way when they have non-lawyer capital."</p><p><strong>To the non-lawyer founder.</strong> "Your equity sits in the UK ABS entity. The US entity is a downstream subsidiary that you cannot directly control as a non-lawyer under Rule 5.4. Effective control is exercised through the UK ABS\'s ownership of the US entity\'s economic interest, with NY-admitted partners providing the lawyer-supervision element required by Rule 5.4. This is the cleanest available route in 2025; it survives state UPL analysis better than alternatives. The downside is multi-entity tax complexity and a more complicated cap table."</p><p><strong>(4) The watch-outs.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>NY UPL enforcement risk.</strong> Even with the hybrid structure, an aggressive NY enforcement action could challenge the practice on UPL grounds. The mitigant: keep the NY-admitted partners as the legal-services delivery layer; have the UK ABS provide platform / IP / technology only. Document the lawyer supervision.</li><li><strong>Insurance and risk.</strong> Professional indemnity insurance must cover both entities. AmLaw 50 carries $50–200m primary; a startup boutique might launch with $25–50m primary across the two entities.</li><li><strong>Tax structure.</strong> Pass-through tax treatment in both jurisdictions is feasible but requires careful structuring (LLC / LLP / hybrid election). Consider treaty implications and transfer-pricing on the cross-entity allocations.</li></ul>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'P4-05', module: 'P4',
    title: 'Pricing for a PE sponsor — $300m portfolio company add-on',
    setup: '<p>A mid-market PE sponsor (Thornwood Partners, $3bn AuM, 9 deals/year, panel-based with five firms) has approached the boutique to advise its largest portfolio company on a $300m strategic add-on acquisition. Thornwood\'s GC has flagged that this is an "audition" — succeed and the boutique gets considered for the next panel review.</p><p>The Thornwood GC says: "We need predictability — our LPs hate fee surprises. Most of our panel is on capped fees with a 1.5x success multiplier. Latham was around $3.2m capped + 1.5x for our last similar-size add-on. Show me how a smaller firm gives me what they don\'t."</p>',
    prompt: 'What pricing structure do you propose, and what is the underlying psychology of pricing for a PE sponsor that the user must understand?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>(1) The proposed pricing structure.</strong></p><p>Three components, each addressing a specific PE-sponsor psychology lever:</p><ol><li><strong>Phased capped fee, slightly below Latham.</strong> $2.8m capped through closing (vs. Latham\'s $3.2m); <em>true</em> cap, no scope-creep escalators except for explicit out-of-scope events (e.g. unexpected regulatory filing in a new jurisdiction; second-bidder stalking horse; material restructuring). Phased breakdown for transparency: $1.6m signing-phase; $0.6m signing-to-closing; $0.6m closing-phase. This costs the boutique approximately what Latham\'s economics would, but the cap is harder (Latham\'s "cap" is typically softened with scope-creep allowances).</li><li><strong>Reduced success multiplier on closing.</strong> 1.25× on closing ($3.5m total at closing) vs. Latham\'s 1.5× ($4.8m total). The $1.3m differential is the boutique\'s pricing edge — explicit and quantifiable.</li><li><strong>Portfolio-AFA framing for the broader Thornwood relationship.</strong> Conditional on this engagement closing successfully: the boutique offers a 2-year portfolio-AFA covering up to 3 Thornwood portfolio companies for ongoing GC work at a 30% discount to standard panel rates, fully capped at $2m / year per company. This converts the audition into a multi-year cross-sell story.</li></ol><p><strong>Total at closing under boutique pricing: $3.5m vs. Latham\'s $4.8m</strong> — a 27% saving on a basis comparable to what Thornwood\'s LPs see in expense reports.</p><p><strong>(2) The underlying PE-sponsor psychology.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Predictability beats lowness.</strong> A capped fee that everyone knows will hold is more valuable to a PE GC than a headline-low fee that everyone knows will overrun. PE carry calculation depends on accurate fee accruals; surprise overage has cascading effects on LP reporting. The boutique\'s "true cap" framing is more valuable than 5% of additional discount.</li><li><strong>Senior-partner attention is the structural commercial differentiator.</strong> The GC has been told "your dedicated partner" by every BigLaw firm she\'s ever hired and seen the actual hours roll up to a senior associate by week 3. The boutique\'s pitch is contractual: "60% of the hours on this deal will be the named M&amp;A founding partner; we\'ll attach the partner-hour commitment to the engagement letter." Putting the commitment in writing is the move.</li><li><strong>"Audition" is shorthand for "I need credit with my IC for taking a risk."</strong> The Thornwood GC will report this engagement to her investment committee. She needs to be able to say: "We saved $1.3m on this deal; the work product was at panel-firm quality; we\'re considering the boutique for the next panel review." The boutique\'s job is to give her the IC-friendly story, not the cheapest fee.</li><li><strong>Cross-portfolio leverage is the relationship-builder.</strong> The portfolio-AFA framing transforms a single $2.8m engagement into a potential $14m / 2-year relationship. PE sponsors love consolidated counsel for portfolio company GC work — fewer relationships, more leverage. The boutique\'s offer is differentiated precisely because BigLaw firms cannot economically deliver portfolio GC work at a 30% discount.</li><li><strong>Speed is the second-most-valued commodity after predictability.</strong> Quantified turnaround commitments (DD memo within 5 business days; SPA mark-up within 48 hours) hit a real PE pain point. PE deal teams run multiple processes in parallel; speed is direct ROI for them.</li><li><strong>"Show me you understand carry."</strong> The implicit ask. The user pitching can win extraordinary credibility by showing she understands that the GC\'s job is to feed accurate fee accruals into the carry calculation — not to minimise the headline fee. Saying out loud "we know your LP reports go quarterly and a 20% overage on this fee is a 12bp drag on your carry calc; here\'s how we eliminate that drag" lands very specifically.</li></ul><p><strong>(3) What does NOT win this pitch.</strong></p><ul><li><em>"We\'ll do it for $1.5m flat."</em> Loss-leader pricing. Reads as desperate or as not understanding the deal. Sophisticated GCs prefer accurate pricing.</li><li><em>"AI makes us faster."</em> Generic. Doesn\'t address her actual problem (predictability + senior attention + IC credit).</li><li><em>"We\'re smaller and more responsive."</em> Generic. Every boutique says this. The contractual partner-hour commitment is the differentiator.</li><li><em>"Trust us."</em> No PE GC trusts a new firm. The pitch has to give her IC-friendly metrics and contractual commitments she can rely on.</li></ul>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  }
];

/* Scenario fragment — Module P5 (AI Companion).
 * 5 scenarios — partner pitch challenges and Day-1 boutique decisions.
 */
window.__SCEN_P5 = [
  {
    id: 'P5-01', module: 'P5',
    title: 'Linklaters partner: "What does your AI do that Harvey doesn\'t?"',
    setup: '<p>You are in a 30-minute pitch meeting with a senior M&amp;A partner from Linklaters whom you are recruiting as co-founder. Linklaters has CreateiQ (their proprietary contract-automation platform launched 2021, version 2.0 in 2025), a 20-strong global AI lawyer team launched November 2025, and uses Legora as a layer on top of CreateiQ. The partner has Harvey on her desktop and uses it weekly. Twelve minutes in, she puts down her coffee and says:</p><p style="margin-left:1em;"><em>"Look, I get the boutique pitch. But I already use Harvey. CreateiQ does our contracts. Legora handles routine work. What does your AI actually do that Harvey doesn\'t already do at A&amp;O Shearman or Macfarlanes Amplify? Why would a client choose your boutique over a Magic Circle firm with a Harvey deployment?"</em></p>',
    prompt: 'Construct your answer in real time. What is the right framing? What are the three structural points the answer must hit? What is the single line that closes the question?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Wrong framing.</strong> Trying to claim that the boutique\'s AI is technically superior to Harvey. The partner uses Harvey daily; she will spot the bluff in two seconds. Claiming proprietary fine-tuned models, secret architectures, or higher accuracy on benchmarks she hasn\'t seen invites scrutiny that ends the meeting.</p><p><strong>Right framing.</strong> The boutique does NOT bet against Harvey on the model layer. The boutique uses Harvey (or its API) too. The differentiation is above and below the model layer.</p><p><strong>The three structural points.</strong></p><ol><li><em>Pricing model.</em> "Harvey at A&amp;O Shearman is partner-leveraged AI on a billable-hour cost structure. The firm reports 30% review-time reduction and 2&ndash;3 hours saved per lawyer per week — but that productivity flows into associate utilisation, not client price reduction. Thomson Reuters\' 2025 survey: only 20% of legal organisations measure GenAI ROI. MIT 2025: 95% of enterprise AI pilots have no P&amp;L impact. Our boutique is fixed-fee from day one — the productivity gain flows to the client at the headline number, and to us in margin. We can\'t match A&amp;O\'s brand; we can\'t match their global footprint; we can match the workflow at 40&ndash;60% lower headline cost on the modules we choose."</em></li><li><em>Sector specialisation.</em> "BigLaw is multi-sector by structural necessity — partners cross-subsidise practices, the brand promises everything-everywhere. We pick FinTech / regulated FS only. Our sector-anomaly database — change-of-control thresholds across PRA / FCA / Fed / ECB / FINRA; capital-treatment edge cases under CRR; AML / KYC inheritance issues — assembles deal-by-deal into something the multi-sector firms cannot economically build. Every deal compounds the moat."</em></li><li><em>The product is a deliverable, not a platform.</em> "We don\'t sell access to AI — Harvey already sells that. We sell a partner-signed DD report at $250k fixed fee, 14-day turnaround. Or a SPA skeleton at $100k flat. Or a warranty package bundled with W&amp;I broker referral. The unit economics are positive on every deliverable; revenue is recognised at delivery; we don\'t have the platform-business pricing problem Harvey has at $50k+ per seat per year."</em></li></ol><p><strong>The single line that closes the question.</strong> <em>"We don\'t bet against Harvey on the model layer — we bet above and below it. Harvey commoditises; our data, our brand, and our pricing model do not."</em></p><p><strong>Why this works.</strong> It explicitly refuses the technical-superiority claim (which the partner is testing for and would punish). It names two real productivity studies (Thomson Reuters; MIT) the partner has either read or is about to read. It locates the differentiation in workflow + sector + pricing + brand &mdash; four layers the partner can verify by checking her own client base. And the closing line is structurally honest: the boutique does not pretend to win the model arms race; it picks a different game.</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'P5-02', module: 'P5',
    title: 'Pricing the $200M PE deal — fixed-fee on AI-cut DD',
    setup: '<p>A mid-market PE house (your client; a relationship inherited from your legal-AI sales work) has agreed your boutique can run buy-side legal DD on a $200M FinTech acquisition. Internal estimates: BigLaw would price legal DD on this deal at $500k–$700k hourly. Your AI tooling (Kira for abstraction; Harvey API for risk-flag synthesis; proprietary FinTech sector-anomaly database) cuts associate-equivalent time by 60%. The PE house\'s GC says: "give me a fixed-fee number by tomorrow."</p>',
    prompt: 'What price do you quote? Walk through the reasoning. What does the engagement letter need to include?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Headline price: $250,000–$300,000 fixed fee.</strong> Recommendation: $275,000 with stretch terms. Below the reasoning.</p><p><strong>Why not BigLaw &minus; 10% ($450&ndash;$630k).</strong> A $50&ndash;$70k discount off BigLaw is not a structural arbitrage; it is a haggling discount. The client takes the meeting but doesn\'t change behaviour. The boutique\'s structural advantage is AI substitution for associate hours, and the price has to <em>signal</em> that &mdash; which means a price BigLaw cannot match without changing its compensation model.</p><p><strong>Why not BigLaw &minus; 80% ($100k).</strong> Two problems. First, $100k is signal-of-low-quality territory &mdash; the GC gets criticised at the IC for cheaping out on a $200m deal. Second, the boutique\'s unit economics need at least a partner&rsquo;s 60&ndash;80 hours plus the AI compute / tooling cost / per-deal allocation of the firm overhead; $100k under-prices the partner\'s time alone. The boutique does not race to the bottom; the boutique exits the hourly-billing axis altogether.</p><p><strong>Why ~$275k.</strong> The reasoning:</p><ul><li><em>BigLaw equivalent</em>: $500&ndash;$700k. Mid-point ~$600k.</li><li><em>Headline discount</em>: 50&ndash;55% off BigLaw mid-point. This is the "structural arbitrage" range &mdash; visible enough to drive procurement, deep enough to be defensible against a follow-up RFP.</li><li><em>Boutique cost structure</em>: ~80 partner hours @ $1,000/hr fully-loaded (= $80k); 40 senior-associate-equivalent hours of HITL review @ $400/hr (= $16k); per-deal AI tooling + tenant + clean-room compute (= ~$8k); allocated overhead / insurance / playbook amortisation (= ~$30k). Total cost ~$135k. Gross margin at $275k = 51%. Acceptable for a wedge-product deal; preserves margin for the inevitable scope creep.</li></ul><p><strong>Engagement-letter must-haves.</strong></p><ol><li><em>Fixed-fee scope</em>: explicit list of in-scope categories (target-level legal DD on commercial contracts, IP, regulatory licences, employment, real estate, data protection, financial-crime / AML, change-of-control / regulatory consents). Out-of-scope: tax DD (separate engagement); commercial / market DD (banker / strategy consultant); environmental Phase II; valuation work.</li><li><em>Scope-creep clause</em>: any in-scope expansion (e.g., additional jurisdictions; subsidiary newly identified) priced at agreed unit rates; any out-of-scope work requires a written change order.</li><li><em>Deliverable spec</em>: partner-signed risk memo (10&ndash;25 pages); executive summary (2&ndash;3 pages); detailed annex / data-room cross-references; summary risk register with severity and recommended SPA treatment.</li><li><em>Turnaround</em>: 14 calendar days from data-room access; 7 days for material updates as the data room is supplemented.</li><li><em>HITL discipline language</em>: every output partner-reviewed; AI tooling named (Kira, Harvey, sector-anomaly DB); explicit AI-disclosure consistent with applicable bar / SRA guidance.</li><li><em>Liability cap</em>: aggregate cap at 2&times; the fee ($550k) for ordinary negligence; carve-outs for fraud and wilful misconduct (uncapped); insurance to $50m for catastrophic claims (the policy proceeds back-stop the cap).</li><li><em>Confidentiality and data handling</em>: per-deal vector store; tenant isolation in the LLM provider&rsquo;s cloud; deal-completion data destruction unless the client requests retention; reference-architecture clean room if there is competitor-overlap exposure (in a FinTech deal, banking-licence-overlap is a real possibility).</li><li><em>Conflicts representation</em>: confirm conflicts cleared as of engagement; flag any new conflicts that arise during DD.</li><li><em>Successor work option</em>: agreed pricing on follow-on SPA skeleton ($100k), warranty package ($50k), and post-closing tracker subscription ($10k/yr) &mdash; locked-in optionality for the boutique to grow within the deal.</li></ol><p><strong>The structural lesson</strong>: the price is set by the structural arbitrage (50&ndash;55% off BigLaw mid-point), not by cost-plus or by competitive bidding. The engagement letter encodes the operational discipline that makes the malpractice profile defensible.</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'P5-03', module: 'P5',
    title: 'Build vs. buy — the boutique\'s drafting AI',
    setup: '<p>You are in a Day-30 strategy meeting with your CTO co-founder. Decision required: how to build the boutique\'s SPA-skeleton-generation product (the M07 deliverable). Three options on the table:</p><ol><li><strong>Buy: Harvey API at ~$50k/year per seat for the firm + per-API-call usage.</strong> Build a thin orchestration layer on top.</li><li><strong>Buy: Spellbook + Lexis+ Protégé bundle.</strong> Rely on their Word-native drafting + transactional workflows.</li><li><strong>Build: fine-tune a Llama / Mistral open-source model on the partner\'s precedent library.</strong> Run it in our own VPC.</li></ol>',
    prompt: 'Which do you choose, why, and what is the sequence over the first 12 months?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Decision: Buy Option 1 (Harvey API + thin orchestration layer) for Year 1. Revisit at month 12.</strong></p><p><strong>Why not Option 3 (fine-tune open-source).</strong> The structural reason &mdash; LLM commoditisation. The base-model improvement curve runs faster than any fine-tune cycle. By the time a fine-tuned Llama-on-precedent-library is production-ready, GPT-6 or Claude 5 has caught up on general capability and the fine-tuned advantage is gone. Empirical signals 2024&ndash;2026: per-token API price falls of ~70% / year on commodity tasks; multi-model routing becoming standard (Harvey added Anthropic and Google to OpenAI in May 2025). Production reality of mid-2026: most legal AI uses prompt + RAG, not fine-tune. Option 3 requires hiring a 3&ndash;5-person ML team ($1.5&ndash;$2.5m/year fully loaded), 6&ndash;9 months of dev time, ongoing re-training cost &mdash; before the first deal ships. The opportunity cost is real: the boutique\'s Year 1 is partner-led origination and learning what the playbook should be, not training a model.</p><p><strong>Why not Option 2 (Spellbook + Lexis+ Protégé).</strong> Spellbook is excellent on commercial contracts (NDAs, MSAs, DPAs at $20&ndash;$40/user/month). It is less depth on high-stakes M&amp;A SPAs than Harvey or Luminance. Lexis+ Prot&eacute;g&eacute; is strong on research and term-sheet-to-customised-agreement workflows but is not the same workflow API depth as Harvey. Bundling both adds two vendor relationships and integration cost without the SPA-grade workflow capability. Defensible alternative position; not optimal for the SPA wedge.</p><p><strong>Why Option 1 (Harvey API + thin orchestration).</strong></p><ul><li><em>Time to first deliverable</em>: 30&ndash;60 days vs. 6&ndash;9 months for Option 3.</li><li><em>Capital efficiency</em>: ~$200k/year on Harvey for a 5-person firm vs. $1.5m+/year for an ML team.</li><li><em>Multi-model resilience</em>: Harvey routes across Anthropic, OpenAI, and Google. If one provider has a capability gap or pricing spike, the platform handles it.</li><li><em>Competitive parity</em>: A&amp;O Shearman, Macfarlanes, and PwC are using Harvey too. The boutique is on parity at the model layer; differentiation lives in the playbook + sector specialisation.</li><li><em>Optionality</em>: thin orchestration layer means migration to a different LLM platform is feasible at month 12+ if Harvey&rsquo;s pricing or strategic posture changes.</li></ul><p><strong>The 12-month sequence.</strong></p><ol><li><em>Months 0&ndash;3</em>: Harvey API integration; partner precedent libraries ingested into a per-firm RAG vector store; first SPA skeleton ships at end of Month 3 with the partner&rsquo;s sign-off.</li><li><em>Months 3&ndash;6</em>: First 5 deals; tight feedback loop with the partner on every output; build the proprietary playbook by codifying every partner correction back into the prompt + RAG layer.</li><li><em>Months 6&ndash;12</em>: Volume to 20+ deals; sector-anomaly database (FinTech / FS) takes shape; Compare-to-Market benchmarking on internal deal data starts to differentiate.</li><li><em>Month 12 review</em>: Has Harvey&rsquo;s pricing held up? Are we hitting the LLM-provider cost ceiling? Is there a workflow Harvey doesn&rsquo;t serve that justifies a thin proprietary layer? At this point the build-vs-buy question is data-driven, not speculative.</li></ol><p><strong>The single-line meeting answer.</strong> <em>"Buy Harvey now; build the playbook on top; revisit the model layer at month 12 with revenue-driven data. We bet on data and brand, not on training the model."</em></p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'P5-04', module: 'P5',
    title: 'The partner objection: "My BigLaw firm just deployed Harvey. What\'s left for the boutique?"',
    setup: '<p>You are in a final-stage pitch meeting with a senior PE-focused M&amp;A partner at a US-headquartered global firm. He sits on the firm&rsquo;s tech-strategy committee. The firm signed a multi-year Harvey enterprise contract three months ago. He says:</p><p style="margin-left:1em;"><em>"Look, I&rsquo;ve been using Harvey for six months. ContractMatrix every day. Vault for deal workspaces. The agentic agents on antitrust. We&rsquo;ve trained 1,800 lawyers. The firm is investing $30m a year on legaltech. Be honest with me &mdash; what&rsquo;s left for a boutique once everyone has Harvey? Aren&rsquo;t you arbitraging a window that closes in two years?"</em></p>',
    prompt: 'Respond. What are the three structural defences the boutique has? Where does the answer concede?',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>The honest concession first.</strong> "You&rsquo;re partly right. There is a window. The structural arbitrage between BigLaw hourly billing and AI-firm fixed-fee is real but not permanent &mdash; my realistic estimate is 30&ndash;40% probability of material erosion within 5 years if the major firms move aggressively to fixed-fee on commodity workflows. So the question is what happens inside that window and what we build that survives the squeeze."</p><p><strong>Three structural defences that survive Harvey at BigLaw.</strong></p><ol><li><em>BigLaw partner compensation is hour-anchored and slow to change.</em> "Your firm has Harvey, but your partner comp model still rewards billable hours. A&amp;O Shearman&rsquo;s 30% review-time reduction shows up in associate utilisation, not in client price reduction at the headline number. Until BigLaw partner compensation flips to fixed-fee origination credit &mdash; which means rewriting partnership agreements, equity-share formulas, and originator credit rules &mdash; the price gap to a fixed-fee boutique persists. That structural lag is 5+ years. Inside that window, every deal where the client cares about the headline price is an opening for us."</li><li><em>Sector specialisation BigLaw cannot match deal-by-deal.</em> "Your firm covers PE, real estate, energy, healthcare, tech, financial services, public M&amp;A &mdash; the brand promises full coverage. We pick FinTech / regulated FS only. Our sector-anomaly database is partner-curated and assembles deal-by-deal &mdash; change-of-control thresholds across PRA / FCA / Fed / ECB / FINRA / OCC; capital-treatment edge cases under CRR; AML / KYC inheritance issues; the BSA / OFAC compliance overlay on US FinTech acquisitions. Your firm could build it for a single sector, but the cost of building 12 sector databases doesn&rsquo;t make sense at scale because most of your origination is partner-led, not data-led. We get a moat that compounds with each deal in our vertical."</li><li><em>The deliverable is the product, not the platform.</em> "Your firm&rsquo;s Harvey deployment is a platform purchase &mdash; you pay $30m/year and ROI is hard to measure (Thomson Reuters 2025: only 20% of legal organisations measure GenAI ROI; MIT 2025: 95% of enterprise pilots have no P&amp;L impact). Our boutique is a deliverable business &mdash; we ship a partner-signed DD report at $250k, recognise revenue at delivery, calculate gross margin per deliverable. The unit economics are positive on every deal; we never have the platform-business problem of investment without measurable return."</li></ol><p><strong>The closing line.</strong> <em>"You&rsquo;re right that the window matters. Inside the window, we ship 50&ndash;100 deals, build the FinTech sector-anomaly DB, lock in PE relationships, and get to $20&ndash;$30m ARR. After the window closes &mdash; if BigLaw matches our pricing &mdash; we still have the data, the brand, the partner network, and the recurring-revenue layer (post-closing trackers). Those don&rsquo;t commoditise. The boutique survives the window because the moat doesn&rsquo;t live at the model layer."</em></p><p><strong>Why this works.</strong> The partner is testing for honesty and structural literacy. Refusing to concede the window&rsquo;s existence loses the meeting; conceding it without a structural answer loses the pitch. The three defences are concrete, named, and verifiable; the closing line maps to a 5-year operational plan, not a vibes-based pitch.</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  },
  {
    id: 'P5-05', module: 'P5',
    title: 'Day 1 — the first 10 actions when the boutique launches',
    setup: '<p>The Series A has closed. You and the founding M&amp;A partner have signed founder paperwork. Day 1 of the boutique. You have 18 months of runway, $12m in the bank, and the partner&rsquo;s portable book signals 3&ndash;5 deals ready to onboard within 60 days.</p>',
    prompt: 'List the 10 actions you take in the first week, in priority order, with the operational reasoning.',
    modelAnswer: '<p>The first week is execution discipline. The wrong moves: hiring six associates, building a website, signing every legaltech vendor contract on offer. The right moves below.</p><ol><li><strong>Conflicts clearance system live (Day 1).</strong> Real-time deal-level conflicts checking against the partner&rsquo;s portable book and the BigLaw runoff list. No first deal can sign before this is operational. Vendor: Intapp Conflicts or similar; deployable in 5&ndash;7 days as SaaS.</li><li><strong>Insurance tower placed (Day 2&ndash;3).</strong> $50m professional indemnity placement (Lockton or Marsh as broker; AIG / Beazley / Hiscox layering). The malpractice answer to every partner-meeting objection depends on the policy being in place before deal one. Engagement letter pricing depends on this.</li><li><strong>Engagement-letter template signed off by external counsel (Day 3&ndash;4).</strong> Including: fixed-fee scope; scope-creep clause; HITL disclosure; AI-disclosure language consistent with SRA / state-bar guidance; liability cap (2&times; fee for ordinary negligence; uncapped fraud / wilful misconduct); per-deal data destruction; conflicts representation. External-counsel review prevents avoidable structural errors.</li><li><strong>Tooling stack contracted (Day 4&ndash;5).</strong> Harvey enterprise contract; Kira (Litera) for abstraction; Datasite or Intralinks pre-arranged for VDR (buyer-mandated more often, but pre-arrangement smooths Day 1 of first deal); CoCounsel as research backstop; Anthropic / OpenAI direct API for orchestration layer; per-tenant cloud deployment (AWS or GCP) with per-deal vector stores; reference-architecture clean room template. Procurement: 5 vendor contracts in week 1.</li><li><strong>First-deal scope memo (Day 5).</strong> Identify the first 3 of the partner\'s portable-book deals; align with each client\'s GC on scope; price each at fixed-fee using the boutique pricing template; lock the engagement letters within Week 2. The first deal closes are the boutique&rsquo;s proof points for the next 50 pitches.</li><li><strong>Playbook v0.1 ingested (Day 5&ndash;7).</strong> The partner\'s precedent library &mdash; SPAs, warranty schedules, indemnification templates, DD playbooks &mdash; ingested into the per-firm RAG vector store. This is what makes Harvey output partner-grade rather than generic. Manual triage of which precedents are deal-shape-applicable; tag every clause with sector, jurisdiction, deal-size band, year.</li><li><strong>FinTech sector-anomaly DB seeded (Day 6&ndash;7).</strong> Initial corpus: PRA / FCA / Fed / ECB / FINRA / OCC change-of-control thresholds; CRR / Basel III capital-treatment edge cases; BSA / OFAC compliance schema; major precedent transactions (M&amp;A in FinTech 2020&ndash;2026). Public-source seed; expand with deal-data starting deal 1.</li><li><strong>HITL operating model written down (Day 7).</strong> Explicit workflow document: every AI output reviewed by [associate-equivalent role]; partner sign-off required before any client-facing delivery; output guardrails (PII; privilege; no-legal-advice flag); escalation paths for novel risk patterns. This is the malpractice defence and the partner-meeting answer to "what about hallucinations?".</li><li><strong>Pitch-deck v1 finalised (Day 7).</strong> The alpha-document version of P5 &mdash; the AI maturity framework; the tools-currently-in-use map; the module-by-module AI map; the boutique product strategy; the partner objection checklist; the partner phrasebook. Written to be deliverable to a Linklaters partner without rewriting. This is the document the next 10 partner pitches depend on.</li><li><strong>First five partner-pitch meetings calendared (Day 7).</strong> Target list: 5 senior M&amp;A partners across one Magic Circle, two US BigLaw, one Tier-1 PE-focused boutique, one in-house Head of M&amp;A at a strategic acquirer. Goal of week 2: 5 first-meeting touchpoints with the alpha-document pitch deck and the first three deals as proof points.</li></ol><p><strong>What is NOT in the first week.</strong></p><ul><li><em>Hiring associates.</em> The first 5&ndash;10 deals are partner + AI; associate hiring waits until the deliverable economics are validated and the playbook is concrete.</li><li><em>Building a public website.</em> Brand visibility is a Year 1 second-half problem. The first-week brand is the partner&rsquo;s LinkedIn and the alpha-document pitch deck.</li><li><em>Building a foundation model or fine-tuning.</em> See scenario 3 for why.</li><li><em>Trying to sell to BigLaw firms.</em> The boutique&rsquo;s buyer is in-house GCs at PE houses and FinTech founders, not partner-equivalents at BigLaw firms (who are competitors).</li><li><em>Conferences / speaking circuits.</em> Year 1 is execution-led; partner brand is built through deal proof points, not through panels.</li></ul><p><strong>The structural lesson.</strong> Day 1 to 7 is operational discipline at every layer &mdash; conflicts, insurance, engagement letters, tooling, playbook, HITL. None of these is glamorous; all of them are required for the boutique to bill its first deal at fixed fee with a defensible malpractice profile. The first-week checklist is what separates the boutique that ships from the boutique that pivots three times before running out of runway.</p>',
    type: 'fact-pattern'
  }
];

